<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The AI loop: an OUTER machine that drives an INNER agent session.

  The inner session is a pane sprag opened (`open_pane`) running an agent CLI.
  This machine never talks to that agent; it decides WHEN the driver should
  prompt it, when to stop, and when the session itself has to be replaced. The
  driver (sprag-plugin) owns every effect — assembling prompt text, reading the
  pane, editing files, closing and reopening the pane.

  Why an inner session is REPLACED rather than reconfigured: the things a loop
  wants to improve about itself — the agent's base context, which MCP servers
  are loaded, CLAUDE.md, the memory index — are all read when a session starts.
  A live session cannot be asked to re-read them. So `reflecting` settles what
  the next session should be working on and `restarting` closes the pane and
  opens a fresh one, which is briefed with it on the way up. That is the whole
  reason this machine has a restart state instead of a "reconfigure" event.

  ⚠⚠⚠ AND `reflecting` ASKS THE AGENT. The one thing a driver cannot decide is
  where somebody else's work should go next, so the reflection is a TURN: the
  agent that has been doing the work says what the next milestone is and what
  the next session should read, and those two answers are what cross the
  boundary. Without it a run is bounded by the checkpoint whoever started it
  wrote down — it can replace its session and greet the replacement with the
  same instruction for ever, which is a run bounded by one agent's context
  wearing a longer life.

  The terminal outcomes are the Driver's (`orchestration.scxml`) on purpose: a
  loop that ends reports the same shapes any plugin run reports, so one reader
  understands both.

  Where this machine DIFFERS from the Driver is `blocked`, and the difference is
  the whole reason a person can supervise a run. In the Driver a blocked peer
  ends the run, because nobody is watching. Here the inner session is a pane on
  a person's screen: they can read every turn as it happens and type into it
  directly. So an agent that stops to ASK sends this machine to `awaiting_human`
  — it stops prompting and waits. If the person answers the dialog, the agent
  goes back to work and the loop resumes where it was. `blocked` stays as the
  terminal for the case that actually ends a run: nobody came.

  ⚠⚠⚠ THIS DOCUMENT USED TO SAY *"the loop still never answers a dialog itself
  … answering is still not this machine's to do"*, and that sentence is now
  FALSE IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. It is kept here, struck through, because what
  replaced it is the shape of the whole feature rather than a detail:

  * This DOCUMENT declares consents (`may_answer`), and the barrier takes an
    offered option on them. That is somebody saying *yes* in advance, and R382
    measured what its absence cost: a loop whose agent asked one permission
    question stopped with ZERO turns judged, and every milestone that touches a
    file ends there.
  * This DOCUMENT writes standing instructions (`screen_rules`), and `screening`
    carries them out by REFUSING the call and saying what to do instead.

  The sentence was right about the principle and wrong about the conclusion.
  Whose decision a tool permission is has not changed: it is the person's — and
  both of these are the person having made it IN ADVANCE, in writing, rather
  than the machine deciding. What the loop still never does is decide something
  nobody wrote down; a dialog neither a consent nor a rule reaches still stops
  the run and waits for somebody.

  ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE FIRST BULLET USED TO READ *«a CALLER may declare consents
  (`AiLoopSpec::may_answer`)»*. It is corrected rather than deleted because the
  correction is the rule this file now lives under: EVERY DECISION IS THIS
  DOCUMENT'S, and a run argument binds a run to its pane — which agent, in which
  pane — never what that run may decide. Approval and refusal were the last pair
  living in two worlds, and they no longer are.

  datamodel="ecmascript" (not the `null` its two siblings use) because a loop's
  prompts and goals ARE its definition: the whole point is that a person edits
  them. Its siblings hold pure topology and keep their data in Rust beside the
  engine; here the data is the part being authored, so it lives in the document
  the author edits.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT "ecmascript" IS EVALUATED BY, MEASURED. At the pinned SCE rev there
  is exactly one IScriptEngine — LuaEngine; sce-rust-runtime's own manifest
  calls QuickJS "future". SCE's build special-cases only the datamodel string
  "null" and routes every other value to whatever engine the consumer supplies,
  with no diagnostic. So every expression below is evaluated as LUA, and the
  generated init strings show the codegen rewriting some of the syntax on the
  way in (`[...]` -> `{...}`, `key:` -> `key =`) and not other parts of it
  (`'a' + b` is passed through, where Lua `+` is arithmetic).

  Reading that, this round predicted the composed prompts would fail to
  initialise. THEY DO NOT: `ai_loop.rs`'s gate drives a real LuaEngine and every
  shape here crosses whole — the bare literal, the `+`-composed prompt, the list
  of objects, the scalar. The prediction was wrong and the gate is what said so.

  What that measurement does NOT license is assuming ECMAScript semantics.
  Nothing here has been shown to work beyond the shapes the gate drives, so
  before writing `===`, `JSON.stringify`, `Array.prototype.map`, `undefined` or a
  template literal into this file, ADD IT TO THAT GATE FIRST. The engine will
  answer; the name at the top of this document will not.

  ⚠ AND `datamodel="lua"` IS NOT AN OPTION, measured at the pinned rev: SCE types
  the attribute and refuses `lua` as *undefined* (and `xpath` as *unimplemented*),
  so `ecmascript` is the only legal declaration for a script datamodel. The
  mismatch above is upstream's shape, not something this file can correct.

  ⚠⚠ NON-ASCII REACHES THIS DATAMODEL BY BOTH ROUTES INTO IT, and that is worth
  writing down because for one round it did not:

  * TEXT AUTHORED HERE, as a `<data expr="'…'">` literal — the three `screen_rules`
    replies below are Korean — always crossed whole.
  * TEXT ARRIVING AS EVENT DATA did not. `sce-rust-lua` rewrote a JSON payload into
    Lua source with `bytes[i] as char`, a Latin-1 decode of UTF-8, so a `brief` in
    a person's own language came back mangled with no error raised anywhere. The
    driver's read-back REFUSED it rather than prompting an agent with the result,
    which is how it was found at all; filed as PR-87 and FIXED upstream, where the
    rewrite was replaced by a real JSON decoder (restoring escapes and arrays with
    it — valid JSON carrying either had been demoting `_event.data` to a string).

  Both routes are measured by `a_non_ascii_string_reaches_the_datamodel_by_either
  _route`, which is what goes red if either regresses.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="ai_loop"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="running">

  <!--
    Everything above the line is the template's editable surface. A person edits
    it here; a caller that did not write the file fills the same parts in through
    the `brief` event on `idle`. Nothing below the line changes per loop.

    ⚠⚠⚠ THE PARTS ARE DATA AND THE PROMPTS ARE COMPOSED FROM THEM IN `priming`,
    which is the one arrangement that lets both authors work. Composing in
    `<data>` — where this document did it until now — happens exactly once, at
    `initialize()`, so a caller filling a part in afterwards left the composed
    prompt stale and there was no way to reach it: MEASURED, the only prompt any
    caller could make this loop send was

        North star: (edit me) the outcome this loop exists to reach
        Milestone: (edit me) the next checkpoint on the way there
        Reference: (edit me) paths, URLs or repos to consult

    — three of the five clauses a live agent reads. Writing the composition in
    BOTH places would keep the file-editing author working and is what this
    document already warns against two comments down: two copies of one rule,
    and the failure of letting them drift is silent. `priming` is where it runs
    instead, once, because it is entered on the way to EVERY prompt this loop
    ever sends — from `idle` at the start, and from `restarting` after a
    reflection has rewritten the parts.
  -->
  <datamodel>
    <!-- Where this loop is ultimately going. Never rewritten by reflection. -->
    <data id="north_star" expr="'(edit me) the outcome this loop exists to reach'"/>
    <!-- The step being worked on now. Reflection may rewrite this. -->
    <data id="milestone" expr="'(edit me) the next checkpoint on the way there'"/>
    <!-- Prior art the agent should read before deciding anything. -->
    <data id="reference" expr="'(edit me) paths, URLs or repos to consult'"/>
    <!--
      The agent the driver launches in the inner pane. INTERACTIVE, never
      `-p`/print mode: sprag reads an agent's state from its TUI — the title's
      leading glyph separates working from idle, and a blocked agent is
      recognised by the shape of its dialog. Print mode renders none of that,
      so a `-p` inner session is one this loop cannot observe and a person
      cannot answer.
    -->
    <data id="model" expr="'claude'"/>

    <!--
      Termination: the loop converges when the agent says this.

      ⚠⚠⚠ DECLARED BEFORE THE PROMPTS, and that is load-bearing rather than
      tidy: `<data>` is evaluated in document order, and every working prompt
      below COMPOSES this string. Move it back down and the prompts initialise
      against a marker that does not exist yet.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE PROMPTS COMPOSE IT RATHER THAN REPEATING IT. R379 measured
      what the alternative costs. The marker existed here, `judging` waited for
      it, the driver looked for it on the screen — and NO PROMPT EVER ASKED THE
      AGENT FOR IT. Nothing in the loop was wrong except that the one word the
      whole termination rests on was never said to the only party who could say
      it back. A run against a real agent could not converge; it could only
      spend all `max_turns` and report `exhausted`, which is the word for *the
      budget ran out* and not for *nobody ever asked*. It went unnoticed for
      eleven rounds because the stand-in agent in the driver's gate was written
      to say the marker, which is a fixture manufacturing the answer it tests
      for.

      Composing means an author who edits the marker edits the instruction with
      it. Retyping the word into each prompt would be three places to keep in
      step, and the failure of doing that badly is silent.
    -->
    <data id="done_marker" expr="'MILESTONE REACHED'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND WHO SAYS THE AGENT IS RIGHT ABOUT THAT — register item 428.

      Everything above rests on a string the WORKING AGENT writes. It prints
      `done_marker`, the driver reads it off the pane, and `judge`'s door below
      sends the run to `reflecting`: the party that did the work is the party
      that certifies it.

      ⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED ON AN UNRELATED SYSTEM, and the numbers are why this is a slot
      rather than a comment: 92 of 100 runs recorded `succeeded` — where
      `succeeded` meant *the branch was pushed* — while 18 of those 100 were
      REPAIRS of runs already recorded that way, and rework was 26% of spend. Its
      pull requests merged 100%, with 0 reviews and a median of 1.9 minutes.

      The remedy is named twice, independently, and identically: *an independent
      check, NOT the model's own say-so, decides when the work is done*, and *a
      DIFFERENT agent, in a NEW session, shown only the artifact*.

      This is an ARGV, split on whitespace, run by nobody this loop is driving.
      The driver appends one rendered question — the milestone, and what the turn
      produced — and reads the first word of the reply as YES or NO.

      ⚠⚠ EMPTY IS THE SHIPPED VALUE AND MEANS NOBODY CHECKS, which is exactly the
      behaviour this document had before the slot existed. It does NOT mean
      *passed*: the driver publishes `checked = false` for it, and the run's walk
      says in words that nothing checked the claim. An author who wants the
      milestone to mean something writes a command here.
    -->
    <data id="milestone_check" expr="''"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT ON THE SCREEN MEANS *THE PEER'S SERVICE FAILED*, WHICH IS NOT
      THE SAME AS THE PEER ASKING SOMETHING.

      A blocked turn has always meant ONE thing here: the agent stopped and wants
      an answer. `screening` looks for a dialog, and when no rule claims one the
      person decides. That is right for a question and WRONG for a hiccup in the
      service behind the agent — nobody can answer it, and a run told there is
      nobody watching ends at `blocked`, which is `<final>`.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED 2026-08-19, WHICH IS WHY THIS EXISTS. A live run worked
      **28m37s** and its turn ended with, verbatim:

          API Error: 529 Overloaded. This is a server-side issue, usually
          temporary — try again in a moment.

      The machine then did every step correctly and the sum was wrong: `working`
      took `turn.blocked` to `screening`, which took `screen.none` to
      `awaiting_human`, which took `unattended` to `blocked` — and `blocked` is
      `<final>`. **The screen said «try again in a moment» and the run was filed
      as «nobody could say».** Twenty-eight minutes of paid work, discarded, over
      a wait.

      ⚠ The arrows are spelled out in words here because an XML comment may not
      contain a double hyphen, which is what the usual notation is made of.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND `screen_rules` CANNOT CARRY THIS — measured in the source rather
      than assumed. A rule's first key is a REFUSAL (`screen::REFUSES`, Escape)
      and Escape at a composer discards whatever is being written; then the rule
      requires its quoted text to LEAVE the screen within `DISMISSED_WITHIN`. A
      printed error line does neither. A rule quoting this would press Escape at
      nothing and then report `not_dismissed` — arriving at `awaiting_human` by a
      longer road. **A rule answers a question; this is not one.**

      So the routing is a branch on `turn.blocked`, above the design one, and the
      driver measures the fact the same way it measures `judged`: it reads the
      pane and publishes `_event.data.service`. The driver measures; the document
      decides. ⚠ It is checked FIRST because a service failure is not a design
      decision and must never be judged as one — a judge asked about an error
      message is a model call spent to reach the wrong state.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND `turn.blocked` IS NOT THE ONLY DOOR AN OUTAGE ARRIVES AT
      — register item 715, measured 2026-08-27, and the reason this is a LIST.

      A 529 ENDS a turn, so the machine hears `turn.blocked`. A USAGE LIMIT does
      not: the peer prints what happened, stops speaking, and waits. Nothing
      ended, so what the machine hears is `peer.silent` — and that edge went
      straight to `awaiting_human`, i.e. to *a person is expected*, for the one
      interruption in an unattended loop's life where **nobody is expected and
      nothing is wrong**. Measured on run 5: `Working` to `AwaitingHuman` at
      01:05:39 and `Blocked` at 02:05:39, exactly one `await_person_ms` later,
      while the peer had already resumed by itself and was working. The run died,
      the work did not stop, and no account of that night said so. So BOTH doors
      ask the same question now, and the answer routes to the same state.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT IS A SET, WHICH IS THE REPAIR ITEM 715 ACTUALLY NAMES.
      This held ONE string, so one sentence could reach the state and its whole
      family walked past it — item 687's lesson in its smallest possible form: a
      check that knows N sentences cannot see the N plus first. Adding one more
      literal moves the trap by one sentence and leaves it standing. What a list
      buys is that a peer's vocabulary becomes a thing an author ENUMERATES and
      extends, and that the gate holding it can be re-taken against a real
      capture the way item 525's dialog gate is.

      ⚠⚠ EACH ENTRY IS AN OBJECT WITH ONE KEY rather than a bare string, for
      `may_answer`'s and `screen_rules`' reason: every authored list in this file
      is read by one shape of reader, and a fourth spelling would be a fourth
      thing to keep in step. It also leaves room for a key this round did not
      need — naming WHICH outage in the walk — without changing the shape.

      ⚠⚠⚠ EMPTY IS THE SHIPPED VALUE AND DECLINES THIS ENTIRELY, for
      `screen_rules`' reason one door down: the sentences above are `claude`'s, at
      one version, in English, and **a template does not know whose agent it will
      be talking to**. A repository that means to survive its peer's bad days
      quotes the words its own peer prints, in its own kind document.
    -->
    <data id="service_needles" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHETHER THE PEER SAID IT IS COMING BACK BY ITSELF — register item
      715, and the one decision in this file that is about WHAT NOT TO TYPE.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE JUDGEMENT THIS RECORDS, BECAUSE THE ALTERNATIVE LOOKS BETTER
      THAN IT IS. The peer states a RESUME TIME on the screen (*"continuing
      automatically at 3:30am"*), so the first idea — and the register's — is to
      parse that clock and wait until it. This document does NOT, and the reason
      is in the same sentence the clock is in: it ends *"esc or type to cancel"*.
      **Anything typed during that window cancels the recovery the wait exists to
      wait for.** A clock that is even slightly early therefore does not merely
      wait wrongly, it destroys what it was waiting for. And a parsed wall clock
      is a second clock nothing keeps in step with `service_retry_ms`,
      `service_retry_max` and `await_person_ms` — which is the argument this file
      already lost once, two `<data>` down, about ceilings.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO THE PEER'S SENTENCE IS READ FOR THE FACT AND NOT FOR THE CLOCK: it
      says *this peer resumes itself*, and that fact selects a way out of
      `service_down` that TYPES NOTHING and lets the peer's own next word be the
      evidence that the outage is over. The budget stays exactly what it was, as
      the bound on how long that may go on. It is not being replaced by the
      clock; it is being relieved of a job it was never able to do.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS SET BY THE DOOR AND NOT BY WHICH NEEDLE MATCHED, which is the
      narrower rule and the right one: what decides whether typing helps is
      whether the TURN ENDED, and the event that arrived is exactly that fact. A
      529 leaves an empty composer and needs a nudge; a peer that never stopped
      its turn needs to be left alone. A per-needle answer would be a second
      authority on something the machine already knows.

      ⚠ FALSE IS THE SHIPPED VALUE, and it is the behaviour every run had before
      this existed: wait, then say the word.
    -->
    <data id="service_resumes_itself" expr="false"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ HOW LONG TO LEAVE IT ALONE BEFORE SAYING ANYTHING, in milliseconds.

      ⚠⚠ A WAIT IS THE WHOLE TREATMENT AND THE NUMBER IS THE DOSE. What is wrong
      is upstream and nothing this run does makes it better; the only two ways to
      get it wrong are to ask again too soon (which is the load that caused it)
      and to never ask again (which is what shipped before this existed). Ten
      minutes is the owner's number, chosen 2026-08-19 against a 529.

      ⚠ THERE IS NO RETRY CEILING HERE, DELIBERATELY, and it is not an oversight:
      the run's own guardrails already bound it. `max_seconds` and
      `max_iterations` are the driver's and cannot be talked past by a document,
      so a service that never comes back costs a run its ceiling rather than
      forever. A second ceiling here would be a number nothing keeps in step with
      those two.
    -->
    <data id="service_retry_ms" expr="600000"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT TO SAY WHEN THE WAIT IS OVER, and it is authored rather than
      composed for `standing_label`'s reason: it is typed at somebody's agent, in
      somebody's language, and the driver must never be the thing that writes
      words into a prompt.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS NOT THE MILESTONE AGAIN. The session still holds everything it had
      — the brief, the turn it was mid-way through, the files it had open — so
      re-asking the whole question would spend the context that survived the
      outage. One word that means *carry on* is the smallest thing that resumes
      it, and `continue` is what a person types at this screen.
    -->
    <data id="service_retry_text" expr="'continue'"/>

    <!--
      HOW MANY TIMES THIS RUN HAS WAITED OUT ITS PEER'S SERVICE — for the walk,
      and for a reader asking whether a run took nine hours because the work was
      hard or because the service was down.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ IT IS A RUN TOTAL AND IT IS NOT WHAT ANY CEILING READS — register item
      729, and the sentence above is the reason rather than an excuse. This value
      was declared for a REPORT: *how much of this run was outage*, asked once at
      the end, of the whole run. Item 447 then also pointed a ceiling at it, and
      one value answering *how long has this run been unlucky* and *has THIS
      outage gone on too long* is one value with two jobs. The two questions want
      opposite things from a second outage: the report wants it added, the budget
      wants it started over.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND ITEM 724 IS WHAT MADE THE COLLISION BITE. While both doors shared
      one ceiling of six the two readings differed by little and never crossed a
      decision. With six at one door and thirty-six at the other, a run that
      waited out a five-hour usage limit arrives at its next 529 already twenty
      waits deep, and a hiccup that deserved six retries gets NONE — reported as
      *a person is needed*, which is the wrong sentence about a working service.
      See `service_retried_carried` below for the half that fixes it.
    -->
    <data id="service_retried" expr="0"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT WAS ALREADY ACCOUNTED FOR WHEN THIS OUTAGE BEGAN — register item
      729, and the whole of the repair: the ceilings read
      `service_retried - service_retried_carried`, which is *waits in THIS
      outage*, while the value above stays exactly the run total it was declared
      as. Two questions, two values, and neither has to lie for the other.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ A WATERMARK RATHER THAN A SECOND COUNTER, AND THAT IS THIS DOCUMENT'S
      OWN IDIOM rather than a new invention: `milestone_at` records `turns` at a
      moment so a guard can read `turns - milestone_at`, and `screened_carried`
      records `screened` so a guard can read `screened > screened_carried`. The
      alternative — incrementing a second counter beside the first — would put
      FOUR assignments where there are two, on edges that already carry one, and
      the failure of letting copies drift is silent. A watermark adds ONE
      assignment, in one place, and cannot disagree with the counter it marks.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE MOMENT IT IS SET IS `judging`'s ENTRY, AND THAT MOMENT ALREADY
      EXISTED — this item was registered believing the document named nowhere
      that an outage ENDS, and the document was asked rather than assumed. Every
      way into `judging` is a `turn.done`, which its own entry comment argues at
      length is *exactly once per judged turn*; and a completed turn is the only
      proof this document ever gets that the peer's service came back. So the
      end of an outage is not a new fact needing a new marker, it is a fact the
      existing marker already carries. ⚠ Nothing is written on the way OUT of
      `service_down`: leaving is a retry, not a recovery, and a retry that types
      into a service still refusing must keep counting.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT A RUN THAT NEVER FINISHES A TURN GETS: nothing is ever carried, so
      the delta equals the total and every ceiling behaves exactly as it did
      before this existed. The value fails toward the OLD behaviour, which is the
      safe direction for a number no run can see.
    -->
    <data id="service_retried_carried" expr="0"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ HOW MANY TIMES IT MAY DO THAT BEFORE A PERSON IS ASKED — the ceiling
      the retry did not have, and the second half of the outage story.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHY A CEILING AT ALL, IN ONE SENTENCE: **an unbounded retry is how a
      loop burns a night instead of losing one.** The state above it was built so
      that a 529 costs a run a wait rather than its life, and that is right; what
      it did not say is what happens when the service never comes back. Without
      this the answer was «wait, type, get refused, wait» until `max_seconds`,
      which the shipped kind authors at 24 hours — the same shape, one door over,
      as the silence this document now bounds with `quiet_within_ms`.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE NUMBER MEANS IS THE PRODUCT OF TWO, AND A READER MUST NOT
      TAKE THIS ONE ALONE. The run tolerates roughly
      `service_retry_max × service_retry_ms` of outage — six waits of ten minutes
      is about an hour. An author who lengthens the wait shortens nothing here
      and lengthens the total; the two are edited together or not at all.

      ⚠⚠ SIX, AND THE ARGUMENT IS THE SHAPE OF THE FAILURE RATHER THAN A
      MEASUREMENT — said plainly because this register's rule is that an undated
      number is a guess. A 529 that clears, clears in minutes; a service that has
      been refusing for an hour is not a hiccup, and by then the honest thing to
      report is that nobody could work rather than to keep paying for silence.
      ⚠ It fails in the direction that STOPS, and that is deliberate: stopping is
      visible and a night spent retrying is not.

      ⚠⚠⚠ `'never'` IS NO CEILING, spelled exactly as `max_turns` spells it and
      guarded the same way — this document already has an idiom for «do not bound
      this» and inventing a second one (0? -1?) would make two things that mean
      the same thing look different. A run that authors `'never'` gets what every
      run got before this existed.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE `!= 'never'` HALF OF THAT GUARD IS INERT AT THE PINNED
      ENGINE — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, AND WRITTEN HERE SO NOBODY READS IT AS
      LOAD-BEARING. Deleting it and re-running the gate leaves the gate GREEN:
      `999 >= 'never'` already answers false, so the numeric half decides the
      whole thing on its own. It is KEPT anyway, for two reasons that are not
      «it might work differently»: it says what the author meant, where a bare
      `>=` against a string says only what the engine happens to do; and this
      document's own header rule is that a shape no gate drives is not known to
      work here — a mixed-type comparison is exactly such a shape, and relying on
      its result is relying on the thing that rule forbids.

      ⚠ So the gate's `'never'` arm is a REGRESSION GUARD ON THE ENGINE, not a
      test of this clause. It is labelled that way where it is written; a reader
      who mistakes it for the second is the reason this paragraph exists.
    -->
    <data id="service_retry_max" expr="6"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE CEILING FOR THE OTHER DOOR, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT NUMBER
      BECAUSE GIVING UP COSTS SOMETHING DIFFERENT THERE — register item 724, and
      the residue item 715 wrote down rather than hid.

      715 gave a usage limit a NAME: the peer prints that it is continuing
      automatically, `peer.silent` carries it to `service_down`, and nothing is
      typed at it. What 715 did not give it is SURVIVAL. Both doors were counted
      by `service_retry_max`, whose argument one block up is the shape of a 529 —
      «a server refusing for an hour is not a hiccup» — and a usage limit is not
      that shape at all.

      ⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED, 2026-08-27, IN THE PEER'S OWN TRANSCRIPTS
      (`~/.claude/projects/*.jsonl`): the reset time it prints lands anywhere in
      the day — TEN distinct ones, `3:30am` `2:30am` `8:30am` `12:30pm` `7pm`
      `12pm` `8:10pm` `7:40pm` `11pm` `1:30pm` — and the window from being cut
      off to that reset runs to FIVE HOURS. Run 5 of this repository was cut off
      at 01:0x for an 03:30 reset: two and a half. Six waits of ten minutes is
      one. **The budget was short by a factor of five, and the run called for a
      person over a peer that came back by itself.**

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS DOOR FAILS IN THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION, which is the argument
      and not a preference. `service_retry_max` fails toward STOPPING, on purpose,
      because stopping is visible. That is right where the loop must TYPE to
      resume: each retry puts a word into a service that refused the last one, and
      after an hour the honest report is that nobody could work. THIS door types
      nothing and the peer is returning on its own, so waiting costs wall-clock
      and nothing else — while giving up costs the DIVERGENCE item 715 measured:
      the peer resumed at 3:30, worked two hours, and no run was driving it, so
      nothing judged the work and no milestone accounting saw it. **The invisible
      failure is the one to fail away from, and here that means waiting.**

      ⚠⚠ THIRTY-SIX, DERIVED RATHER THAN CHOSEN: thirty-six waits of ten minutes
      is six hours, covering the measured five-hour window with an hour to spare.
      The spare hour IS a judgement and is said to be one — a bound equal to the
      longest window measured decides by rounding the day a window ends on it.
      What it is not is unbounded: six hours still fails visible well inside
      `max_seconds`, so a peer that printed the notice and then died still reaches
      a person the same day.

      ⚠ AND THIRTY-SIX RETRIES SPEND NO TURNS — measured here rather than hoped:
      `turns` moves only on `turn.done`, and this budget's retries reach `working`
      without one, so a long outage cannot eat the run's own `max_turns`.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE PRODUCT IS RATCHETED RATHER THAN COMMENTED. The block above warns
      that `service_retry_max` and `service_retry_ms` are edited together or not
      at all — a hazard a reader has to remember. This one is held by a gate:
      `the_budget_for_a_peer_that_resumes_itself_outlasts_the_longest_limit_measured`
      reads the shipped kind's own wait alongside this count and refuses a product
      under five hours, so halving the wait without raising this count is RED.

      ⚠ `'never'` IS NO CEILING here as everywhere in this document, and it is the
      same regression guard on the engine that `service_retry_max`'s is rather
      than a load-bearing clause: the numeric half already decides at the pin.
    -->
    <data id="service_resumes_max" expr="36"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ AND WHAT THE AGENT SAYS WHEN THERE IS NOTHING LEFT TO DO AT ALL.

      `done_marker` ends a MILESTONE; this ends the RUN. Until both existed a
      reached milestone was the end of everything: `judging` sent a run that had
      finished one step straight to `closing`, so a loop could only ever be as
      long as its first checkpoint.

      MEASURED, on a live run: an agent asked to work through a LIST of items
      finished ONE, said `MILESTONE REACHED`, and the run converged after a
      single working turn — judged straight into `closing`, and converged. The
      request had been *"keep going until the list is done"*, and no path
      through this document could express it. There was nothing wrong with the
      loop; the document simply had no edge from *this step is done* to *what is
      the next one*.

      ⚠⚠ TWO MARKERS AND NOT ONE, because they are two different claims a person
      would want reported differently: *"the thing you asked for is done"* and
      *"and there is no more of it"*. A run that converged on the first would end
      whenever its agent finished anything.

      ⚠ It is asked for in the REFLECTION and nowhere else. A working turn is
      about the milestone in front of it; whether the whole journey is over is a
      judgement about the journey, which is what a reflection is for.
    -->
    <data id="north_star_marker" expr="'NORTH STAR REACHED'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE TWO LABELS A REFLECTION'S ANSWER IS READ BACK THROUGH, and they are
      authored for `done_marker`'s reason: they are said to somebody's agent, in
      somebody's language, and the driver must never be the thing that writes
      words into a prompt. Declared BEFORE the prompts because `priming` composes
      `reflect_prompt` out of them.

      ⚠⚠⚠ A LABEL AND NOT A WHOLE ROW, where `done_marker` is a whole row, and
      the difference is what each has to carry. *Done* is a yes or a no, so the
      marker IS the answer and `said_done` can demand it stand alone. A milestone
      is a SENTENCE the agent writes, so the label can only ever open the row it
      is on — which is a weaker rule, and the driver pays for that weakness in
      two places rather than pretending it does not exist (see
      `OuterLoop::proposed`): the row must OPEN with the label, and the text
      after it must not be something this loop itself said.

      ⚠ NEITHER APPEARS AT THE START OF A LINE IN THE PROMPT BELOW, deliberately.
      The instruction that asks for them names them mid-sentence, so the peer's
      echo of the prompt does not produce a row that opens with one. That is a
      mitigation and not a proof — a terminal wraps where it likes — which is
      why the driver's second rule exists at all.
    -->
    <data id="milestone_marker" expr="'NEXT MILESTONE:'"/>
    <data id="reference_marker" expr="'NEXT REFERENCE:'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE FOUR COMPOSED PROMPTS ARE DECLARED EMPTY HERE AND WRITTEN IN
      `priming`. Their text is up there, in one place, where a person editing
      this template reads it; declaring them is what makes them assignable, and
      what lets a consumer ask whether this machine is one it can drive at all.

      A loop sitting in `idle` therefore holds three empty strings, and that is
      the honest answer rather than a gap: nothing has been primed, so nothing
      has been composed. What a caller can read before then is the PARTS, which
      are what it filled in.
    -->
    <data id="done_instruction" expr="''"/>
    <!-- Sent once, into a freshly opened session. -->
    <data id="start_prompt" expr="''"/>
    <!-- Sent on every turn after the first. -->
    <data id="turn_prompt" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ SENT WHEN THE LOOP STOPS TO IMPROVE ITSELF, AND IT IS A TURN LIKE ANY
      OTHER — the agent is asked, and what it answers is what the NEXT session
      is briefed with.

      This is the whole difference between a run bounded by one agent's context
      and one that outlives it. Until this prompt existed a reflection could
      carry only what the AUTHOR had written down — the standing instructions a
      `screen_rule` had fired — so a loop replaced its session and greeted the
      replacement with the same milestone somebody wrote before any of the work
      had been done. A run meant to carry on past one checkpoint could only
      re-issue the first one for ever.

      ⚠⚠ WHERE THE WORK SHOULD GO NEXT IS A JUDGEMENT ABOUT THE WORK, so the
      only party that can make it is the agent that has been doing it. The
      driver reads the answer back and hands it over; it never writes one.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT DOES NOT SPEND `max_turns`. A reflection turn is not judged —
      `reflecting` does not pass through `judging` — so the budget still counts
      the turns the agent spent on the WORK. What bounds reflections is
      `reflect_every`, which is what makes their number a function of the
      budget rather than a second unbounded cost.
    -->
    <data id="reflect_prompt" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      Sent once before the loop reports converged. NOT composed — it names
      nothing else in this datamodel — so it stays a plain literal, and a
      reader can tell the two kinds apart by whether `priming` writes them.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT A REPOSITORY ADDS TO ITS OWN CLOSING QUESTION — EMPTY HERE, AND
      DECLARED SO A KIND CAN FILL IT.

      The sentence below is true of every loop that finishes: summarise, say what
      was verified, say what is open. What a PARTICULAR repository wants beyond
      that is its own — a sweep for work the session turned up, a form to file, a
      record to update — and every one of those is a decision about a place this
      file has never been. It belongs in the KIND document, not in a file other
      repositories copy.

      ⚠⚠ DECLARED-AND-EMPTY RATHER THAN ABSENT, for `may_answer`'s reason: a
      `<param>` can only fill an id the child declares, and deleting the
      declaration is the cheap green that makes the split impossible.

      ⚠ It is APPENDED rather than substituted, so a kind cannot quietly drop the
      account this document asks every ending for.
    -->
    <data id="closing_rules" expr="''"/>
    <data id="end_prompt"
          expr="'Summarise what changed, what was verified, and what is left open.' + closing_rules"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE SAME REQUEST MADE OF A RUN THAT IS ENDING WITHOUT GETTING
      THERE — `stopping`'s prompt, and the answer to *"an exhausted run has no
      account at all"*.

      Until this existed, `closing` was the ONLY state that asked its agent for
      anything, so the account belonged to the one ending that needed it least: a
      run that reached its north star has plainly succeeded, and the person who
      started it can see that from the word alone. **The ending a person most
      wants an account of is the one that stopped short**, and that ending
      answered with the single word `exhausted`.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION AND NOT A COPY, which is the whole reason it
      is a second string rather than `end_prompt` reused. *"Summarise what
      changed, what was verified, and what is left open"* is asked of an agent
      that FINISHED; it presumes a body of completed work to summarise, and an
      agent that has run out of turns mid-edit would answer it by describing a
      thing that does not exist. What a person picking this run up needs instead
      is where it got to and what to do first — so this asks for that.

      ⚠ NOTHING OF THE BRIEF IS COMPOSED IN, for `end_prompt`'s reason: the agent
      being asked has been briefed with the north star and the milestone since
      `priming`, and naming them back at it buys nothing it does not already
      hold. What IS composed in is the one thing the agent cannot know and this
      document can — WHICH CEILING ended the run. See `stop_said` below.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT USED TO SAY THE WRONG ONE, TO THE ONE PARTY THAT COULD NOT CHECK.
      This was a single literal opening *"This run has spent its whole turn
      budget"*, and `stopping` is reached by FOUR ceilings: this document's own
      `max_turns`, and the RUN's three (`stop_short`, below). So for three of the
      four the sentence was FALSE — and it is not a journal line a reader can
      weigh, it is TYPED INTO A LIVE AGENT'S PANE in the one turn that asks that
      agent to account for itself. A run a wall clock stopped was told it had run
      out of turns, and whatever it wrote back about *what a run picking this up
      should do first* was reasoned from that. Register item 264, and item 261's
      class one state over: 261 misled a reader of a journal, this misled the
      WITNESS.
    -->
    <data id="stop_opening"
          expr="'This run is ending here, short of what it was asked for.'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH CEILING ENDED IT, IN THIS DOCUMENT'S OWN WORDS — one clause per
      ceiling, keyed by the word the driver spells (`Ceiling::wire_str`).

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE KEYS ARE A CLOSED SET AND THIS MAP IS TOTAL OVER IT — AND THAT IS
      NOT A TIDINESS RULE. MEASURED, by deleting the `duration` clause and
      running the ceiling gate: a missing key does NOT make the `<assign>` in
      `stopping` fail and leave the shipped question standing. The concatenation
      SUCCEEDS and the agent is asked

        "This run is ending here, short of what it was asked for. nil Say where
         you got to: …"

      — the word `nil`, typed into a live agent's pane, in the turn that asks it
      to account for the run. So the gate that holds these keys equal to
      `Ceiling::ALL` is the only thing between a ceiling added in Rust months
      from now and that sentence. It reads THIS FILE, and it goes red the day the
      type grows an arm nobody wrote a clause for.

      ⚠ A DEFENSIVE FALLBACK WAS CONSIDERED AND REFUSED: `stop_said[stop_reason]`
      falling back to an empty clause would compose a true, ceiling-free question
      and nothing anywhere would say the map had a hole. The loud version is the
      one a gate can hold and a reader can see.

      ⚠ ONE PLACE, NOT ONE PER EDGE. `stopping` has two doors and four reasons —
      the outside one carries any of the run's three ceilings — so a clause
      written on the transitions would be the copy-drift this file warns against
      wherever it composes rather than repeats. A ceiling is one line to add here
      and one to delete.

      ⚠ THE PROSE IS THE AUTHOR'S BECAUSE AN AGENT READS IT. The driver knows
      which ceiling fell and says so in the run's JOURNAL, in its own words, to a
      person. What is said to somebody's agent, in somebody's language, is never
      the driver's to write — `done_instruction`'s rule, and the standing rules'.
    -->
    <data id="stop_said"
          expr="{
            turns:      'This run has spent every turn its document budgeted for it.',
            iterations: 'This run has taken every step its run was allowed.',
            cost:       'This run has spent everything its run was allowed to spend.',
            duration:   'This run has run out of the wall-clock time its run was given.'
          }"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ WHAT IS ACTUALLY ASKED, kept apart from the opening so the ceiling
      clause has somewhere to go BETWEEN them. Every stand-in peer in this crate
      keys its answer on a verbatim slice of this, and one paints a second slice
      as its wrapped echo — reworded, a peer stops recognising the question, its
      turn never ends, and the gate reports a wall clock instead of a budget.
    -->
    <data id="stop_asking"
          expr="'Say where you got to: what you finished, what you left half-done, and what a run picking this up should do first.'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ SHIPPED WITHOUT A CEILING CLAUSE AND TRUE THAT WAY, which is what a
      caller previewing a loop should see: WHICH ceiling will end a run is not
      known until one does, and a preview that named one would be this defect one
      layer out. `stopping` composes the clause in when the answer exists.

      ⚠ COMPOSED FROM THE PARTS RATHER THAN FROM ITSELF, so entering `stopping`
      twice would compose the same sentence twice rather than accumulate — the
      state cannot be re-entered today (every one of its transitions targets a
      final), and this does not rest on that staying true.
    -->
    <data id="stop_prompt" expr="stop_opening + ' ' + stop_asking"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT AN AGENT WHOSE CLAIM WAS REFUSED IS TOLD — register item 448,
      and the half of item 428 that was never built.

      An independent check refusing a milestone is item 428 working. The agent
      hearing NOTHING about it is not: the refusing edge sent the ORDINARY turn
      prompt, so the next turn opened with *Continue toward: …* and nothing
      else. Measured live on run 16 — iterations 6, 8, 10 … 22, NINE refusals,
      **1129 identical bytes each**. The agent said the same thing every time
      because it was asked the same thing every time, and the one party who
      could act on the verdict was the one party never shown it.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE COST OF THE SILENCE IS PAID TWICE. The run buys a turn per refusal
      (deliberately — a convergence nobody earned is the expensive mistake), and
      an agent that does not know why it was refused spends that turn re-doing
      what it just did. Nine turns bought, nine turns spent on the same claim.

      ⚠⚠ AND THIS IS `standing`'s SHAPE REFUSED, DELIBERATELY. `screening` solved
      the identical disease with a standing instruction carried in the datamodel
      and composed into every later prompt — right there, wrong here: a verdict
      about THIS milestone that outlived the milestone would go on telling an
      agent about a claim it has since abandoned. What is true here is true for
      exactly one turn, so it is composed for exactly one turn, on entry to the
      state that delivers it.
    -->
    <data id="dispute_opening"
          expr="'You said this milestone was reached. An independent check was shown what this turn produced and did not agree.'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE CHECKER'S OWN WORDS, FRAMED AS QUOTED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. The
      clause the reason arrives in has to say WHOSE reason it is: an agent told
      *the artifact is empty* in this document's voice reads it as the loop's
      finding, and this document has not looked at anything. Register item 461
      is what makes there be words at all; this is what stops them being put in
      the wrong mouth.
    -->
    <data id="dispute_quoting" expr="'It said:'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND WHAT IS SAID WHEN IT GAVE NO REASON, which is a real answer and
      not a defensive fallback. The check's prompt ASKS for a reason (item 448's
      first half), and a checker may still answer the bare word — `explained` is
      `None` for exactly that, and `judges` is careful to answer `None` rather
      than an echo of our own question.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS SPELLED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO THE CONCATENATION, AND THE
      SENTENCE IT REPLACES WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED. Deleting the branch
      below and composing the reason unconditionally tells a live agent

        "… did not agree. It said: false Do not repeat that claim as it stands…"

      — the wire's *nobody explained* rendered as the check's own words, in the
      turn where the agent is being asked to act on them. `stop_said` measured
      the same class one prompt over and got `nil`; the word differs because the
      absence travels differently, and the failure is identical. A refusal the
      agent would try to ACT on is worse than the silence this item is about.
    -->
    <data id="dispute_unexplained"
          expr="'It gave no reason beyond the verdict.'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT IS ACTUALLY ASKED, kept apart from the opening so the check's
      words have somewhere to go BETWEEN them — `stop_asking`'s arrangement, for
      `stop_asking`'s reason.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT ASKS FOR THE OBJECTION TO BE ANSWERED AND NOT FOR THE CLAIM TO BE
      DROPPED. The check can be wrong — run 16's own refusals were about evidence
      the agent's account did not SHOW rather than work it had not done — so an
      instruction to abandon the milestone would spend the bought turn undoing
      finished work. Both remedies are named: fix what it names, or show what it
      could not see.
    -->
    <data id="dispute_asking"
          expr="'Do not repeat that claim as it stands: either fix what the check names, or show the evidence it was not shown. Then carry on.'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ THE CLAUSE THE REASON ARRIVES IN, composed on entry to `disputing` out
      of the two above. Empty here because no claim has been refused yet, which
      is the honest preview: WHICH words a check will use is not knowable until
      one refuses.
    -->
    <data id="dispute_said" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ SHIPPED WITHOUT THE CHECK'S CLAUSE AND WITHOUT THE TURN, and true that
      way — `stop_prompt`'s arrangement exactly. A caller previewing a loop sees
      what its agent will be told when a check refuses it; what the check will
      SAY, and which milestone the turn will name, are facts that do not exist
      until a run reaches them.

      ⚠ COMPOSED FROM THE PARTS RATHER THAN FROM ITSELF, so a run refused three
      times composes one sentence three times rather than three sentences once —
      and this state IS re-entered, unlike `stopping`, so that is load-bearing
      here rather than merely careful.
    -->
    <data id="dispute_prompt" expr="dispute_opening + ' ' + dispute_asking"/>

    <!--
      Standing instructions for dialogs the person has already decided about.
      When the inner agent raises a question no consent covers, `screening`
      matches it against these before anybody is woken; a match TURNS THE CALL
      DOWN and tells the agent what to do instead, and the run carries on.

      This is not the loop deciding. It is the loop CARRYING OUT a decision the
      person made in advance and wrote down — which is why the rules live in the
      authored half of this document and not in the driver. A rule with no match
      wakes the person, exactly as before.

      ⚠⚠⚠ EACH RULE IS `when` (WORDS THE DIALOG CARRIES) AND `text` (WHAT TO SAY
      ONCE THE CALL IS REFUSED), and both halves of that shape were decided by
      measurement rather than by argument.

      * `when` USED TO NAME A DIALOG *KIND* — 'design-decision', 'multiple-choice'
        — which needed somebody to classify other people's dialogs into a
        taxonomy, and `sprag-detect` classifies none. R383 asked a live agent
        instead: three tool families ask three DIFFERENT questions and one clause
        quoting the agent's own words covers all three. A taxonomy buys nothing
        quoting does not already have, and it would rot the first time an agent
        reworded anything. So a rule quotes, exactly as a consent's `asked` does,
        and they share one matcher.

      * `keys` IS GONE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE SAFETY PROPERTY. R384 pressed
        Escape, Tab and the offered `3` at a live claude's permission dialog:
        **Escape REFUSES THE TOOL CALL** (`User rejected write to PROBE.txt`, the
        file never created) in 25 ms, identically to `3. No` — while **Tab leaves
        the dialog up** and rewrites option 1 into *"Yes, and tell Claude what to
        do next"*, so the probe that typed into it **had its file written**.

        A rule that could name its own key could therefore name the key that
        APPROVES. The key is the product's now (`sprag_plugin::screen::REFUSES`),
        and a rule authors only which dialog and what to say — so a standing
        instruction cannot grant a permission, structurally rather than by hope.

      ⚠⚠ AND THAT IS WHY THERE IS NO `screen_permissions` FLAG ANY MORE. It read:
      *"approving a permission grants an agent the ability to act, and a standing
      yes is a different kind of promise than a standing think-about-it-again;
      nobody gets it by writing a rule that happened to match."* Every word of
      that is still true and none of it needs a flag: the act's first key is a
      REFUSAL, so no rule can reach the promise the flag was guarding. What the
      flag could never have done is tell a permission dialog from a design one —
      it needed the very taxonomy R383 measured away.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE SHIPPED RULE USED TO BE A PLACEHOLDER, and half of its reason has
      been discharged by measurement since. It read: *«a shipped needle would have
      to be invented: the dialogs a loop meets are MEASURED for tool permissions
      and unmeasured for design questions, and quoting words nobody has seen is
      the mistake this file made the last time»*. **The first half of that
      sentence is the discharge**: tool-permission dialogs ARE measured, and every
      one seen so far — `Do you want to make this edit?`, `Do you want to create
      PROBE.txt?` — opens with the same four words. So the needle below quotes
      what has been SEEN; it is not invented, and the design-question case is
      still left to whoever meets one.

      ⚠⚠ AND AN UNEDITED PLACEHOLDER WAS NOT VISIBLE, WHICH IS WHY THIS CHANGED.
      The argument for it was that a loop meeting an unclaimed dialog reports
      `no_rule` naming it. True — and what a person does with that report is
      answer the dialog, which nobody was there to do: the live run of 2026-08-16
      stood at one for an hour and died at a ceiling. A document whose shipped
      state cannot run its own loop is not a safe default, it is a broken one.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT QUOTES `create`, NOT THE WHOLE `Do you want to` FAMILY — a
      widening that was tried and MEASURED WRONG in the same round. The bare
      family needle swallows the COMMAND dialog (`Bash command … Do you want to
      proceed?`), so a loop carrying this rule would have been refused every
      `cargo test` it asked for and told to think again: **a loop that may edit
      but may not run is a loop that cannot verify a milestone it claims to have
      reached.** Running is a consent's job, one door up, and it has one now.

      ⚠ WHAT IS LEFT FOR SUCH A RULE IS THE PAIR THIS COMMENT HAS ALREADY USED
      TWICE: `Do you want to create PROBE.txt?` writes the word *ready*, and `Do
      you want to create DESIGN.txt?` commits 256 lines that chose JSON over
      SQLite. **No quote separates those two** — which is `judged_rules`'
      argument, not this one.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT SHIPS EMPTY, FOR `may_answer`'s REASON ONE DOOR DOWN. A rule is
      weaker than a consent — the act's first key is a REFUSAL
      (`screen::REFUSES`), so it can never GRANT anything, and the worst it can
      cause is an agent told to reconsider too often. But it is still somebody
      being answered on somebody else's behalf, in words this file's author
      chose, in a language this file's author speaks. A template does not know
      whose agent it will be talking to.

      So a rule belongs in the ADOPTING REPOSITORY'S KIND DOCUMENT, beside its
      consents and read the same way — see `may_answer` for the whole
      arrangement and for why a kind is a sibling rather than a parent.

      ⚠⚠ AN EMPTY LIST IS NOT A SAFE DEFAULT, IT IS A NEUTRAL ONE. A loop meeting
      an unclaimed dialog reports `no_rule` naming it and waits for a person —
      correct, and useless when nobody is watching. A repository that means to
      run this unattended writes its rules down first.
    -->
    <data id="screen_rules" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH DIALOGS THIS LOOP MAY ANSWER *YES* TO, and with which option —
      `screen_rules`'s opposite number, and the only thing in this file that can
      put a byte into a permission dialog.

      Each clause is `{ asked, answer }`: text the QUESTION must carry for the
      clause to be about it, and text the OPTION must carry for the clause to
      authorise it. Empty means ANSWER NOTHING, which is what every loop did
      before consents existed and is still the honest default — a run that types
      into a menu nobody authorised is the failure class the whole contract sits
      inside.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT MOVED HERE FROM A CONSTRUCTION ARGUMENT, and the reason the old note
      gave for keeping it out no longer holds. It read: *«a construction argument,
      because the barrier holds it and the barrier is built once»* — true until
      the round that made the barrier re-read what it needs at the top of every
      pass. What was left was the same asymmetry twice over: refusal authored in
      this file, approval authored somewhere this file cannot see.

      ⚠⚠ WHOSE DECISION IT IS HAS NOT CHANGED, and this is the paragraph one door
      up saying it: a tool permission is the PERSON's. An author writing a clause
      here and a caller passing one are both *the person having decided in
      advance, in writing* — never the machine deciding. What the loop still
      never does is decide something nobody wrote down, and a dialog neither a
      clause nor a rule reaches still stops the run and waits for somebody.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE COST OF WRITING ONE HERE IS THAT IT IS COMMITTED, and that is the
      real difference from an argument. A clause in this file authorises EVERY
      run of it, for everybody, until somebody edits the file — where an argument
      authorised one run. **A standing yes is a different kind of promise from a
      standing think-about-it-again.**

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT SHIPS EMPTY, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS SAFE. Measured, in this
      document's own history: an empty list met `Do you want to make this edit?`
      on a run's first milestone, nobody answered, and it stood in
      `awaiting_human` until an iteration ceiling ended it. Every milestone a
      loop like this exists to reach touches a file, so a run whose consents are
      empty is a run that stops at the first dialog.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO WHERE DO THE CLAUSES GO? INTO THE ADOPTING REPOSITORY'S OWN
      DOCUMENT. A clause here would authorise EVERY run of this file, in every
      repository that copied it, until somebody edited it — and the people it
      would be deciding for are people this file's author has never met. A
      standing yes is the one thing a template must not carry.

      So a KIND document holds them: a small `.scxml` beside this one, owned by
      the repository whose runs it authorises, whose datamodel declares exactly
      the ids this one declares. The driver reads that document and assigns its
      values here at `start` — which is what the unconditional `<assign>` in
      `idle` is for. This file stays the machine; the kind stays the decisions.

      ⚠ A KIND IS A SIBLING, NOT A PARENT, and that was measured rather than
      preferred: a driver cannot read, step or send to an `<invoke>`d child at
      the pinned engine, so a template invoked by its kind would be a template
      nothing could drive.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AN AUTHOR WRITING TWO CLAUSES SHOULD WRITE BOTH HALVES. A run allowed
      to edit but not to run commands cannot test what it edited, and a milestone
      a loop calls reached is one it has verified. **One consent is not half a
      working loop, it is a loop that stops at the other dialog.**

      ⚠⚠ AND NO `answer` NEEDLE SHOULD BE `Yes`, measured: the edit dialog offers
      `1. Yes` / `2. Yes, allow all edits during this session` and the command
      dialog offers `1. Yes` / `2. Yes, and do not ask again` / `3. No, and tell
      me what to do`. **`Yes` is carried by two options in each, so it names
      neither** — `Consents` answers nothing at all when a needle reaches two
      (`a_needle_two_options_carry_authorises_neither`). A needle must be text
      only ONE option carries.

      ⚠ A clause may never name the key that APPROVES a dialog `screen_rules`
      would refuse: rules author WHICH dialog and WHAT TO SAY, never the key, and
      `screen.rs` holds the measurement that settled it. Approval has its own
      door and this is it.
    -->
    <data id="may_answer" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT MAKES A DIALOG ONE THIS LOOP TURNS DOWN, and what it says instead.

      A `screen_rule` above QUOTES a dialog. This JUDGES one — and the difference
      is not a preference, it is a measured limit. These two dialogs ask the
      identical question:

          Do you want to create PROBE.txt?    <- writes the word "ready"
          Do you want to create DESIGN.txt?   <- commits 256 lines that chose
                                                 JSON over SQLite

      No quote separates them, because what differs is what the action MEANS,
      and that is in the diff the dialog carries rather than in its wording.

      ⚠⚠ THIS IS NOT THE DIALOG-KIND TAXONOMY COMING BACK. R383 removed one
      because it asked an author to name a KIND from a list somebody else
      maintained, and `sprag-detect` classifies none. A criterion is the author's
      own sentence about their own run, decided per dialog against that dialog's
      actual contents rather than looked up in a table.

      ⚠ MEASURED, which is why it is spellable at all: a cheap judge (haiku, in
      print mode) separated four captured live dialogs 3 times
      out of 3, the identical-sentence pair included, at 4-6 s per call. The
      verdict is the FIRST WORD of the reply — the same model answers some
      dialogs in one word and others with a paragraph, so a reader taking the
      whole reply would call a stable verdict unstable.

      ⚠⚠⚠ A LIST, SO A DECISION IS ONE ELEMENT TO ADD AND ONE TO DELETE. Each
      carries three things and nothing else:

        name   what to call it. The driver publishes this as
               `_event.data.rule`, so a document that wants DIFFERENT handling
               per decision routes on it and one that does not can ignore it.
        judge  the sentence a second agent answers about ONE dialog.
        text   what the agent is told once that dialog has been refused.

      Shaped after `screen_rules` above deliberately: an author who has written
      one has written the other, and the only difference is whether the claim is
      quoted or judged. To add a decision, add an element:

        { name: 'security',
          judge: 'going ahead would weaken an authentication or authorisation
                  boundary',
          text:  'Stop and state the boundary this crosses before continuing.' }

      ⚠⚠ THE EMPTY LIST IS THE DEFAULT AND DECLINES THIS ENTIRELY: no judge is
      asked, no model call is paid for, and every blocked turn goes to
      `screening` exactly as it did before this existed. A run that did not ask
      for a second agent must not acquire one.

      ⚠ AND A LIST HERE IS ONLY HALF. The other half is WHO answers — the
      caller's `judge` argument — which lives nowhere in this document. Rules
      with nobody to ask change nothing, which is a run's default state.
    -->
    <data id="judged_rules" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ HOW A STANDING INSTRUCTION IS INTRODUCED once it has been carried out,
      and this prose is AUTHORED for the same reason the rules' own `text` is: it
      is said to somebody's agent, in somebody's language, and the driver must
      never be the thing that writes words into a prompt.

      A rule fires ONCE — `screening` refuses the call, types the instruction and
      the turn carries on — and until this existed that was the whole of its
      life. R384's live agent measured what that costs, in its own words:

          "the loop is re-issuing the original milestone, but your last direct
           instruction was to not create that file … 루프가 매 턴 같은 요청을
           반복하고 저는 매 턴 같은 이유로 거절하게 되므로, 진전이 없습니다."

      Measured offline as two numbers on a six-turn run: the instruction reached
      the pane ONCE and the milestone it overrides was asked for SIX times. So an
      instruction that has to STICK has to reach every later prompt, which means
      it has to be one of the PARTS the prompts are composed from — and the
      composition happens in `priming`, so adopting one is a session
      replacement. That is what `reflecting` and `restarting` are for.
    -->
    <data id="standing_label"
          expr="'A standing instruction you have already been given, which overrides the milestone wherever the two conflict: '"/>

    <!--
      Budgets. `max_turns` bounds the run; `reflect_every` sets how often
      the loop stops to improve itself. `judged_rules` is beside them because a
      judged dialog costs a model call too — see its own comment.

      ⚠⚠⚠ BOTH ARE COUNTS OF TURNS, AND A TURN IS NOT A UNIT OF ANYTHING THE
      BILL IS DENOMINATED IN. Re-measured over 76 local sessions of 20 requests
      or more (33,395 turn-to-turn samples): what one billed request adds to the
      agent's context is 683 tokens at the median, 1,353 at p75, 2,542 at p90,
      10,843 at p99 and 888,072 at the maximum. A `date` invocation and a
      3,000-line file read are both one turn, and the two ends of that spread
      are a factor of 1,300 apart.

      ⚠ THESE ARE ONE MACHINE'S NUMBERS AND THE TEMPLATE SAYS SO. They are one
      person's usage, not a team average, and an author adopting this file should
      re-derive rather than inherit them: every billed request records
      `cache_read_input_tokens`, and the per-turn growth is the difference
      between consecutive ones. The numbers are here because a reader deciding a
      cadence needs to know the SHAPE of the spread; the shape is what travels,
      the values are not.

      * `max_turns` IS NOT A COST CEILING. It is honestly named — it bounds
        turns — and `Ceiling::Turns` says so. But two runs both ending
        `exhausted, 40 of 40` are not comparable on spend, because cost tracks
        accumulated context and context per turn spans three orders of
        magnitude. A caller who wants a budget wants a different ceiling, and
        this loop does not offer one.

      * `reflect_every` IS ALSO A COST KNOB, WHICH IS NOT WHY IT IS HERE.
        `reflecting` exits through `restarting`, which throws the accumulated
        context away and pays a cold start to rebuild the fixed prefix. Whether
        that trade wins depends on how much there was to throw away: a
        restart-cost curve measured against this loop found a restart paying for
        itself only above roughly 15,000-21,000 tokens of discardable context,
        and found this default losing by 11.4% on its workload. The same task in
        one session and in three cost 0.5795 and 0.6456 dollars: the split saved
        0.023 on cache reads and spent 0.073 rebuilding caches.

        ⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHY A RESTART IS SO HARD TO PAY FOR, WHICH IS THE PART A CADENCE
        ARGUMENT KEEPS SKIPPING. Writing a cache costs TWENTY TIMES what reading
        one does (6.00 against 0.30 per million), so a restart must save twenty
        times what it rewrites merely to break even. Measured, it saved 38,550
        tokens per restart and came up 3.2 to 4.4 times short.

        ⚠⚠ AND MOST OF A SESSION'S CONTEXT CANNOT BE DISCARDED AT ALL. The
        second request already reads about 38,500 tokens in the session this was
        measured on — 21,350 at the median of the 250 measured where the loop
        actually runs, which is a TOOL SET's difference and not a drift; that
        is the system prompt and the tool definitions, and a restart pays for it
        again rather than escaping it. What a restart can actually drop is only
        what sits ON TOP: 17,358 tokens at the end of the single session, about
        5,400 at the end of each third. Even at its most expensive, the
        discardable part was 31% of the floor beneath it.

        ⚠ HOW MUCH IS REWRITTEN CAN BE COUNTED THREE WAYS AND THEY DISAGREE ON
        THE MARGIN, not on the sign: the whole segment's cache writes (14,767,
        7.7x short), the first request measured on its own (8,477, 4.4x short),
        or the marginal increase the split added (6,089, 3.2x short). The 11.4%
        above does not depend on the choice; it was measured per component.

        ⚠⚠⚠ AND A NEW SESSION'S FIRST TURN COSTS 4.7 TIMES A LATER ONE, of which
        cache writing is 88%. That is the toll on every restart, and it is
        charged again each time. It also GROWS with the author's own base
        context: the 4.7 was measured on a nearly empty project, so a repository
        with a large standing instruction file pays more than this.

        ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE AWAITED FIGURE HAS ARRIVED, AND IT IS RECORDED HERE SO THE NEXT
        READER DOES NOT DERIVE IT AGAIN. This entry used to say the sign was
        UNMEASURED for real work, against an estimate of 1,745 tokens per turn.
        Re-measured over the 76 sessions above, real work accumulates 2,342 per
        turn, so eight turns discard 18,736 — INSIDE the 15,000-21,000 band
        rather than under it. The experiment's own task grew at 755, which is
        32% of the real rate, which is why it read as a clear loss.

        So the settled reading at `reflect_every = 8` is *somewhere between a
        small loss and break-even*: the direction is probably still a loss, and
        there is no longer any basis for writing *by a wide margin*. Below eight
        the margin worsens, because the discardable amount falls while the toll
        does not.

      ⚠⚠ SO DO NOT TUNE EITHER NUMBER ON COST ALONE. What `reflect_every` is
      actually for is how often the loop gets to improve its own setup, and
      whether eight turns is the right cadence FOR THAT has never been measured
      at all. Changing it to save money would trade a measured axis against an
      unmeasured one.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE SENTENCE THE WHOLE MEASUREMENT REDUCES TO: *"keep sessions
      short"* and *"keep context small"* ARE NOT THE SAME INSTRUCTION, and only
      the second one is right. Splitting a session is not a cost measure. The
      quantity to reduce is not the number of sessions; it is how much each turn
      is charged to re-read.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IF A RESTART POLICY IS EVER WRITTEN, IT MUST NOT READ THESE
      COUNTERS. Predicting context from turn count is off by 19% at the median,
      63% at p90 and 30x at worst — against a 15-21k threshold, an eight-turn
      estimate carries a band that straddles the entire decision. The exact
      quantity is reported on every billed request as `cache_read_input_tokens`,
      and an interactive inner session writes it to
      `~/.claude/projects/<cwd-slug>/<session>.jsonl` even though it publishes
      no structured JSON result envelope for `reply.rs` to read (that is print
      mode's `output-format json`, which this loop forbids). Nothing needs to be
      instrumented; the number is already being written down.

      ⚠⚠ AND THE QUANTITY THAT POLICY WOULD WANT HAS A ONE-LINE FORM, so nobody
      has to estimate it: the discardable amount is THIS request's cache read
      minus the SECOND request's of the same session. The subtrahend is the
      floor described above, which a restart cannot escape; what remains is the
      only part a restart actually drops.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THIS DOCUMENT DOES WITH ALL OF IT IS HAND IT OVER. `reflecting`
      composes these quantities into the prompt it sends, so the agent weighing
      what to do next can weigh its own cadence too, and NOTHING GUARDS ON A
      COUNT OF TURNS — that is what this whole entry is about and it has not
      changed.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT DID CHANGE IS THAT A GUARD ON THE QUANTITY ITSELF NOW EXISTS, and
      the difference is the point rather than a detail: `reviewing` replaces a
      session once `context - floor` has reached twenty times `cold`, which is a
      break-even computed from what the run measured about itself. The paragraph
      that used to stand here called a threshold *"a policy nobody can defend"*,
      and it was defending the count against a threshold placed inside a band —
      see `context`, which records both the refusal and the re-measurement that
      ended it. ⚠ The CADENCE is still not a cost knob and still must not be
      tuned as one.
    -->
    <data id="max_turns" expr="40"/>
    <data id="reflect_every" expr="8"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ HOW LONG `awaiting_human` WAITS FOR THE PERSON, in milliseconds — the
      number that state's only run-ending edge is written against.

      This document has always DESCRIBED it (*"wait up to their patience, then
      `unattended`"*) while a construction argument OWNED it, and that is the
      split this round ends. It is the arrangement `screen_rules` already has and
      for the reason written beside `Brief::screen_rules`: **a field on the spec
      would be a SECOND place the same decision lives, and the failure of letting
      two copies drift is silent.**

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE COST OF THE OLD ARRANGEMENT WAS MEASURED, not argued. A live run sat
      at one permission dialog for an hour and was ended by `max_iterations` — a
      ceiling this document cannot see either — so this state's own `unattended`
      never fired, and the run reported *exhausted (iterations)*: a sentence
      about a hundred thousand steps of work, for thirteen transitions and a
      question nobody answered. Register items 275, 276, 279, 280.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS AUTHORED AND NOT DERIVED, and says so the way `repeat_limit` does.
      An hour is a person's coffee break rather than a measurement; what would
      make it right has never been measured, and a number that pretended
      otherwise would be the folklore this workspace keeps finding.

      ⚠ ZERO MEANS NOBODY IS WATCHING — the run ends at the first dialog it
      cannot answer, reporting `blocked` WITH the question. It answers for both
      keys, and is the way to say it.
    -->
    <data id="await_person_ms" expr="3600000"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ HOW STILL A PERSON'S HAND MUST BE BEFORE THE PANE IS THE RUN'S AGAIN,
      in milliseconds — `await_person_ms`'s other half, and one decision with it:
      `Handback` lives INSIDE `Attended::APerson`, so *hand the pane back to a run
      nobody is watching* cannot be constructed.

      ⚠⚠ Measured before the key existed: a supervisor typed ONE key into a pane a
      loop was driving, finished, and let go — and left a run holding
      **thirty-seven of its forty iterations unspent** with its goal one turn
      away. Somebody had to restart it by hand.

      ⚠⚠⚠ ZERO MEANS NEVER — a person who takes this pane keeps it, and the run
      ends. That is `Handback::Never`, what every run did before the key existed,
      and what the wire says the key's ABSENCE means; a document that spells
      absence as zero says the same thing. It is NOT the mirror of the key above:
      that one turns the whole wait off, this one only declines to take the pane
      back.
    -->
    <data id="handback_still_ms" expr="15000"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ HOW LONG SOMEBODY MAY HOLD THIS RUN BEFORE IT IS ABANDONED, in
      milliseconds. Four hours: longer than any session of reading a pane, and
      shorter than a night.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THE HOLD NEEDS A CEILING OF ITS OWN, MEASURED. `hold` is the one
      order with a way back, and until this key existed it was the one order with
      no ENDING. A held run waits on the person letting go and on nothing else:
      its patience is deliberately not spent while they hold it (see
      `awaiting_human`), `unattended` is refused for it by the `held` state
      below, and `max_iterations` cannot bound a step that never returns. So a
      person who held a run and went home left it parked on a pane, holding a
      daemon slot, until somebody cancelled it by hand. Register item 534.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS AUTHORED AND NOT DERIVED, and says so the way `await_person_ms`
      two entries up does. Four hours is a working afternoon rather than a
      measurement; what would make it right has never been measured, and the
      honest thing is to say which it is.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS NOT `await_person_ms` REUSED, and the two are different questions
      asked of the same person. That one is how long a run waits for somebody to
      deal with a dialog it may not answer; this one is how long a run waits for
      somebody who has told it to stop and LOOK. A run may be held while nobody
      is watching at all, which is exactly the case that used to park for ever,
      so this ceiling cannot live inside the contract that says whether anybody
      is expected.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THERE IS NO SPELLING FOR *"no ceiling"*, and that is the decision
      rather than an omission. An unbounded hold has no ending anybody can be
      told about, and this document already spent a whole final saying what an
      immortal run costs (see `peer_gone`). A caller who wants a long hold names
      a long number.

      ⚠⚠ ZERO IS REFUSED rather than read as a bound of zero: *hold this run and
      end it at once* is `cancel` spelled wrong, and a caller who arrives at zero
      by arithmetic is the one who needs telling rather than obeying. The driver
      refuses the brief, the way it refuses an unreadable patience.
    -->
    <data id="hold_within_ms" expr="14400000"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ HOW LONG THE BARRIER WAITS FOR THE PANE TO BE READY FOR THIS LOOP'S
      FIRST PROMPT, in milliseconds — three minutes, and the number is a person's
      allowance rather than a fact about any program.

      ⚠⚠⚠ ITS COMPANION `ready_when` IS NOT HERE, AND THE SPLIT IS THE WHOLE
      POINT. What makes a pane ready is DERIVED from which program is in it —
      `AiLoopSpec::driving` builds `settles(agent)` out of the agent's own name,
      and it is the same for every run against that peer. How long anybody is
      willing to wait for it is not read off the peer at all: nothing about
      `claude` says 180,000 and nothing about it says 60,000. **The wire
      validates the two keys as a pair; a wire pairing is not evidence of a
      shared owner** (register item 300).

      ⚠⚠ THE NUMBER IS THE ONE THE LIVE LOOP HAS BEEN PASSING AS A RUN ARGUMENT
      since it started — `ready_timeout_ms 180000` on the command line in the
      skill that drives it, where a person had already had to decide it.
      It is authored here rather than invented: a cold `claude` on this machine
      takes tens of seconds, and two minutes (the substrate's own default, chosen
      for a shell) had already been judged too short by whoever wrote that line.

      ⚠⚠ ZERO IS A BOUND OF ZERO — one look, then `NeverReady` — and NOT *"use
      the substrate's default"*, unlike `handback_still_ms` two entries up. The
      asymmetry is not taste: `Handback` has two VARIANTS and needed a spelling
      for the second, and this is a plain duration a caller has always been able
      to send verbatim. Reading a zero here as *decline* would change what
      `ready_timeout_ms: 0` does to a caller who has been sending it.

      ⚠ THIS DOCUMENT NEEDS NO SPELLING FOR *"decline"*. A `<data>` always holds
      a number; the ABSENCE that used to mean *the substrate's default* lives on
      the wire, where omitting the key now means THIS FILE DECIDES.
    -->
    <data id="ready_timeout_ms" expr="180000"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ HOW LONG ONE OF THE INNER AGENT'S TURNS MAY TAKE, in milliseconds —
      half an hour, and `ready_timeout_ms`'s argument applied to the other end of
      a turn.

      ⚠⚠⚠ ITS COMPANION `done_when` IS NOT HERE FOR THE SAME REASON. Which
      evidence ends a turn is how the PROGRAM signals it has stopped —
      `INNER_SESSION_ENDS` is a fact about an agent CLI that settles rather than
      exits, true of every run against one. How long a person will let one turn
      run before the loop stops waiting is a judgement, and this document is
      where the judgements live.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT IT BOUNDS IS THE WAIT, NOT THE AGENT. Nothing here interrupts a
      peer: the bound decides when `working` stops WATCHING for this pass. So a
      number that is too small does not kill a turn — it makes the loop stop
      looking at one that had not finished, and look again on the next pump.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE SENTENCE ABOVE USED TO SAY «the machine is told `turn.blocked`
      instead of `turn.done`», WHICH THE PRODUCT HAS NEVER DONE. `Over::NotYet`
      reaches `AiLoopEvent::Null` — *nothing happened that this document has a
      word for* — and the machine stays exactly where it is. `turn.blocked` is
      what a peer that stopped to ASK produces, which is a different fact
      arriving by a different door. The correction is written here rather than
      quietly applied because the false version is the one that made this bound
      look like it had a ceiling behind it: it does not, and `quiet_within_ms`
      below is the ceiling that was missing.

      ⚠ ZERO MEANS THIS DOCUMENT DECLARES NO BOUND OF ITS OWN, and the run's own
      clock and cancel — the bounds every run already has — are the only ones.
      That is what the key's ABSENCE meant on the wire, and it is a real answer
      rather than a degenerate one: `Turn::lasting` REFUSES a zero bound — on the
      wire too, still, through `opt_ai_loop_turn_ms` — so zero cannot mean *wait
      no time at all*, which leaves exactly one reading.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS THE OPPOSITE READING TO `ready_timeout_ms` ABOVE, and that is on
      purpose rather than an inconsistency: a zero barrier is a thing a caller
      can mean and has always been able to send, and a zero turn bound is not and
      never could be. **What a zero means is decided by whether the wire ever
      accepted one.**
    -->
    <data id="turn_within_ms" expr="1800000"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ HOW LONG NOTHING MAY SPEAK FOR THE PANE before the loop stops calling
      its peer «thinking» and asks for a person, in milliseconds — TEN MINUTES,
      inside `turn_within_ms`'s thirty.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT IT IS FOR, MEASURED, AND WHY THE BOUND ABOVE COULD NOT DO IT.
      On 2026-08-19 a turn a person stopped with Escape emitted NO PAYLOAD OF ANY
      KIND for fourteen minutes: the agent restores the prompt into its composer
      and suppresses its own idle nag while the composer holds text, so nothing
      was ever going to speak again. The pane read `working seq=6 asked=2 said=0`
      throughout — which is exactly what a long turn reads — and the loop
      re-waited its half hour, pass after pass, toward the 24-hour ceiling. Both
      of that day's incidents were ended by a PERSON typing at the pane.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE TWO BOUNDS MEASURE DIFFERENT THINGS AND ONE NUMBER CANNOT SAY
      BOTH. A turn may legitimately take the whole half hour; it may not be
      SILENT for ten minutes of it, because a turn that is working reports every
      time it calls a tool. That fourth counter is what makes this decidable at
      all — `seq` counts published verdicts, and a turn calling tools publishes
      none of them, so before it existed a working turn and a dead one were the
      same picture.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE EDGE IT PRODUCES IS `peer.silent`, AND IT GOES TO `awaiting_human`
      RATHER THAN ENDING ANYTHING. The driver cannot tell a frozen agent from a
      reporter that was replaced under a running daemon — a rebuild does exactly
      that — so the honest remedy is the person this document already knows how
      to wait for. A peer that speaks again is back in `working` by `turn.done`
      and has lost nothing; a person who never comes reaches `blocked` through
      `unattended`, on `await_person_ms` and not on a clock invented for this.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHERE TEN MINUTES COMES FROM — MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN. The longest
      thing a turn of this loop does in silence is ONE TOOL CALL: `PreToolUse`
      reports when it starts and `PostToolUse` when it ends, and between them
      nothing speaks. Over **1,972 real build-and-test commands a working loop
      ran** (2026-08-19): p50 = 7 s, p90 = 107 s, p99 = 273 s, and the LONGEST
      EVER = 447 s. Ten minutes clears the measured maximum by two and a half.

      ⚠⚠ AN AUTHOR WHOSE AGENT DOES SOMETHING SLOWER RE-MEASURES THIS, and the
      measurement is the same one: the longest single tool call the work makes.
      A loop that compiles a large tree, waits on a remote job or sleeps on a
      deploy has a longer one, and this number is the only thing that has to
      move.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE HONEST DIRECTION TO BE WRONG IN IS *SHORT*, WHICH IS THE
      OPPOSITE OF `turn_within_ms` ABOVE — so the two numbers must not be reasoned
      about together. Too small here raises ONE attention notice at a peer that
      was merely slow, and `awaiting_human`'s own `turn.done` picks the turn
      up when it lands, so the run loses nothing but the noise. Too large is the
      twenty-four hours this exists to end. A bound that cries wolf is a cost
      somebody pays attention with; a bound that never fires costs a day.

      ⚠⚠ A VALUE AT OR ABOVE `turn_within_ms` DECIDES NOTHING, and that is a
      real authoring rather than a mistake to guard against: the wait ends on the
      turn bound first, so a document whose quiet bound is the larger has said
      *do not notice silence*.

      ⚠ ZERO IS THAT SAME DECLINING, spelled the way `turn_within_ms` spells it —
      `Quiet::of` refuses a zero bound, so *call my peer silent the instant I stop
      looking* is not a reading in play, which leaves exactly one.
    -->
    <data id="quiet_within_ms" expr="600000"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ HOW MANY TIMES IN A ROW A CHECK MAY REFUSE BEFORE THE RUN REFLECTS
      — register item 449, and the number that stops a refusal being a wall.

      A refusal buys ONE MORE TURN (`judging`'s refusing edge, and item 428's
      whole point), and until this existed it bought them without limit while
      preempting every other way out. **Measured on run 16: nine consecutive
      refusals across seventeen iterations, each one 1129 identical bytes, and
      the run left the state only because a person pressed Escape.**

      ⚠⚠⚠ THREE, AND NOT ONE. One would end the buying that item 428 exists to
      do; two is an agent that fixed it on the second attempt, which happens. By
      the THIRD identical refusal there is no evidence in the run that anything
      is converging.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE NUMBER WAS CHOSEN WHILE THE REFUSALS WERE MUTE. When this was
      written the agent was not told it had been refused at all (item 448,
      `disputing`), so three was generous rather than hasty — three turns spent
      answering a question nobody had asked. Now that each refusal arrives with
      the check's own words, an agent that fixes it on the second attempt is the
      LIKELY case rather than the hopeful one, and three refusals is three
      INFORMED attempts. The number is unchanged and its argument is not: a kind
      that finds it slack now has a fact it did not have then.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS THE KIND'S TO AUTHOR, like `max_turns` and `reflect_every`: how
      patient to be with a checker is a judgement about the work, not about the
      loop. ⚠ A kind that sets it to 0 or 1 gets a reflection on the first
      refusal, which is a choice this document allows and does not recommend.
    -->
    <data id="reflect_after_refusals" expr="3"/>

    <!-- ── below the line: loop bookkeeping, not authored ── -->
    <data id="turns" expr="0"/>
    <data id="turns_since_reflect" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      WHICH TURN THE MILESTONE WAS SET ON, so a briefing can say how old the
      text it is about to type really is. Register item 592.

      MEASURED 2026-08-22. A run's brief carried
      `Milestone: 전수 라운드(pid 3988592, 현재 18/21 CAUGHT ...)` and
      `What to carry: the fix is done, do not build it again`. The pid was
      gone; `ps` found no such process. The run went 55 turns and committed
      nothing, because the agent was told to continue something that did not
      exist and told not to rebuild it.

      THE DEFECT IS NOT THAT THE pid DIED. It is that the milestone HOLDS a
      snapshot and is then re-typed VERBATIM at every re-priming, so a number
      true when it was written is presented as true now, for ever. This
      document cannot check somebody else's pid and must not pretend to. What
      it can say, and what nothing said before, is HOW OLD the claim is - and
      the reader who can act on that is the agent being briefed.

      IT IS A TURN NUMBER AND NOT A CLOCK, deliberately. This machine's own
      unit of progress is a turn; a wall-clock age would be a second authority
      on how far a run has got, and the number a reader compares it against
      (`turns`) is right here.

      SET WHEREVER `milestone` IS, and both sites carry the assignment beside
      the one they already had: `brief` at the start and `reflect.applied` when
      a reflection moves the checkpoint. A third writer of `milestone` that
      forgot this would leave the age reading as somebody else's milestone, and
      a gate in `ai_loop.rs` counts the pair.
    -->
    <data id="milestone_at" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      THE CLAUSE `priming` COMPOSES OUT OF IT, empty while the milestone is
      fresh. Held as a variable rather than inlined so the composition above
      stays one expression per prompt - this document's own rule about the
      three prompts being read side by side.
    -->
    <data id="milestone_age" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠ CONSECUTIVE refusals, cleared when the run reflects — see
      `reflect_after_refusals` above and `reflecting`'s own `onentry`.
    -->
    <data id="refusals" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE INNER SESSION HAS BEEN CHARGED TO READ, as of its last billed
      request. Written by the driver on every `turn.done` and by nothing else.

      This is the number the two budgets above are NOT. Measured across forty
      local agent sessions: cache read is 99.0% of tokens and 78.1% of cost,
      while the part a prompt's size resembles is 10.3% of cost — and it is the
      part that FALLS as a session grows. A turn is not a unit of it either: one
      request adds 861 tokens at the median and 633,749 at the maximum.

      ⚠⚠ ZERO MEANS "DO NOT DECIDE ON THIS", not "nothing has accumulated". A run
      whose session cannot be reached — an agent that states no transcript and
      that sprag did not name either, a host that cannot read a process, an agent
      that has not written yet — reads 0, and the two are indistinguishable here
      on purpose: the alternative is refusing to drive a loop over a number that
      is only ever an optimisation.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ ONE CAUSE OF THAT ZERO WAS A DEFECT AND HAS BEEN PAID (register item
      431). The driver used to resolve the record from the name the pane was
      LAUNCHED with, and an agent may file under another one: measured
      2026-08-17, a pane born as session `97f5ffd9` reported `3f4ffa52` while it
      worked, no `97f5ffd9` record exists anywhere, and the `3f4ffa52` record held
      466,013 tokens of cache read on its last request. This variable was 0 and
      `reflecting` composed a paragraph of arithmetic out of it. The agent states
      where it writes on its own submit hook, and that is read first now.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THREE GUARDS READ IT, AND THIS PARAGRAPH HAS NOW SAID *none* AND
      *two* IN ITS TIME — register item 437's class, met twice in this one entry,
      which is why the count is spelled out rather than implied. `reviewing` asks
      whether there is room to take the next milestone HERE and `judging` asks
      whether to hand over AT ALL because the window is filling, both of which are
      CAPACITY (register item 424(b)); `reviewing` also asks whether replacing
      this session has already paid for itself, which is the ECONOMIC one this
      entry spent two rounds refusing (register item 424(a)).

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE REFUSAL AND WHAT ENDED IT, KEPT IN THAT ORDER BECAUSE THE REASON
      MATTERS MORE THAN THE VERDICT. This entry said the deciding figure was
      unmeasured; it arrived, and at the re-measured rate the eight-turn cadence
      discarded 18,736 tokens — INSIDE the 15,000-21,000 band. So the entry
      concluded THERE MUST BE NO GUARD, because *"a threshold placed anywhere in a
      band the workload sits inside decides by rounding, and would read as a
      measured policy while being a coin toss"*. **That was right about a band and
      wrong about this workload**, and the entry named its own release: *"a guard
      remains writable the day a workload sits clearly outside the band, and what
      would justify one is a RE-MEASUREMENT, not a preference."*

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE RE-MEASUREMENT SAYS OUTSIDE, BY A FACTOR OF THIRTY-ONE. Over 214
      local agent sessions of 20 billed requests or more, the discardable part
      grows 72,212 tokens per TURN at the median where the settled conclusion was
      computed at 2,342 — and of those 214 sessions, 211 are ABOVE the band after
      five turns and NOT ONE is inside it, at five turns or at eight. A guard is
      therefore not a coin toss here, and `reviewing` carries it: see the edge
      itself for the break-even it is written on and for what that trade does not
      price.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT SURVIVES THE CHANGE IS THE DIVISION, NOT THE REFUSAL. `reflecting`
      still composes all three numbers into the prompt — what a restart could
      discard and what one costs — and the agent being asked what to do next is
      still the party that weighs its own cadence. What moved is only the case
      where the arithmetic has already answered: the driver measures, the document
      holds the trade, and a judgement about somebody's work belongs to whoever is
      doing it. ⚠ The CAPACITY question is not carried in that sense and cannot
      be: an agent asked to weigh a full window has already spent the window it
      would be weighing.
    -->
    <data id="context" expr="0"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ HOW MUCH THIS SESSION MAY HAVE READ BEFORE THE NEXT MILESTONE IS
      TAKEN SOMEWHERE ELSE — the number `reviewing` decides on, and the reason
      the decision is no longer a count of turns.

      `context`'s own entry above spent two rounds refusing a guard, and the
      argument was about a different question: whether a restart is worth its
      cold start, which is economics, and a threshold placed inside the band the
      workload sits in decides by rounding. **This is not that question**, and it
      never depended on the answer to it: this one is CAPACITY — a session's
      reading is bounded, and a bound is not a coin toss, because the argument
      against a threshold in the MIDDLE of a band says nothing about one near the
      end. ⚠ That entry's refusal has since been ended by a re-measurement, so
      `reviewing` now carries an economic guard as well; this ceiling was correct
      before it and is unchanged by it.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE WORKLOAD IS OUTSIDE THE BAND THAT REASONING WAS COMPUTED
      FOR, which is exactly the condition it named as making a guard writable
      (*"what would justify one is a re-measurement, not a preference"*).
      Measured on a live loop, 2026-08-18: ONE turn reached `context - floor` of
      **255,809** tokens across 293 billed requests, where the eight-turn figure
      the conclusion rests on is **18,736**. A count of turns cannot bound that —
      the same measurement puts one request's growth between 861 and 633,749.

      ⚠⚠ ZERO MEANS NO CEILING WAS AUTHORED, and the loop then behaves exactly
      as it did before this existed: every reflection replaces the session. A
      caller who has not thought about capacity is not given a number somebody
      guessed for them.

      ⚠ It is the KIND's to author, like `max_turns` and `reflect_every` — what
      a session may spend depends on the work, and this template decides nothing
      for the next repository.
    -->
    <data id="context_ceiling" expr="0"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE TWO NUMBERS THAT MAKE `context` LEGIBLE, written by the driver on
      the same `turn.done` and by nothing else. Alone, `context` says how big the
      session has become and nothing about what could be done with that.

      `cold` is WHAT A RESTART RE-PAYS: the cache this session had to write on
      its first billed request, before there was anything to read back. It is a
      toll and it is charged again on every replacement.

      `floor` is WHAT A RESTART CANNOT ESCAPE: the cache read of the SECOND
      request, which is the standing cost of a session — the system prompt and
      the tool definitions, about 38,500 tokens where this was measured. The
      first request cannot show it, having nothing to read back yet.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THAT NUMBER BELONGS TO A POPULATION, WHICH THIS PARAGRAPH DID NOT
      SAY. The 38,500 is a PLAIN agent session. Measured 2026-08-20 over every
      one of THIS repository's own transcripts that carries the two billed
      requests these numbers are defined on — 250 of the 255 on disk — the same
      quantity is a median of 21,350 and the toll `cold` is 28,981, four times
      the 7,000 the gates' fixture used to carry. Neither number is wrong; they
      are different tool sets, and a reader who took either for the other would
      mis-price the trade by a factor of four.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT THAT DOES TO THE BREAK-EVEN, stated because it is the whole point
      of the pair: at those measured values a restart pays for itself only past
      `20 * 28,981 + 21,350` = 600,970 tokens of reading, and 49 of the 250
      sessions ever got there. The economic edge is therefore a MINORITY door in
      production and the fixture made it look ordinary — registered rather than
      left for somebody to infer.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE FIRST FILING OF THAT FINDING WAS ITSELF A SAMPLE OFFERED AS A
      POPULATION: it read 25 sessions of the 255 and reported 24,106 / 29,083
      and *4 of 25*, which moves the break-even by about 5,000 and the rate by a
      fifth. The numbers above are all of it. ⚠⚠⚠⚠ A MEASUREMENT IS A MOMENT, so
      the date is part of the claim, and the round that re-measures must move
      this paragraph AND `MEASURED_HERE` in `testing.rs` together — the ratchet
      `an_economic_edge_carries_the_population_it_was_measured_in` reds on either
      one alone, and it recomputes this arithmetic against the multiplier the
      guards below actually use rather than trusting the sum written here.

      ⚠⚠ SO THE DISCARDABLE AMOUNT IS `context - floor`, and the trade is that
      against `cold` at twenty to one. `reflecting` composes all of it into the
      prompt AND `reviewing` decides on it: the edge that replaces a session with
      room left is written on exactly this comparison (register item 424(a)).
      ⚠ This paragraph used to end *"nothing here guards on any of it"*, which was
      true for as long as the workload was inside the band and stopped being true
      when it was re-measured outside — see `context`.

      ⚠ THEY DEGRADE TO 0 TOGETHER with `context` and for the same cause, so
      *"do not decide on this"* stays one rule rather than three.
    -->
    <data id="cold" expr="0"/>
    <data id="floor" expr="0"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠ HOW MANY TIMES THIS RUN HAS ALREADY REPLACED ITS SESSION. Counted where
      the replacement happens, so it is the act rather than an intention.

      It is in the reflection prompt because a toll paid once reads differently
      from the same toll paid eight times: `cold` says what one restart costs and
      this says how many have been bought. Without it the agent weighing its own
      cadence can price a restart but not the policy that produces them.
    -->
    <data id="restarts" expr="0"/>
    <!-- ⚠⚠ How many calls a standing instruction TURNED DOWN. Counted so an
         auto-screened run does not read like a hand-answered one.

         ⚠ Incremented on `screen.matched` and NOT on entry to `screening`,
         which is where it used to sit and where it counted the wrong thing:
         entering that state means a dialog was LOOKED at, and this variable's
         name promises the ones a rule actually acted on. A loop whose rules
         claim nothing would have counted every question it declined to
         answer. -->
    <data id="screened" expr="0"/>
    <!-- ⚠⚠ How many dialogs a JUDGEMENT turned down, kept apart from `screened`
         for the reason that variable is kept apart from a hand-answered run: two
         different authorities acted, and a reader of a finished run must be able
         to tell which. A quote the author wrote is auditable by re-reading the
         document; a judgement is not, so its count is the only trace it leaves. -->
    <data id="redirected" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE STANDING INSTRUCTIONS THIS RUN HAS CARRIED OUT, one labelled line
      each, accumulated on `screen.matched` and composed into both working
      prompts by `priming`.

      It is BOOKKEEPING and not authored: the words are the author's (the rule's
      own `text`, behind the author's own `standing_label`), and what this
      variable holds is which of them have actually happened. A person editing
      this file has nothing to say here.

      ⚠ The label is repeated per line rather than written once above the list,
      and that is deliberate: making it a heading would need this composition to
      know whether the list is EMPTY, and a conditional in executable content is
      a construct this document has never been measured with. A self-describing
      line costs a few words and needs no `<if>`.

      ⚠⚠ IT HAS NO CEILING, and that is stated rather than capped. Each DISTINCT
      instruction adds one line to every prompt the loop sends for the rest of
      the run (repeats are dropped — the driver normalises at every reflection),
      so a long run whose agent asks about many different things pays for all of
      them on every turn. What the right bound would be is a question about
      somebody else's work — the oldest instruction is not obviously the least
      important — so nothing here guesses at one.
    -->
    <data id="standing" expr="''"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE REVIEW OF THE RUN'S OWN CLOSED SESSIONS HANDED BACK — one
      line, or empty.

      It is `standing`'s SIBLING and not `standing` itself, because the two are
      different kinds of instruction and merging them would make one of them a
      lie. A standing instruction is a thing a person or a judge decided, and
      `standing_label` says it *overrides the milestone wherever the two
      conflict*. This is an observation about how the last few sessions SPENT
      THEMSELVES — «one log was tailed eighty-four times» — which overrides
      nothing and is nobody's ruling.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT GOES INTO `start_prompt` ONLY, and that is the point of WHEN the
      review runs. The review happens between the reflection and the restart, so
      the party it has something to tell is the session that does not exist yet —
      one that remembers none of the habit and is about to repeat it. In
      `turn_prompt` the same line would be re-sent on every turn of a session
      that has already read it: a cost `standing` pays deliberately and this has
      no reason to.

      ⚠ It holds its own line terminator and is EMPTY until a review carries
      something, so a loop whose reviews find nothing composes exactly the text
      it always did — `standing`'s arrangement, for `standing`'s reason: no
      conditional in executable content.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS OVERWRITTEN, NEVER APPENDED. Each review speaks about the sessions
      it has just read, so an older reading is not evidence about the current
      one — and a list that only grew would carry a broken habit into every
      prompt of a run that had already stopped doing it.
    -->
    <data id="carried" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ How many screened calls the loop has already ADOPTED — the value
      `screened` had at the last reflection.

      `judging` compares the two, so a standing instruction that fired is
      reflected on at the very next judgement rather than waiting out
      `reflect_every`. Without it the loop asks for the original milestone again
      on the turn straight after being told not to, which is the defect above.
    -->
    <data id="screened_carried" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS REFLECTION WAS ASKED FOR, written by whichever `judge`
      transition reached `reflecting`.

      Three roads lead there and they are NOT the same question:

        milestone     the agent said the checkpoint is reached, so the only
                      thing left to settle is what the NEXT one is
        instruction   a standing instruction fired that the prompts do not
                      carry yet
        budget        `reflect_every` came round

      Registered as owed before it existed (*"a run cannot say WHY its session
      was replaced … a person whose agent's session was just replaced has no way
      to tell because you told it something from because eight turns went by"*),
      and what forced it is that the three need DIFFERENT endings. A reflection
      asked because the milestone is done, which then names no next milestone,
      must END THE RUN: sending it back to `working` would ask an agent to reach
      a checkpoint it has just reached, for ever. The other two go back to work,
      because the work is not finished.

      ⚠ It is read by the DRIVER, in Rust, and never by a `cond` here: `==` over
      this datamodel is unverified (see `judged_rules`), and a guard that
      compared strings would be the first place to find that out.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT REACHES A PERSON NOW, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT WAS MISSING. The
      paragraph above says the three need different ENDINGS and that was all this
      variable ever bought: the driver read it to decide one of them, and a reader
      of the run got the same six words whichever had fired. It is now also the
      journal's word for the edge into `reflecting` — see the driver's
      `ReflectReason`, whose arms are these three and no others, held against this
      list by a gate that reads this file.
    -->
    <data id="reflect_reason" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH CEILING ENDED THE RUN, written by whichever `judge` transition
      reached `stopping` — `reflect_reason`'s twin, one state over, and for the
      same finding.

      Two transitions reach `stopping` and FOUR facts arrive on them: this
      document's own `turns >= max_turns`, and the run's `iterations`, `cost` and
      `duration`, which all come in through the one outside door
      (`_event.data.stop_short`, whose value IS the driver's word for the ceiling
      that bound). Before this existed neither reader could tell them apart:

        the AGENT   was told, in `stop_prompt`, that it had run out of turns —
                    false for three of the four, and typed into its pane
                    (register item 264)
        a READER    saw one `judge` arrow from `judging` to `stopping` and
                    nothing else, whichever ceiling had fallen. The only
                    thing separating the two doors in a run's journal was
                    whether a `note_to_itself` line PRECEDED the edge — telling
                    them apart BY THE ABSENCE OF A KEY, which is the reading this
                    workspace has burned wire numbers over (register item 265)

      ⚠ It holds the DRIVER's word (`Ceiling::wire_str`) and not a word of this
      document's own, which is the one difference from `reflect_reason`: the fact
      is the run's, three quarters of the time. `turns` is spelled here because
      that ceiling is this document's and no driver can see it.

      ⚠ Read by the driver, in Rust, and never by a `cond` here — `==` over this
      datamodel is unverified (see `judged_rules`). `stop_said` above is indexed
      by it, which is a lookup rather than a comparison.
    -->
    <data id="stop_reason" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH OF THE TWO ENDINGS CLOSED THE RUN — `reflect_reason`'s and
      `stop_reason`'s third, one state further on, and the same finding again.

      ONE transition reaches `closing` and TWO different runs take it:

        declared       the agent said `north_star_marker` — the whole job is
                       finished, and it says so
        no_successor   the milestone was reached and this reflection named no
                       next checkpoint, so there was nothing left to ask THIS
                       agent for. **Nobody said the work was done.**

      Both end `converged`, so before this existed a run that reported its own
      completion and a run that merely ran out of things to propose were the
      same three words in a journal — and they want opposite things from a
      reader. The first is an account to weigh against what was asked; the
      second is a checkpoint to look at and a north star to decide about.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE WORD IS THE DRIVER'S, LIKE `stop_reason`'s AND UNLIKE
      `reflect_reason`'s, and here it is the driver's for BOTH of them. Neither
      fact is visible from this document: one is a marker on somebody's pane,
      the other is that marker's absence together with a reflection reason. So
      the value arrives as `_event.data.done_reason`, whose vocabulary is the
      driver's `DoneReason::word`, and this file spells no ending of its own.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE TRANSITION IS GUARDED ON IT, which is the difference between
      this and its two elders. There the causes are EDGES, so a cause that
      forgot to say why is a missing `<assign>` and a gate over this file finds
      it. Here they are `return`s in Rust, and no reader of this document can
      count them — so the guard is what holds the direction instead.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE GUARD ACTUALLY BUYS, MEASURED BOTH WAYS by raising this event
      with no word (the first draft of this comment claimed something else and
      the mutation said otherwise):

        guarded     the edge does not fire. The walk writes a SELF-ARROW —
                    `reflecting` to `reflecting` on `reflect.done` — which no
                    correct run ever produces, and the pass falls through to
                    whichever ending the next look decides, so the run ends
                    saying the wrong one. Wrong, and visibly wrong.
        unguarded   the edge fires, `done_reason` is assigned `nil`, and the
                    walk writes the ordinary `reflecting` to `closing` line
                    with no clause on it: the shipped defect exactly, and
                    indistinguishable from a correct run.

      So the guard does NOT stop the run, which is what the first draft said it
      would. What it does is make a wordless raise unable to REACH this state,
      and leave a shape in the journal that cannot otherwise happen. That is the
      reading register items 264, 265 and 269 were all about, kept on the side
      where an absence is loud instead of silent.

      ⚠ Truthiness over Lua, exactly as `_event.data.stop_short` is: the only
      false values are `nil` and `false`, so an ending whose word was EMPTY
      would pass the guard and then read back as nothing. No `DoneReason` can
      spell itself `''` and a gate holds that rather than this comment.

      ⚠ Read by the driver, in Rust, and never by a `cond` here — `==` over this
      datamodel is unverified (see `judged_rules`).
    -->
    <data id="done_reason" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS SESSION WAS REPLACED — `reflect_reason`'s fourth, one state
      further on again, and the same finding a fourth time.

      SIX transitions reach `restarting` (three per review ending) and THREE
      different decisions take them:

        economics       there was room to spare and replacing has already paid
                        for itself — what this session could discard has passed
                        twenty times what its replacement re-writes. Nothing is
                        wrong; this is the trade working
        capacity        the session has read past `context_ceiling`, so there
                        was no room to take the next milestone here. The number
                        to change, if these are too frequent, is the CEILING
        nobody_could_say
                        the fall-back. Either no ceiling is authored (`0`, a
                        caller who has not said) or the session's reading could
                        not be taken at all (`context` of `0`) — so the two
                        questions above could not be asked, and replacing is
                        what this loop has always done when it cannot see

      Before this existed all three wrote the one `reviewing` to `restarting`
      arrow byte for byte, and they disagree about the remedy: one says change the
      ceiling, one says nothing is wrong, and one says go and find out why the
      record is unreadable. A reader watching handovers get more frequent could
      not tell which they were looking at. Register item 445.

      ⚠⚠ THE FALL-BACK SAYS SO IN A WORD RATHER THAN IN SILENCE. An edge that
      assigned nothing would leave the PREVIOUS replacement's word standing —
      naming the wrong cause, confidently — and *nobody could say* is a fact a
      reader can act on, where an absent clause is one they have to guess at.
      That is register item 423's whole lesson, arriving before the fact.

      ⚠ WHAT THAT ONE WORD STILL MERGES, stated rather than hidden: an
      unauthored ceiling and an unreadable record are both *nobody could say*,
      and their remedies differ — author a ceiling, or find out why the file
      cannot be read. The run's own walk already says the second out loud when
      it happens (register item 431(a)), which is why this is registered here
      rather than split into a fourth word nothing else distinguishes.

      ⚠ The words are THIS document's, like `reflect_reason`'s and unlike
      `stop_reason`'s: all three facts are visible from here, and the driver
      transcribes them into `RestartReason`. A gate reads this file and holds
      the two lists together.

      ⚠ Read by the driver, in Rust, and never by a `cond` here — `==` over this
      datamodel is unverified (see `judged_rules`).
    -->
    <data id="restart_reason" expr="''"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ HOW MANY SESSIONS HAVE BEEN REPLACED FOR REFUSING THE QUESTION SINCE
      ONE LAST TOOK IT — the bound on register item 446's recovery, and the only
      `cond` in this document that reads a number this file itself keeps.

      ZERO means the last question asked of this run was TAKEN, so a peer that
      now will not take one is a session-level condition a replacement fixes —
      measured: the wedged draft could not be submitted by Enter, by `Ctrl-C` or
      by an interrupt, while ordinary typed text in the same pane submitted at
      once, and a fresh session took the identical prompt.

      ONE means the session opened to fix exactly that will not take a question
      either. Nothing another restart does reaches that, so the run says
      `failed` and a person looks. ⚠ It is reset by `prompt.sent` — a question
      that landed is what makes the next refusal a NEW fact rather than the same
      one continuing.
    -->
    <data id="unasked_since_taken" expr="0"/>

    <!--
      WHICH ERROR OF ITS OWN THIS DOCUMENT STOPPED ON, and empty for the runs
      that raised none — which is every healthy run. Register item 505.

      W3C SCXML 3.12.2 puts an `error.*` on the internal queue and IGNORES it
      when nothing matches, and W3C 3.8 abandons the REST OF THE BLOCK that
      raised it. Between them, a document that answers no error runs on with
      half of an `onentry` executed and NOTHING ANYWHERE SAYS SO. Measured on
      this file: one <send> naming a type nobody serves, in `priming`, and a
      real run walked `Priming` to `Working` and then eleven `Working` to
      `Working` on nothing at all — going nowhere, silently, with every other
      gate in this crate green.

      So the region answers its own errors now (see the foot of `work`), and
      this is what the answer WRITES DOWN. The value is the event's own name,
      because the class is the repair: `error.execution` is this document's
      content failing and a person edits the file; `error.communication` is a
      <send> the host did not serve and a person edits the host.

      Read by the driver, in Rust, like `restart_reason` and `reflect_reason`
      beside it, and never by a `cond` here.
    -->
    <data id="fault" expr="''"/>

  </datamodel>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠ TWO THINGS GO ON AT ONCE IN A RUN: THE WORK, AND WHAT A PERSON HAS TOLD
    IT.

    Until this existed, the only thing anybody could say to a running loop was
    `cancel` — *stop now, mid-turn, and throw the turn away*. **The sentence a
    person actually wants is a different one**: *finish what you are doing and
    then stand down.* It could not be said, and the reason was structural rather
    than missing vocabulary: a flat machine has nowhere to PUT an instruction
    that arrives while something else is going on. It would have to be a
    transition repeated on all fourteen working states, which this workspace has
    already paid for once as *two copies of one rule, and the failure of letting
    them drift is silent*.

    So the run's states became one REGION and the person's orders became
    another, and they run side by side.

    ⚠⚠⚠ AN ORDER IS A STATE, NOT A FLAG — measured, and this is the whole reason
    `In()` was probed before any of this was written. A boolean set by an order
    is invisible in the run's walk: nothing records when it arrived or what the
    run was doing at the time. Held as a state it is in the journal by
    construction, which is this workspace's rule for machines everywhere else.
    `probe_parallel.scxml` asked whether one region can be guarded on what
    another is doing, and the answer at the pinned engine is yes.

    ⚠⚠ THE FINALS STAY OUTSIDE, deliberately. A run that ends is a run whose
    orders are over too, and a transition targeting a state outside the parallel
    exits every region — so reaching `converged` takes the orders region with it,
    which is exactly right. Putting the finals inside would ask a different
    question (does the PARALLEL complete) that nothing here needs to answer.

    ⚠ THAT LAST SENTENCE USED TO END *"the driver learns a run ended from the
    state it is in, by an exhaustive match, not from `is_in_final_state`"*, and
    since register item 470's stage 3 the opposite is true: the exhaustive match
    is gone and `is_in_final_state` is what answers. It answers CORRECTLY only
    because of the arrangement this comment is about — a final outside the
    parallel exits every region — so the two facts belong together.

    ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE DRIVER MUST STOP FLATTENING. With two regions there are two
    active leaves, and `get_current_state` — and any reader that just takes the
    deepest — answers whichever the arrangement happens to yield.
    `OuterLoop::state` therefore reads THIS region by name. The probe holds both
    halves: that a named region answers with its own leaf, and that it keeps
    answering after its sibling has finished.
  -->
  <parallel id="running">

  <state id="work" initial="idle">

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT A PASS OF THE DRIVER DOES, ANSWERED BY THIS REGION FOR WHEREVER
    IT IS — register item 470, stage 3, and the largest thing that item was
    filed about.

    The driver raises `pass` at the top of every pass and carries out the act
    this table answers with. Before it, `OuterLoop::pump` chose from a `match`
    over all TWENTY-EIGHT states of this document — *this one watches a turn,
    that one waits an outage out, that one asks a person* — which is a SECOND
    COPY OF THE TOPOLOGY, in Rust, keyed by the ids written here. There is now
    one copy, and it is this one, in the file the ids belong to.

    ⚠⚠⚠ TARGETLESS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS SHAPE WORKS. W3C SCXML
    3.13: a transition with no `target` runs its content and changes no state.
    A state ENTRY could not carry it — `working` is looked at many times over
    one entry, for as long as its peer keeps working — and a SELF transition
    would re-run every `<onentry>`, which here means re-typing at the peer the
    sentence the state was entered with, once per look.
    `probe_send_type.scxml`'s `edged` measures exactly this shape, re-entry
    counter and all, and its three shapes of NO were all made to fire.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT IS ON THE REGION RATHER THAN ON EACH STATE, WHICH WAS THE
    ENGINE'S ANSWER RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. Written one per child first, and
    `sce-build` refused the document: putting an event on EVERY child of `work`
    satisfies the "common ground" precondition of its handler-consistency
    check, and this compound then reports the twenty-seven event gaps it has
    always had — `brief` in `idle` alone, `judge` in `judging` alone, and so on
    — every one of them the deliberate protocol-stage shape that precondition
    exists to excuse. The note above `orders` predicted this exactly, for the
    other region, in 2026-08-21. `sce:unhandled` cannot pay it (some three
    hundred declarations), and a parent-level fallthrough per event would
    swallow events `OuterLoop::unseen` is a reader of. What the validator's own
    documentation names as the repair is a PARENT-LEVEL handler, and that is
    what this is.

    ⚠⚠ SO THE GUARD IS `In(...)` — the document asking where the document is,
    which is the one reader that cannot drift from the topology. First match in
    document order wins, and a state missing from this table is answered by
    nobody: the driver reports `Pumped::Unbuilt` and the run stops, which is
    what the deleted match's exhaustiveness used to buy.

    ⚠ THE WORDS ARE A CLOSED SPACE (`crate::act::Does`) and one outside it is
    REFUSED where this machine can hear it, on the `error.execution` edge below.

    ⚠ THE SEVEN FINALS ANSWER NOTHING AND ARE RIGHT TO: a finished machine is
    reported, not driven, and it is told apart by BEING final rather than by
    being named in a list out in Rust.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND EVERY ONE OF THE TWELVE CARRIES THE SAME THREE NUMBERS. `within`,
    `awaits` and `stills` are `ready_timeout_ms`, `await_person_ms` and
    `handback_still_ms`: the readiness barrier's own bound, how long a person is
    waited for at this pane, and how long their hand must be still before the
    pane is this run's again. The driver used to fetch all three out of the
    script session with private typed readers, which register item 470 named as
    going BEHIND THE MACHINE'S BACK: nothing in this file said the values were
    consulted at all, so no reading of the document could reveal that a wait
    this file authors is a wait this file's driver honours.

    ⚠⚠ AN ACT OF THEIR OWN WAS BUILT, MEASURED AND REJECTED. A `ready.set` on
    its own unguarded transition read beautifully and cost a SECOND MACROSTEP ON
    EVERY PASS, which put a stand-in timing gate red across two whole sweeps
    while the same sweep on the unchanged tree was clean three times running.
    This act is already raised once per pass, so the numbers ride it for
    nothing. Repetitive in the document, free at run time, which is the right
    way round.

    ⚠ ZERO IS A REAL ANSWER IN BOTH OF THE NEW ONES AND NOT AN ABSENCE:
    `await_person_ms` of zero says NOBODY IS WATCHING, and `handback_still_ms`
    of zero says a person who takes this pane KEEPS it. `probe_send_type.scxml`
    measured that a number crosses as a `<param>` with zero, nil and a negative
    all still tellable apart, which is what made this move sayable at all.
  -->
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('idle')">
    <!-- Nothing has happened yet. Starting is the caller's act, but the
         transition it causes DELIVERS the start prompt — so the peer has to be
         ready before that sentence can mean anything. -->
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'ready'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <!-- ⚠ TWO STATES, ONE WORD. Both were entered by a transition that delivered
       a sentence and neither waits for anything else, so *the prompt was sent*
       is the whole of what either has to report. In the driver these were two
       arms of the match; saying it once is what the move found. -->
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('priming') || In('disputing')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'sent'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <!-- ⚠ THREE STATES, ONE WORD, for a reason worth stating: what makes a
       closing turn a REPORT is the `asks` on the sentence that opened it, not
       which state is watching. -->
  <transition event="pass"
              cond="In('working') || In('closing') || In('stopping')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'watch'"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT AN OUTAGE LOOKS LIKE, DECLARED WHERE IT WOULD BE MET.

        The driver used to fetch `service_needle` out of the script session with
        a private reader, which register item 470 named as going BEHIND THE
        MACHINE'S BACK: nothing in this file said the value was consulted, so a
        reader of the document could not tell that a blocked turn is matched
        against it at all.

        ⚠⚠ IT RIDES THE `watch` PASS AND NO OTHER, because watching a turn is
        the only pass that can MEET an outage. A needle on `judge` or `attend`
        would be this document saying something nothing reads.

        ⚠⚠⚠ AND ONE PASS IS STILL ENOUGH NOW THAT TWO EVENTS READ IT — register
        item 715. `turn.blocked` and `peer.silent` are both endings of the SAME
        watch, decided inside it, so the words they are matched against arrive on
        the pass that produced both rather than on two.

        ⚠ The template ships an EMPTY LIST, which declines the whole behaviour;
        an adopting repository quotes the words its own peer prints.
      -->
      <param name="needles" expr="service_needles"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('judging')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'judge'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('screening')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'screen'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('service_down')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'wait'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <!-- ⚠ NOT `watch`: a reflection is not judged, does not spend `max_turns`,
       cannot converge a run, and its answer is read back into the brief of the
       session that replaces this one. `reflecting`'s own `asks='direction'`
       makes the same distinction one layer over. -->
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('reflecting')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'reflect'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('reviewing')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'review'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('restarting')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'replace'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('resuming')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'resume'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('awaiting_human')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'attend'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="pass" cond="In('redirecting')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="pass.do">
      <param name="within" expr="ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <param name="awaits" expr="await_person_ms"/>
      <param name="stills" expr="handback_still_ms"/>
      <param name="answers" expr="may_answer"/>
      <param name="does" expr="'redirect'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ CAN THIS STATE'S AGENT BE ASKED WHERE THE RUN GOT TO?

    `AiLoop::ask_for_an_account` answered that with a `match` over all twenty
    eight states of this file: eight granting a window and twenty naming a
    reason not to. It was the last of the driver's copies of this topology.

    ⚠⚠ A TARGETLESS TRANSITION FOR `pass`'s REASON, not for tidiness. One of the
    RUN's ceilings falls due at a moment nobody can predict, and the state it
    finds may have been entered long before — so a state ENTRY could only answer
    once, at a time that has nothing to do with the asking.

    ⚠⚠⚠ EACH WORD IS A FACT ABOUT THE PANE and not a ranking. Nobody was ever
    asked anything; somebody else's hand is in it; the agent that did the work is
    being replaced; the service is not answering. A reader of the run's journal
    gets the reason, and `no account` alone would send them to the wrong thing.

    ⚠ THE WINDOW IS NOT DECLARED HERE. `within` says the agent CAN be asked; how
    long it gets is two of the CALLER's own turns, a number this document neither
    holds nor should. The word is the document's, the quantity is the driver's.

    ⚠ AND THE SEVEN FINALS ANSWER NOTHING, as they answer no `pass`: a finished
    machine is told apart by having PUBLISHED an ending, not by being named here.
  -->
  <transition event="account"
              cond="In('priming') || In('working') || In('judging')
                    || In('screening') || In('redirecting') || In('disputing')
                    || In('closing') || In('stopping')">
    <!-- A turn is in flight or one has just landed, so the latch reaches
         `judging` on the very next judgement. `closing` and `stopping` are in
         this list and are already asking their own question; the flag changes
         nothing for them, and leaving them out would be an exception nobody
         could state a reason for. -->
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="account.ask">
      <param name="can" expr="'within'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="account" cond="In('idle')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="account.ask">
      <param name="can" expr="'never_asked'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="account" cond="In('awaiting_human')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="account.ask">
      <param name="can" expr="'not_ours'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="account"
              cond="In('reflecting') || In('reviewing')
                    || In('restarting') || In('resuming')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="account.ask">
      <param name="can" expr="'between_sessions'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="account" cond="In('service_down')">
    <!-- ⚠ Its own word rather than folded in with `not_ours`: typing here is
         ALLOWED, nobody's hand is in the pane, and it would still buy nothing
         because the answer has to come back from the same service that just
         refused a turn. What a reader needs is the outage. -->
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="account.ask">
      <param name="can" expr="'service_down'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHICH OF THE RUN'S OWN READINGS BELONGS ON THE ARROW JUST DRAWN?

    `OuterLoop::pumping` chose that from a `match` over all twenty eight states:
    six naming a reading and twenty two written out to say `this arrow needs no
    word`. It was the last of the driver's copies of this topology.

    ⚠⚠ A `Because` EXISTS ONLY FOR DOORS AN ARROW CANNOT TELL APART. A state
    reached from several places, for reasons that disagree about the remedy,
    renders one arrow between them; a one doored state answers NOTHING and is
    right to, because the walk's own arrow (from state, event, to state) is
    already the whole story. So the states missing from this table are not an
    oversight, they are the answer.

    ⚠ THE READING ITSELF STAYS WITH THE DRIVER — a ceiling, a reflect reason, a
    done reason, a restart reason and a judgement are five different values it
    latched as it went. What is decided here is WHICH of them the arrow wants.
  -->
  <transition event="arrived" cond="In('reflecting')">
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="arrival.note">
      <param name="note" expr="'reflected'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="arrived" cond="In('stopping')">
    <!-- ⚠ FOUR CEILINGS ARRIVE ON TWO EDGES, and a reader could otherwise tell
         them apart only by whether the driver's own note PRECEDED the arrow,
         which is reading the ABSENCE of a key. -->
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="arrival.note">
      <param name="note" expr="'stopped'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="arrived" cond="In('closing')">
    <!-- ⚠ ONE TRANSITION AND TWO RUNS: this state's doors are not transitions
         at all but two returns in the driver's own `reflect`, which is why
         counting this file's edges would never have found it. -->
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="arrival.note">
      <param name="note" expr="'closed'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="arrived" cond="In('restarting')">
    <!-- ⚠ ELEVEN DOORS AND THREE DECISIONS: replacing has paid for itself,
         there was no room, and neither question could be asked. They disagree
         about the remedy and render one arrow between them. -->
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="arrival.note">
      <param name="note" expr="'restarted'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>
  <transition event="arrived" cond="In('disputing')">
    <!-- ⚠⚠ THE JUDGEMENT'S OWN READING, carried by ARRIVAL rather than by the
         edge, and it needs no guard: exactly one transition reaches this state
         and it comes from `judging`. The state next door cannot say the same,
         which is why `working` declares this word on its edges instead. -->
    <send type="x-sprag-host" event="arrival.note">
      <param name="note" expr="'judged'"/>
    </send>
  </transition>

  <state id="idle">
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE TEMPLATE, FILLED IN BY SOMEBODY WHO DID NOT EDIT THIS FILE.

      A person authoring a loop edits the `<data>` above. A GUI — or any caller
      that starts a run — has no file to edit, and until this transition existed
      it had no other way either: the composed prompts were built at
      `initialize()`, so writing a part afterwards left them stale, and the only
      prompt a caller could actually send was the `(edit me)` template.

      TARGETLESS ON PURPOSE. W3C SCXML 3.13: a transition with no `target`
      executes its content and changes no state, so a brief does not exit and
      re-enter `idle` — there is nothing to re-run here, because the composition
      lives in `priming` and every path to a prompt goes through it.

      ⚠ ONLY BEFORE THE RUN STARTS, and that is the whole reason it sits on
      `idle` rather than on the machine. A loop already driving an agent adopts
      new parts through `reflecting` — which writes them, restarts the session,
      and passes back through `priming` — because changing what a run is for
      underneath a working agent is a session replacement, not an assignment.
    -->
    <transition event="brief">
      <assign location="north_star" expr="_event.data.north_star"/>
      <assign location="milestone" expr="_event.data.milestone"/>
      <!-- WHEN IT WAS SET - register item 592, beside the assignment it dates. -->
      <assign location="milestone_at" expr="turns"/>
      <assign location="milestone_age" expr="''"/>
      <assign location="reference" expr="_event.data.reference"/>
      <assign location="max_turns" expr="_event.data.max_turns"/>
      <assign location="reflect_every" expr="_event.data.reflect_every"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE CEILING `reviewing` DECIDES ON — register item 492, and it
        is the line that makes this document's own sentence true. `context_ceiling`
        says *"it is the KIND's to author, like `max_turns` and `reflect_every`"*,
        and until this assignment existed there was no road for a kind, a brief or
        a caller to author it: the number was 0 on every run anybody has ever
        driven. **A decision no channel carries is a decision nobody made**, and
        item 477 measured the price at the far end — eight of eight `reviewing`
        exits taking the fall-back, which is that state never once deciding.

        ⚠⚠ UNCONDITIONAL, like its two neighbours: the driver echoes this
        document's own number back when nobody named one, so a caller who says
        nothing cannot delete an author's ceiling.
      -->
      <assign location="context_ceiling" expr="_event.data.context_ceiling"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE PATIENCE `judging` SPENDS ON A REFUSING CHECK — register item
        494, which is the line above's twin and was found by sweeping the CLASS
        rather than the instance. This document says «it is the KIND's to author»
        about exactly two numbers; item 492 built the road for one of them and the
        other stood here unreachable, its only writer a gate reaching straight into
        this datamodel by a door no caller had.

        ⚠⚠ UNCONDITIONAL, like its three neighbours, and the reason is sharper for
        this one: a nil assignment would delete a kind's PATIENCE and the run would
        then reflect on the first refusal, which this document allows and does not
        recommend.
      -->
      <assign location="reflect_after_refusals" expr="_event.data.reflect_after_refusals"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠ THE STANDING INSTRUCTIONS TOO, and this assignment is UNCONDITIONAL, which is why the
        driver always sends them: a caller that says nothing about screening has the document's own
        rules read out and echoed back here. Omitting the key would assign nil and delete an
        author's rules for every caller that did not happen to care about them.
      -->
      <assign location="screen_rules" expr="_event.data.screen_rules"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ WHO IS EXPECTED AT THIS PANE, ON THE SAME UNCONDITIONAL TERMS AS THE
        RULES ABOVE and for the same reason: the driver always sends both keys,
        echoing this document's own numbers back when the caller named none, so
        omitting one cannot assign nil and delete an author's patience.

        ⚠⚠ AND THE BARRIER IS HANDED THEM ON EVERY PASS rather than holding what
        it was built with: the `pass.do` act above carries both as `awaits` and
        `stills`, so a value written here is in force before the next wait. A
        barrier that kept its construction copy would wait out the AUTHOR's hour
        for a caller who asked for a minute, which is how this crate's own suite
        hung 59 tests in before the numbers reached the barrier at act time.
      -->
      <assign location="await_person_ms" expr="_event.data.await_person_ms"/>
      <assign location="handback_still_ms" expr="_event.data.handback_still_ms"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ AND HOW LONG A HOLD MAY LAST, ON THE SAME UNCONDITIONAL TERMS — the
        third number about the same person, and the one that is NOT half of the
        pair above: `Handback` lives inside `Attended::APerson`, and a hold does
        not, because a run nobody is watching can still be held. See the
        `hold_within_ms` entry in the datamodel for why it is its own key rather
        than the patience reused.

        ⚠⚠ THE DRIVER RE-READS IT WHEN THE HOLD IS ACTUALLY BEING WAITED OUT —
        `OuterLoop::hold_within`, at the top of the held arm — so a number
        written here is in force before any hold, and no copy taken at
        construction can drift from it.
      -->
      <assign location="hold_within_ms" expr="_event.data.hold_within_ms"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE RUN'S TWO OTHER DURATIONS, ON THE SAME UNCONDITIONAL TERMS.
        They are the last two judgements that lived on `AiLoopSpec`: how long to
        wait for the pane to be ready, and how long to let one turn run. Their
        PREDICATE halves — `ready_when` and `done_when` — stay run arguments,
        because those are read off which program is in the pane; these two are
        read off nobody and are therefore this document's (register item 300).

        ⚠⚠ THE DRIVER RE-READS BOTH WHEN IT ACTS — `OuterLoop::patience` and the
        barrier's `waiting`, at the top of every pass — so a number written here
        is in force before any wait, and no copy taken at construction can drift
        from it.
      -->
      <assign location="ready_timeout_ms" expr="_event.data.ready_timeout_ms"/>
      <assign location="turn_within_ms" expr="_event.data.turn_within_ms"/>
      <!--
        ⚠ THE CONSENTS TOO, on the same unconditional terms and for the same
        reason: the driver always sends the key, echoing this document's own
        clauses back when the caller named none, so omitting it cannot assign nil
        and delete an author's approvals.
      -->
      <assign location="may_answer" expr="_event.data.may_answer"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE TWO A KIND DECIDES, WHICH REACHED THIS DOOR AND FOUND IT
      SHUT — register items 428 and 441.

      Both are decisions this file deliberately leaves EMPTY for a repository to
      make: what certifies a milestone, and what a repository adds to the
      question every ending is asked. A kind document authored both, the driver
      read both, the brief payload carried both — and neither was assigned here,
      so a live run judged `NOTHING CHECKED THAT CLAIM` while the kind document
      driving it named a checker. Measured 2026-08-18, on the run, not read off
      a file. **A decision no channel carries to the end is a decision nobody
      made**, and the missing link was one line in this transition.

      ⚠⚠ UNCONDITIONAL, on the same terms as every assignment above it: the
      driver always sends both keys, echoing this document's own empty values
      back for a kind that names none, so omitting one cannot assign nil and
      delete an author's decision.
    -->
      <assign location="milestone_check" expr="_event.data.milestone_check"/>
      <assign location="closing_rules" expr="_event.data.closing_rules"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE THREE THE PEER'S BAD DAY NEEDS, on the same unconditional
        terms: the driver always sends all three, echoing this document's own
        values back for a kind that names none, so a kind that declines cannot
        assign nil over a number the driver later divides time by.

        ⚠⚠ ALL THREE OR NONE IS A KIND'S BUSINESS AND NOT ENFORCED HERE, because
        two of them already have working defaults. A kind that writes only the
        needle gets ten minutes and the word `continue`, which is the whole
        behaviour; one that writes only a wait has still declined, because an
        empty needle matches nothing.
      -->
      <assign location="service_needles" expr="_event.data.service_needles"/>
      <assign location="service_retry_ms" expr="_event.data.service_retry_ms"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE ONE ASSIGNMENT HERE THAT IS GUARDED, AND THE GUARD IS A
        DECISION THAT CAME IN FROM THE DRIVER — register item 470, stage 3.
        `Owed::fallback` used to stand this file's own `continue` in when the
        word arrived empty, and it was the LAST thing the driver's `Owed` table
        did besides naming variables. The reason it existed has not changed:
        `service_down`'s only way back to work is something being SAID, so an
        empty word is a run that waits for ever, which is precisely the
        `blocked` that state exists to prevent.

        ⚠⚠ WHAT MOVED IS WHERE IT IS DECIDED. Standing a default in at DELIVERY
        meant the driver held a second copy of this file's default; keeping the
        authored value HERE means there is one copy, in the `<datamodel>` above,
        and nothing downstream has to know a fallback exists at all.

        ⚠⚠⚠ IT COMPARES AGAINST THE EMPTY STRING RATHER THAN TESTING TRUTH,
        because this datamodel is evaluated by LUA and the empty string is TRUE
        there — a bare `cond` would take this branch for exactly the value it is
        here to refuse. `disputing` pays for the same fact and says so.

        ⚠ EVERY OTHER ASSIGNMENT ABOVE IS UNCONDITIONAL and must stay so: the
        driver always sends every key, echoing this file's own values back when
        a caller named none, so a guard elsewhere would be answering a question
        nobody asks.
      -->
      <if cond="_event.data.service_retry_text != ''">
        <assign location="service_retry_text" expr="_event.data.service_retry_text"/>
      </if>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE CLOSING QUESTION IS COMPOSED AGAIN, because `end_prompt` was
        composed in the `<datamodel>` — before any brief existed — so assigning
        the clause alone changes a variable nothing reads again. The sentence is
        the SAME one the datamodel holds; it is repeated here rather than moved
        because `closing` must have a prompt even for a run that is never
        briefed, and a prompt composed only here would leave that run silent.
      -->
      <assign location="end_prompt"
              expr="'Summarise what changed, what was verified, and what is left open.' + closing_rules"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="start" target="priming"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ A LOOP CAN BE UNSTARTABLE BEFORE IT EVER PROMPTS, and until this edge
      existed `idle` was the one non-final state with no way out but forward.
      What made it concrete: a brief whose text the script engine could not
      carry is already assigned by the time anybody can tell, so the run must
      not be startable — and with no `fail` here the driver's refusal could be
      walked straight past by pumping.
    -->
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    A session exists and has not been prompted yet. Entered at the very
    beginning and again after every restart, which is what makes a restarted
    loop resume with the CURRENT prompts rather than the ones it started with.
  -->
  <state id="priming">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE COMPOSITION, WRITTEN ONCE AND RUN ON EVERY PATH TO A PROMPT.

        `priming` is entered from `idle` at the start, from `restarting` after
        every session replacement, and from `reviewing` when a reflection has
        changed the parts and the session was worth keeping. Those are the only
        three ways this loop ever comes to send a prompt, so composing here is
        composing exactly as often as the parts can have changed — a brief
        before the run, a reflection during it — with one copy of the text.

        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE THIRD DOOR IS REGISTER ITEM 523, AND THIS PARAGRAPH IS WHAT
        HID IT. It read *the only two ways* while `reviewing` held a door of its
        own straight back to `working`, so a reflection during the run moved
        `milestone` in the datamodel and left `turn_prompt` exactly as this
        block had composed it before any work was done: judged against the new
        checkpoint, briefed on the old one, for ever. **The sentence defending
        the arrangement was the evidence against it**, which is why the door was
        moved here rather than the prompts being composed a second time.

        ⚠ ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, as it was in `<data>` before: `done_instruction`
        is composed FIRST because the two working prompts end with it. Executable
        content runs in document order, so moving it below them would append the
        PREVIOUS entry's instruction — silently, and correctly for every entry
        but the first.

        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE PROMPTS COMPOSE `done_marker` RATHER THAN REPEATING IT. R379
        measured what the alternative costs. The marker existed, `judging` waited
        for it, the driver looked for it on the screen — and NO PROMPT EVER ASKED
        THE AGENT FOR IT. Nothing in the loop was wrong except that the one word
        the whole termination rests on was never said to the only party who could
        say it back. A run against a real agent could not converge; it could only
        spend all `max_turns` and report `exhausted`, which is the word for *the
        budget ran out* and not for *nobody ever asked*. It went unnoticed for
        eleven rounds because the stand-in agent in the driver's gate was written
        to say the marker, which is a fixture manufacturing the answer it tests
        for.

        Composing means an author who edits the marker edits the instruction with
        it. Retyping the word into each prompt would be three places to keep in
        step, and the failure of doing that badly is silent.

        How the agent is told to end a turn it believes finished the milestone is
        prose a person may want in their own language, which is why it is here
        and not in the driver — the driver must not be the thing that appends
        instructions to an authored prompt (that is a second author).
      -->
      <assign location="done_instruction"
              expr="'When the milestone is fully reached AND verified, make the last line of your ' +
                    'reply exactly: ' + done_marker"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ `standing` IS COMPOSED INTO BOTH WORKING PROMPTS, and it is what makes
        a standing instruction outlive the turn it was given in. It holds its own
        line terminators and is EMPTY until a rule has fired, so a loop that has
        never been screened composes exactly the text it always did — which is
        why no conditional is needed here.

        ⚠ It goes into `start_prompt` too, and that is the half that matters most:
        `start_prompt` is what a FRESH session is greeted with after a restart,
        and a fresh agent remembers nothing of having been redirected. A loop
        that carried its instructions only in `turn_prompt` would hand its
        replacement a clean slate and the original milestone.
      -->
      <!--
        HOW OLD THE MILESTONE IS, RE-READ HERE - register item 592.

        `priming` is entered at the start AND again on `session.ready`, which is
        what a replacement session is briefed through. So this subtraction is
        computed at the moment somebody is told the text, against a counter that
        has been running the whole time: it is a live read, not a snapshot, and
        that is the whole of the fix. A run 55 turns in now says so.

        THE EMPTY STRING FOR A FRESH MILESTONE IS THE POINT, not a saving. At
        turn 0, and on the turn a reflection moves the checkpoint, the age is
        zero and there is nothing to warn anybody about - a clause that said
        `(set 0 turns ago)` on every first briefing would be noise on the
        common path, and noise is what gets skimmed past on the rare one.
      -->
      <assign location="milestone_age"
              expr="turns - milestone_at &gt; 0
                    ? ' (set ' + (turns - milestone_at) + ' turns ago; check it still describes live facts)'
                    : ''"/>
      <assign location="start_prompt"
              expr="'North star: ' + north_star + '\n' +
                    'Milestone: ' + milestone + milestone_age + '\n' +
                    'What to carry: ' + reference + '\n' +
                    standing +
                    carried +
                    'Report what you did and what is left.' + '\n' +
                    done_instruction"/>
      <assign location="turn_prompt"
              expr="'Continue toward: ' + milestone + '\n' +
                    standing +
                    'Do the next smallest thing that is verifiable, then report.' + '\n' +
                    done_instruction"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE REFLECTION'S OWN PROMPT, composed here for the reason the other
        three are: it names `north_star`, `milestone` and `standing`, and a
        reflection that asked about a milestone the run had already moved past
        would collect an answer about the wrong work.

        ⚠⚠⚠ THE LABELS ARE NAMED MID-SENTENCE AND NEVER OPEN A LINE. The peer
        paints what it is given, so an instruction that began a line with
        `NEXT MILESTONE:` would put a row on the screen that the driver's own
        reader cannot tell from an answer. Written this way the echo carries the
        label with words in front of it, which is the shape the reader rejects.
        ⚠ It is a mitigation, not a proof — a terminal wraps where it likes —
        and the driver's second rule (*discount what this loop itself said*) is
        what actually decides. See `OuterLoop::proposed`.

        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE LAST CLAUSE BELOW CANNOT BE WRITTEN THAT WAY, which is why
        the driver has a rule of its own for it. `north_star_marker` has to be
        the END of what the agent is told to say — the instruction IS *"make the
        last line of your reply exactly this"* — so unlike the two labels it
        cannot be buried mid-sentence. This line is 152 characters with the
        marker at 134, so a pane 67 or 134 columns wide puts those three words
        alone on a row of a screen where nobody has answered anything. Register
        item 270, paid: `OuterLoop::said_marker` reads a logical LINE rather
        than a row, and discounts a line that is this sentence's own tail.
        ⚠ The same is true of `done_instruction` above, at 109 and 92.

        ⚠⚠ NO TOOL IS ASKED FOR, deliberately: a reflection is a question about
        work already done, and an agent that reaches for a tool here raises a
        dialog in a state that cannot screen one (see `reflecting`'s own
        comment). Asking for an answer from what it already knows is the cheapest
        way to keep the turn inside the state that owns it.

        ⚠⚠⚠ THE SECOND QUESTION ASKS FOR KNOWLEDGE, NOT FOR AN ADDRESS, AND A
        MEASUREMENT IS WHY. It used to ask *"what the next session should read
        first"*, and what came back was an ADDRESS — one live reflection named a
        file, three item numbers in it and a whole section. A pointer costs its
        reader the whole read. Measured across that replacement:

          the session that was replaced   reached its first change at 161,507
          the fresh one it briefed        reached its first change at 285,599

        The restart discarded 348,000 tokens of context and the replacement had
        bought 266,000 of it back within eighty requests. **A handover that names
        where to look hands over nothing; it hands over an errand.**

        So the question changed and the slot's own clause with it (`What to
        carry:`, above): a caller's first briefing may still be prior art, and
        everything a reflection puts there afterwards is what the last session had
        to work out.

        ⚠⚠ WHAT JUDGES IT IS NOT A UNIT TEST. This is authored prose, and prose
        cannot be gated by asserting the words back — that is a test agreeing with
        the renderer. What settles it is `Warmup` (`spend.rs`) on the NEXT
        replacement: **285,599 is the number to beat**, on comparable work. If it
        does not fall, the fault was not the wording and a distiller is owed.
      -->
      <assign location="reflect_prompt"
              expr="'North star: ' + north_star + '\n' +
                    'You have been working toward: ' + milestone + '\n' +
                    standing +
                    'What this session has cost, in the tokens the bill is actually denominated in: ' + context +
                    ' read on its last request, of which ' + floor + ' is a floor no restart escapes, leaving ' + (context - floor) +
                    ' that replacing this session would discard. Replacing it wrote ' + cold + ' of cache to begin with and would write that again, and this run has already bought ' + restarts +
                    ' replacements. Writing cache costs about twenty times reading it, so discarding has to beat that toll to be worth anything. A zero is a number that could not be read, not a small one.' + '\n' +
                    'Stop and decide what comes next, from what you have just done and what you now know. Answer from what you already have; do not use a tool.' + '\n' +
                    'Reply with exactly two lines and nothing else. Open the first with the label ' + milestone_marker +
                    ' and then the next checkpoint in one line. Open the second with the label ' + reference_marker +
                    ' and then, in one line, what the next session must KNOW that you had to work out — not what it should read.' + '\n' +
                    'If the north star itself is fully reached and nothing is left to do, ignore both labels and make the last line of your reply exactly: ' + north_star_marker"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THIS STATE ASKS THE HOST TO PUT ITS SENTENCE rather than
        announcing a name the machine drops. Register item 470, stage 2, and
        the same move `closing` and `stopping` made before it.

        What stood here was `<send event="prompt.start"/>`. NOTHING in this
        document transitions on that name, so the only thing it ever did was
        mint an event; what actually decided the sentence was a Rust table
        keyed by this state (`Owed::on`, arm `Priming`). The state now says
        both facts itself, and the driver reads them off the act.

        ⚠⚠ `asks='work'` AND NOT `'account'`, which is the whole reason the
        argument exists: this sentence opens the run's FIRST turn. One that
        asked for an account would take a report off a session that has not
        been given anything to do yet.

        ⚠ THE ORDER IS THIS BLOCK'S OWN RULE, stated at its top: a send's
        arguments are evaluated when the send executes (W3C SCXML 6.2.3), so
        what goes out is the `start_prompt` the assigns above just composed and
        never the template it was composed from.
      -->
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="start_prompt"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'work'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ A QUESTION THAT WAS TAKEN CLEARS THE ONE FREE REPLACEMENT BELOW. See
      `unasked_since_taken`: what that number bounds is a peer that will not
      take a question TWICE RUNNING, and a peer that took this one is not that.
    -->
    <transition event="prompt.sent" target="working">
      <assign location="unasked_since_taken" expr="0"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE PEER WOULD NOT TAKE THE QUESTION — REPLACE THE SESSION RATHER
      THAN END THE RUN. Register item 446, measured on the owner's own daemon.

      What happened: an unattended run paid two register items, committed and
      pushed both, replaced its session, primed the successor — and then died on
      the next prompt. The text reached the pane and was read back off it; the
      submit that followed never became a question, and the pane's own counter
      said so (`asked` did not move, for fifteen minutes). The driver refused to
      press again, correctly: a second submit onto a composer the first one
      emptied asks an empty one.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE REMEDY IS NOT A SECOND KEYSTROKE. Measured by hand on the
      wedged pane: Enter did not submit that draft, `Ctrl-C` did not clear it,
      and an interrupt left it standing — while ordinary typed text in the SAME
      pane submitted immediately (`asked` 2 → 3). So the pane works and the
      SESSION's draft cannot be sent. A fresh session takes the identical prompt
      (measured, twice, on a new pane).

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE EDGES THAT SAY SO USED TO BE HERE AND ARE NOW THE REGION'S, at
      the foot of `work` — because the condition was never `priming`'s. Every
      state that owes the peer a prompt can meet it, and five of the six had no
      edge at all: the event was raised, nothing took it, the machine stayed put,
      and the driver watched a pane for a turn nobody had been asked to take.
      This state's own behaviour is unchanged; what changed is that it is no
      longer the only state with it.
    -->
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!-- The inner agent is running. The driver watches its pane. -->
  <state id="working">
    <transition event="turn.done" target="judging">
      <assign location="turns" expr="turns + 1"/>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="turns_since_reflect + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE AGENT ASKED, AND THE FIRST QUESTION IS WHAT KIND OF QUESTION IT IS.

      A dialog that would commit a DESIGN DECISION is turned down and redirected;
      anything else goes to `screening`, where the author's quoted rules and then
      the person decide as they always have.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS IS A `cond` AND NOT A FLAG THE DRIVER READS. The routing IS
      the decision, so it belongs where a person editing this file can see it —
      two lines, in order, saying what happens to each kind. A driver that
      branched internally on the same fact would make this document say
      *"a blocked turn goes to screening"* while the product did something else.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT THE GUARD CANNOT DO, and why the driver still measures. Deciding
      whether a dialog commits a design decision needs a second agent reading the
      dialog's own contents — MEASURED, because the two dialogs it must separate
      say the identical sentence:

          Do you want to create PROBE.txt?    <- writes the word "ready"
          Do you want to create DESIGN.txt?   <- commits 256 lines that chose
                                                 JSON over SQLite

      No expression over this datamodel can tell those apart. And a guard must
      not try: SCXML does not promise how often it evaluates one, and the
      measured judgement takes 4-6 seconds. So the driver judges ONCE, when it
      raises the event, and publishes the verdict as `_event.data.design` — the
      same arrangement `judge`'s own `_event.data.done` has always used. The
      driver measures; the document decides.

      ⚠ A run with no judge configured, a judge that errs, and a judge that
      answers something which is not a verdict all arrive here as FALSE. Silence
      is not a yes: the alternative would refuse an agent's tool call on nobody's
      decision, which is the act `screen.rs` removed the `keys` field to prevent.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ FIRST, BECAUSE IT IS NOT A QUESTION AT ALL. The peer's SERVICE
      failed — see `service_needle` — and there is nobody to ask and nothing to
      decide. Above the design branch deliberately: a judge asked to read an
      error message is a model call spent to arrive at the wrong state, and a
      run whose judge is slow would pay 4-6 seconds of it per outage.

      ⚠ A run whose kind declines this (the shipped empty list) never sees the
      key set, so both branches below are reached exactly as they were.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT SAYS THE PEER IS *NOT* COMING BACK ON ITS OWN, which is what
      makes the way out of `service_down` speak. A turn that is OVER left an empty
      composer and a service that has to be asked again; see
      `service_resumes_itself` for the door that means the opposite, and note that
      the assignment is UNCONDITIONAL rather than only on the true side — a run
      that meets a limit and then a 529 would otherwise carry the limit's silence
      into an outage that needs a word.
    -->
    <transition event="turn.blocked" cond="_event.data.service" target="service_down">
      <assign location="service_resumes_itself" expr="false"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" cond="_event.data.judged" target="redirecting"/>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="screening"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠ ROUTING A SINGLE DECISION SOMEWHERE OF ITS OWN, if a document ever wants
      to. The driver publishes the matched rule's `name` as `_event.data.rule`
      beside the boolean, so a fork per decision is one more line above this one:

          <transition event="turn.blocked"
                      cond="_event.data.rule == 'security'"
                      target="halting"/>

      ⚠⚠⚠ NOT SHIPPED, AND NOT BECAUSE NOBODY WANTED IT. `==` over this datamodel
      is UNVERIFIED — the header's rule is that a shape not driven by a gate is
      not known to work here, and `===` is already recorded as one nobody has
      run. Add it to `ai_loop.rs`'s datamodel gate before writing it into a
      document somebody depends on.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE BOOLEAN IS PUBLISHED SEPARATELY, AND THE REASON THIS USED TO GIVE
      WAS FALSE. It read: *"it is Lua, where the only false values are `nil` and
      `false`; an empty string is TRUE, so a guard reading the rule's name would
      fire on every blocked turn of a run that matched nothing."* That is Lua's
      rule and NOT this engine's — measured, and the engine's own source says so
      in one line:

          _scxml_truthy(val): nil/false -> false, 0/0.0/"" -> false, else true

      Every `cond` in every document here is wrapped in that call by the code
      generator, so an empty string is FALSE and the failure that paragraph
      predicted cannot happen.

      ⚠⚠ THE SHAPE STAYS, WITH ITS REAL REASON. A name and a verdict are two
      facts — *nothing matched* and *nobody judged* are different answers, and a
      document that wants to route on WHICH rule fired needs the name whatever
      the truthiness rules are. What changed is that this is no longer defended
      by a fact about the engine that was never driven by a gate.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THAT IS THE LESSON, not the correction: the paragraph above this
      one says *"a shape not driven by a gate is not known to work here — add it
      to `ai_loop.rs`'s datamodel gate before writing it into a document"*, and
      the sentence four lines below it made an engine claim with no gate at all.
      `a_guard_reads_ecmascript_truth_and_not_lua_truth` is now that gate.
    -->

    <!--
      The person reached into the inner pane and interrupted the agent
      themselves (Escape, Ctrl-C, or just typing). They are plainly here and
      doing something, so the loop stops driving and waits — screening would be
      answering a question the person is already handling.
    -->
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!-- A watching person can halt the loop between turns. -->
    <transition event="hold" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ NOTHING HAS SPOKEN FOR THE PANE FOR THE WHOLE OF `quiet_within_ms`.
      See that `<data>` for what was measured; this is where the decision about
      it lives.

      ⚠⚠⚠ TO `awaiting_human` AND NOT TO `peer_gone`, WHICH IS THE WHOLE
      DISTINCTION. A gone process cannot come back and its state is final. A
      silent one is a peer that may speak on its very next tool call — the thing
      that stopped speaking may be its REPORTER, which a rebuild replaces under a
      running daemon, and no reading of a pane can separate the two. So this
      names the one remedy that settles either: a person. `turn.done` above is
      still open to a turn that returns, so being wrong here costs a run nothing
      but the notice.

      ⚠⚠ AND NOT `hold`, THOUGH IT LANDS IN THE SAME STATE. `hold` is a person
      SAYING to stop; this is nobody saying anything. Reusing the word would make
      a run that fell silent unattended read, forever afterwards, as one somebody
      paused on purpose — and the two want opposite things from whoever finds
      them.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND FIRST, THE ONE SILENCE THAT IS NOT ONE — register item 715.
      A peer that PRINTED WHY IT IS QUIET has not fallen silent; it has said it is
      waiting and, in this peer's case, that it will carry on by itself. The
      paragraph above is right that no reading of a pane separates a frozen agent
      from a replaced reporter — and it is wrong that this is always that
      question, because a pane carrying the peer's own account of its silence
      answers it. `service_needles` is what that account looks like, matched by
      the driver on the same watch that produced this event; the state below is
      where *nobody is expected and nothing is wrong* already lives.

      ⚠⚠ THE CONTROL THIS MUST NOT SWALLOW, said here because it is the whole
      risk of the edge: a peer that is genuinely silent says NOTHING, so it
      matches no needle and takes the edge below exactly as before. A repair that
      let real silence reach `service_down` would trade a visible ending for an
      invisible wait, and the gate holding this holds that half too.
    -->
    <transition event="peer.silent" cond="_event.data.service" target="service_down">
      <assign location="service_resumes_itself" expr="true"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="peer.silent" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE AGENT'S PROCESS IS GONE. THIS IS THE STATE THE 43 HOURS WERE SPENT
      IN, and until this line the document had no word for it: the turn contract
      this loop runs on (`settles`) asks a supervisor whether the agent came back
      to rest, and a process that has left is reported by NOBODY — so a dead
      agent and a thinking one were the same picture and the loop waited out its
      whole half-hour bound, every pass, saying nothing was wrong.
    -->
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    A dialog is up. Does a standing instruction cover it?

    A state rather than something the driver swallows, because a rule that fires
    is a thing that happened to the run: it consumed a turn, it steered the
    agent, and whoever reads the trace afterwards needs to see it. A screening
    step hidden inside the driver would make an auto-answered run and a
    hand-answered one look identical.
  -->
  <state id="screening">
    <!--
      A rule claimed it. The driver refused the call and told the agent what to
      do instead, and the agent is working on that — which is why this edge
      declares NO `prompt.say`: a prompt here would type over a peer that has
      just been spoken to. ⚠ It used to say *see `Owed::on`*, the driver table
      that answered what an edge owed; that table is deleted, and an edge owes
      what it declares here and nothing else.
    -->
    <transition event="screen.matched" target="working">
      <assign location="screened" expr="screened + 1"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE INSTRUCTION IS REMEMBERED, not only said. The driver carries
        the rule's own `text` on this event; keeping it is what lets `priming`
        compose it into every later prompt once `reflecting` has adopted it. See
        `standing`.
      -->
      <assign location="standing"
              expr="standing + standing_label + _event.data.text + '\n'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      NO STANDING INSTRUCTION GOT THE RUN PAST THIS ONE. The person decides.

      ⚠⚠ SEVERAL DIFFERENT THINGS reach this edge and the driver's own report is
      what tells them apart, because a state cannot carry a reason: no rule
      claimed the dialog (`no_rule`); one did and the refusing key did not take
      it off the screen (`not_dismissed`); one did, the key went in, and the RUN
      ENDED inside the wait, so nobody watched (`unwitnessed`). NOTHING WAS
      TYPED in any of them, and that is what this edge is for — a dialog still
      up reads an Enter as an answer to itself, which is measured, so the run
      stops rather than saying its piece.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE SENTENCE ABOVE USED TO SAY *TWO*, which stopped being true the
      round `Refused::StillUp` grew its second refusal and did not move with it.
      What this edge MEANS is *the question is still up and this run has nothing
      left to do about it*; WHICH refusals satisfy that is `Unanswered`'s
      business and `Refusal::ALL` is the only list of them. A count written here
      is a count nothing keeps in step.

      ⚠⚠ AND *THE DRIVER'S OWN REPORT* IS THE JOURNAL LINE, which is where that
      first sentence only became true. The step that takes this edge answers
      `continue` — `awaiting_human` is not final — so nothing structural on it
      carried the reason, and a run cancelled at the refusing key never reached a
      step that did. `AiLoop::walked` now writes the refusal the pass ARRIVED at
      onto the line itself.
    -->
    <transition event="screen.none" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE DIALOG WENT AWAY BEFORE THE RULE COULD ACT, so nothing was pressed and the turn
      simply carries on.

      The question reaches `screening` from the reading the BARRIER took on the pump before, and in
      the gap a person can answer it or the agent can withdraw it. Without this edge the driver's
      only choices were to press into whatever is there now — Escape into a composer discards what
      a person was in the middle of typing — or to end the run over a dialog that resolved itself.
      Neither is what happened, and a machine that cannot say *nothing happened* forces its driver
      to pick a lie.

      ⚠ It carries no prompt and does NOT count as screened, for the same reason: the turn was
      never interrupted, the peer is still working on the prompt it already has, and this loop's
      `Completion` is still armed from it.
    -->
    <transition event="screen.moot" target="working"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ THE PEER'S SERVICE IS DOWN AND THE RUN IS WAITING IT OUT.

    Nothing is sent on entry, which is the difference between this state and
    every other one that leads back to `working`. The peer is not stuck and it is
    not asking: the thing it depends on failed, and the only act that helps is to
    let time pass. `service_retry_ms` is how much.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT IS NOT `awaiting_human`, WHICH IS THE DECISION THIS STATE IS.
    Both are *the run is not working right now*, and they send their reader to
    opposite places: `awaiting_human` says **a person is expected**, and ends at
    `blocked` when the run was told nobody is watching. This says **nobody is
    expected and nothing is wrong with the run** — so an unattended run, which is
    every run this loop was built for, survives it. Filing an outage under the
    first is what cost a live run 28 minutes on 2026-08-19.

    ⚠⚠ THE SESSION IS NOT REPLACED AND NOTHING IS RE-BRIEFED. The agent still
    holds its brief, its turn and its open files; what the wait buys is exactly
    the chance to carry on with all of it. That is why the way out types
    `service_retry_text` rather than the milestone — see `restarting` for the
    case where starting over IS the answer, and note that this is not it.

    ⚠ THREE WAYS OUT AND THREE WAYS TO DIE. `service.retry` is the driver saying
    the wait is over; where it LANDS is this document's decision and it is the
    three transitions below. The other three are the endings every state here
    carries. There is no edge for *the service came back on its own* where the
    turn ENDED, because a run that says nothing learns nothing: such a peer only
    tells us it is healthy by answering, and it can only answer something asked.
    ⚠⚠ THAT SENTENCE IS ABOUT AN ENDED TURN AND NOT ABOUT EVERY ARRIVAL —
    register item 715 found the case it did not cover: a peer that never stopped
    its turn resumes on its own and says so, and the third edge below is that
    reading. `service_resumes_itself` is which of the two this is.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE DRIVER TYPES NOTHING ON THE WAY OUT — `service_retry_text` is
    OWED by the edge that reaches `working` AFTER AN ENDED TURN, on the same terms
    as every prompt in this document, so the edge that reaches `awaiting_human`
    owes nothing and says nothing, and neither does the one for a peer that
    resumes itself. That ordering is not tidiness: it used to type first and route
    afterwards, which put a word into the composer of a service that had just
    refused and then handed a person a pane with somebody else's text in it —
    against this document's own rule that `awaiting_human` leaves the pane
    exactly as the person will find it.
  -->
  <state id="service_down">
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE SERVICE NEVER CAME BACK. FIRST, because the guard is what makes
      the edge below it bounded, and an unguarded first match would take every
      retry for ever.

      TO `awaiting_human` AND NOT TO `failed`: nothing about this run is broken.
      Its session is intact, its work is where it left it, and the one thing
      nobody here can answer is whether the peer's service is coming back — which
      is a judgement, and this document has exactly one state for a judgement it
      cannot make. A person who comes finds a pane that still holds the work; one
      who does not, reaches `blocked` through `unattended`, on the caller's own
      `await_person_ms` rather than on a clock invented for outages.

      ⚠⚠ AND THE WALK NAMES IT WITHOUT A WORD OF ITS OWN, because these are the
      only edges from `service_down` to `awaiting_human`: a reader meeting that
      pair in the morning knows a server was down and nobody came, where the same
      state reached from `screening` by `screen.none` says a question really
      needed a person. Telling those two apart is the whole of what a morning
      reader wants, and it was one bucket until now.

      ⚠ The walk's own arrow notation is deliberately NOT written out here:
      `sce-build` refuses an XML comment containing a double hyphen, and reports
      it against line 0 of the whole file (register item 341).

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THERE ARE TWO OF THEM, ONE PER DOOR — register item 724. Which
      door the outage came in at is already decided and already written down
      (`service_resumes_itself`), and the two want budgets that differ by a factor
      of six: see `service_resumes_max`, where the argument lives. The split is
      here rather than in a single guard reading a chosen number because a reader
      asking «how long will this run wait» must find TWO answers and be told
      which is which, not one expression that is quietly both.

      ⚠⚠ THE WALK STILL TELLS THEM APART WITHOUT A WORD OF ITS OWN, because the
      door is upstream of here: an outage that reached this state by `PeerSilent`
      and then a person is a limit that outlasted six hours, and one that reached
      it by `TurnBlocked` is a server that refused for an hour. The morning reader
      gets the distinction from the pair it arrived by, which is where item 715
      put it.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND BOTH COUNT THIS OUTAGE, NOT THE RUN — register item 729, which is
      what makes the two budgets independent rather than merely different. They
      read `service_retried - service_retried_carried`; against the bare counter a
      run that had just survived a five-hour usage limit would arrive at its next
      529 already twenty waits deep and be sent to a person without one retry,
      reporting that a person is needed about a service that had hiccuped once.
      **Two ceilings drawing on one budget is not two ceilings**, and that is the
      difference this subtraction buys.
    -->
    <transition event="service.retry"
                cond="service_resumes_itself &amp;&amp; service_resumes_max != 'never' &amp;&amp; service_retried - service_retried_carried &gt;= service_resumes_max"
                target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="service.retry"
                cond="service_resumes_itself == false &amp;&amp; service_retry_max != 'never' &amp;&amp; service_retried - service_retried_carried &gt;= service_retry_max"
                target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE WAY BACK THAT SAYS NOTHING — register item 715, and the
      only edge in this document whose whole content is a decision NOT to speak.

      The peer that arrived here through `peer.silent` printed that it is
      continuing automatically, and printed beside it that a keystroke CANCELS
      that. So the word that `service_retry_text` exists to say would, typed
      here, destroy the recovery this state waits for. It is not that the word is
      unnecessary; it is that it is harmful, which is a different claim and the
      reason this is an edge rather than an empty string somebody could author.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT ENDS THE OUTAGE THEN, since the block above this state says a run
      that says nothing learns nothing: the peer's own next report. That sentence
      was written when every arrival here was a turn that had ENDED, and for those
      it still holds — a service that refused a turn tells us it is healthy only
      by answering something. A peer mid-turn is the case it did not have: it goes
      back to `working`, the turn contract is still armed from the sentence that
      opened it, and either the peer speaks (`turn.done`) or it is still quiet and
      this state is reached again. **The budget is what bounds that**, which is
      the edge above, and it is why the clock on the screen is not parsed.

      ⚠⚠ THE COUNTER MOVES HERE TOO. A reader asking in the morning why a run
      spent the night in one state must see the same number whichever door the
      outage came in at, and an edge that skipped the `<assign>` would look
      identical to this one from anywhere except the walk.
    -->
    <transition event="service.retry" cond="service_resumes_itself" target="working">
      <assign location="service_retried" expr="service_retried + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="service.retry" target="working">
      <assign location="service_retried" expr="service_retried + 1"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE LAST THING THE DRIVER'S `Owed` TABLE ANSWERED FOR — register
        item 470, and this send is what retires the table rather than shrinking
        it. Every other act moved because it was a PROMPT; this one is not, and
        the block above this state says so: what it buys is *carry on with the
        turn you already have*, into a session nothing has re-briefed.

        ⚠⚠⚠⚠ `asks='work'` IS THEREFORE ARGUED RATHER THAN COPIED FROM ITS
        NEIGHBOURS. `asks` says what the sentence puts to the peer ASKS IT FOR,
        not how long the sentence is: `account` would file the resumed turn as
        this run's closing report, and `direction` would claim the loop is
        asking where to go next when it is asking for nothing of the kind. What
        `continue` asks for is the next piece of the job, which is `work`'s own
        definition. ⚠ The residue, stated: `asks` does not distinguish a
        composed question from a one-word nudge, and nothing reads it for that
        — the only reader is the judgement that collects an account, and
        neither of these is one.

        ⚠⚠ IT IS ON THE EDGE THAT REACHES `working` AND NOT ON THE STATE,
        because `service.retry` has TWO destinations since this file gained a
        ceiling and only one of them is spoken to. The edge above reaches
        `awaiting_human` and says nothing, which is this state's own rule: a
        person finds the pane exactly as they will find it.
      -->
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="service_retry_text"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'work'"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    The run is paused and a person is expected. Two ways in: the agent raised a
    dialog it cannot pass without an answer, or somebody watching asked the loop
    to stop and let them look.

    Nothing is sent from here. The pane stays exactly as the person found it,
    which is what makes it readable — a loop that kept prompting while somebody
    was reading would move the screen out from under them.
  -->
  <state id="awaiting_human">
    <!-- The person answered (or typed a turn themselves) and it completed. -->
    <transition event="turn.done" target="judging">
      <assign location="turns" expr="turns + 1"/>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="turns_since_reflect + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      The person interrupted again while the loop was already waiting — they are
      working in the pane by hand. Stay put; do not start prompting underneath
      somebody who is typing.
    -->
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!--
      The person looked and waved it on without touching anything.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE ACT IS DECLARED ON THE EDGE, which is register item 470 stage
      2's LAST prompt and the one no state entry could have carried. What is
      owed here is a property of the TRANSITION and not of where it lands: four
      edges reach `working` carrying this sentence and three others reach it
      carrying nothing, so `working` itself cannot say what a run arriving there
      is owed. `probe.rs` asked the engine whether an edge may declare an act at
      all before any of this was written; see that gate.
    -->
    <transition event="resume" target="working">
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="turn_prompt"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'work'"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
    <!--
      Nobody came within the driver's patience. This is the run-ending one.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ UNLESS SOMEBODY IS HOLDING IT, which is what the guard says and
      register item 470's first stage is. `unattended` means *a person was
      expected here and did not come*; said about a run a person paused ON
      PURPOSE it sends whoever reads it looking for somebody standing right
      there. The order lives in the `orders` region as `held`, so this is one
      question about the run's own walk rather than a flag in the driver.

      ⚠⚠ NEGATED RATHER THAN NAMING WHAT IS PERMITTED. The set this excludes is
      one order; the set it permits is the one that grows, and `orders` is
      documented as the place the next order goes. A guard naming the permitted
      states would have to be revisited every time one is added, and the way it
      would fail is by silently refusing an ending nobody was excluding.
      `not In()` at this engine is measured, not assumed: see
      `probe::tests::a_cross_region_guard_can_be_negated_and_can_name_a_compound_ancestor`.
    -->
    <transition event="unattended" cond="!In('held')" target="blocked"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ WAITING FOR A PERSON IS STILL WAITING ON A PANE, and the run has one
      fewer reason to go on holding it once the agent underneath has left: there
      is nothing for a person to come back TO. The ending is the peer's, not the
      person's, so it is not `unattended`.
    -->
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    One turn has landed. Decide in priority order: goal met, budget spent,
    time to improve ourselves, otherwise take another turn.
  -->
  <state id="judging">
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ ONE PLACE, AND IT IS THE ENTRY RATHER THAN THE EDGES. Every way into
      this state is a `turn.done` — from `working` and from `awaiting_human` —
      so `_event.data` here is that event's on both paths, and the assignment
      runs exactly once per judged turn.

      Writing it on the `judge` transitions instead would be FOUR copies of one
      line, which is what this document warns against wherever it composes
      rather than repeats: the failure of letting copies drift is silent, and
      here it would be silent in the worst way — the guard that eventually reads
      this would see a level from some earlier turn and be right about a session
      that no longer exists.
    -->
    <onentry>
      <assign location="context" expr="_event.data.context"/>
      <assign location="cold" expr="_event.data.cold"/>
      <assign location="floor" expr="_event.data.floor"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE OUTAGE, IF THERE WAS ONE, IS OVER — register item 729. A
        completed turn is the only proof this document ever gets that the peer's
        service came back, so this is where *waits in the current outage* goes
        back to zero. It is written as a watermark rather than a reset because
        the value it marks is a run TOTAL that a reader still needs whole; see
        both `<data>` blocks for why one value could not be both.

        ⚠⚠ IT BELONGS HERE FOR THE REASON THE BLOCK ABOVE ARGUES FOR ITS
        NEIGHBOURS, and gets the same benefit: every way in is a `turn.done`, so
        this runs exactly once per judged turn, where the two `turn.done` edges
        would be two copies of one line waiting to drift.

        ⚠ IT IS UNCONDITIONAL, and that is cheaper than being clever: a turn that
        followed no outage carries `service_retried` unchanged, so assigning it
        is a no-op. A guard here would be a second place that has to agree about
        whether an outage happened, for no gain.
      -->
      <assign location="service_retried_carried" expr="service_retried"/>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE RUN'S BUDGET IS SPENT ON A CEILING THIS DOCUMENT CANNOT SEE, so ask
      for the account and end — the SAME state `max_turns` reaches below, entered
      by the only door the outside has.

      `max_turns` is this document's own budget and the guard below reads it. The
      run is ALSO bounded by its host's guardrails — a step count, a spend, a wall
      clock — and none of those is expressible here: they count things the machine
      has no name for, and they are decided between steps rather than inside one.
      Before this edge a run stopped by one of them was simply never stepped
      again: measured, the loop was left standing in `working` or `judging` with
      its agent at rest, and the run answered `exhausted` and nothing at all —
      exactly the silence the guard below had just removed from the ending next
      door. **Three ways to run out, one of them explained.**

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS FIRST, ABOVE *the milestone was reached*, and that is the OPPOSITE
      order from `max_turns` below. The reason is who decided. `max_turns` is a
      number in this document, so an agent that reaches its milestone on the last
      turn has plainly done what it was asked and deserves the reflection. This
      one is a ceiling the run has ALREADY been stopped by, from outside, and
      nothing said in a courtesy turn can un-end it — sending such a run to
      `reflecting` would spend the window replacing a session for work that will
      never happen.

      ⚠⚠ IT DOES NOT COST A RUN THAT IS NOT STOPPING SHORT ANYTHING: the driver
      publishes `false` on every other judgement, and Lua's only false values are
      `nil` and `false`.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND WHEN IT IS NOT `false` IT IS THE CEILING'S OWN WORD, not merely
      `true`. Three of the run's ceilings come through this one door and they are
      three different facts about what to raise — a step count, a spend, a wall
      clock — so a boolean here left `stop_prompt` telling every one of them it
      had run out of TURNS (register item 264). The value is
      `Ceiling::wire_str`'s, a closed set of non-empty words, which is what keeps
      this guard a truth test: Lua's only false values are `nil` and `false`, so
      an EMPTY word would fire this edge on every judgement of every run. Nothing
      can publish one — there is no `Ceiling` that spells itself `''` — and a
      gate holds that rather than this comment.
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="_event.data.stop_short" target="stopping">
      <assign location="stop_reason" expr="_event.data.stop_short"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE MILESTONE IS REACHED, SO ASK WHAT THE NEXT ONE IS. This used to go
      straight to `closing`, and that made a run exactly as long as its first
      checkpoint: a live loop working through a list finished ONE item, said the
      marker, and converged after a single working turn — judged straight into
      `closing` and out — against a request to keep going until the whole list
      was done.

      A reflection is already the state that decides where the work goes next, so
      *this step is done* and *the budget came round* now arrive at the same
      place and differ only in what brought them. The run ends when the agent
      says the NORTH STAR is reached, which `reflecting` asks for and nothing
      else does.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT IT COSTS, STATED: reaching a milestone now spends a reflection turn
      before anything else happens, where it used to spend a closing one. A run
      whose LAST turn reaches its milestone therefore reflects, restarts, and
      then meets the budget guard below on the next judgement — one session
      replacement for nothing. Accepted rather than guarded around, because the
      alternative (testing the budget first) would report `exhausted` for a run
      that had plainly reached what it was asked for.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ SOMEBODY ASKED THIS RUN TO STAND DOWN, AND THE MILESTONE IS NOW DONE.
      The sentence that could not be said before the orders region existed:
      *finish what you are doing and then stop.*

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS ORDERED ABOVE THE MILESTONE EDGE BELOW, AND THE ORDER IS THE
      DECISION. Both guards are true at this moment — the agent said the word AND
      an order is standing — and document order is what picks between them.
      Below, the run would reflect and go looking for the next checkpoint, which
      is precisely what the person asked it not to do.

      ⚠⚠ IT WAITS FOR `_event.data.done`, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT A STAND-DOWN AND
      NOT A CANCEL. A cancel takes the run mid-turn and throws that turn away;
      this one fires only once the milestone the agent was working toward is
      finished, so the work is BANKED and `closing` still takes its account. The
      two are different endings and are reported as different words.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ `&amp;&amp;` AND NOT `and`, AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS EDGE WROTE `and`
      AND NEVER FIRED. This is the trap this file's own header warns about,
      sprung: a `cond` is PARSED as ECMAScript (the datamodel's declared name)
      and EVALUATED as Lua (the only engine there is). Lua's `and` is not
      ECMAScript, so the generator turned the whole guard into

          error("SCXML cond is not valid ECMAScript: ... unexpected token: 'and'")

      which the runtime evaluates to FALSE. The machine compiled, the run ran,
      and the edge was simply never taken — **no error reached anybody**. It was
      found by reading the generated Rust after a run that would not stand down.

      Written `&amp;&amp;`, the generator rewrites it to Lua and keeps the call:
      `(_scxml_truthy(_event.data.done) and _scxml_truthy(In("standing_down")))`.

      ⚠⚠ SO THE RULE FOR THIS FILE IS: WRITE ECMASCRIPT, NOT LUA. The header says
      the same thing about `===`, `JSON.stringify` and template literals, and
      this is the first time it has been paid for.

      ⚠ `In('standing_down')` READS THE OTHER REGION, and it works in a COMPOUND
      guard — measured here after a wrong first reading blamed `In()` for what
      `and` had done. `probe_parallel.scxml` asks the same question standalone.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE WORD IS A LITERAL HERE, unlike the other two endings. Those are
      facts only the driver can see — a marker on somebody's pane, and that
      marker's absence beside a reflection reason — so this file could not
      correctly spell either. A stand-down is this document's OWN fact: the order
      is a state in its own orders region. So it says so itself, and a gate holds
      this spelling against `DoneReason`'s.
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="_event.data.done &amp;&amp; In('standing_down')" target="closing">
      <assign location="done_reason" expr="'stood_down'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE CLAIM WAS CHECKED AND THE CHECK SAID NO — register item 428, and
      the only door in this document that can refuse a milestone.

      `_event.data.checked` is the word an INDEPENDENT process gave when it was
      shown the milestone and what the turn produced (`milestone_check`, in the
      datamodel above). `'failed'` is that process saying the checkpoint was not
      reached, and the run buys ONE MORE TURN — which is `unheard`'s direction
      further down, for the same reason: **a convergence nobody earned is the
      expensive mistake, and one more turn is the cheap one.**

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS BELOW THE STAND-DOWN AND ABOVE THE PLAIN `done`, and both halves
      of that placement are decisions:

        below   a stand-down is a PERSON saying stop. Refusing to stop because a
                check disagreed would keep a run going against an order, which is
                worse than banking an account whose milestone is arguable — and
                the account says what was finished in the agent's own words, so a
                reader can weigh it.
        above   otherwise this edge is unreachable: the plain `done` below fires
                first and the check would be a number nobody acts on, which is
                exactly the state item 428 describes.

      ⚠⚠ ONLY `'failed'` REFUSES. `false` is *nobody checked* — the shipped
      document, and the behaviour every run had before this existed — and
      `'silent'` is a checker that would not start or would not answer. Neither is
      a verdict against the agent, and treating either as one would stop every
      run whose checker is merely broken. What says so instead is the walk: the
      driver puts the difference in words on the edge itself, beside the reason
      the edge was taken rather than instead of it (`Pumped::Moved`'s `checked`).
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND A RUN THAT KEEPS BEING REFUSED HAS TO BE ABLE TO LEAVE —
      register item 449, watched live and paid for by a human keystroke.

      The edge below buys ONE MORE TURN per refusal, which is right. What it
      could not do was STOP buying: SCXML takes the first enabled transition, so
      while `checked == 'failed'` held, every escape further down this list —
      `max_turns`, the screened edge, capacity, and the reflection cadence — was
      **never evaluated at all**. Run 16 was refused on iterations 6, 8, 10 … 22
      — NINE times, at 1129 identical bytes each — and passed a
      `reflect_every` of 5 TWICE with nothing firing. It did not get out by
      itself; the owner pressed Escape in the pane. **A loop built to run
      unattended needed a person to leave a state its own document could not
      leave.**

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ SO THE CEILING SENDS IT TO `reflecting`, WHICH IS THE POINT rather
      than a convenient target: reflection is how this document replaces a
      session, and the owner's question was *"왜 다음 세션으로 안 넘어가고 같은
      세션에서 계속 반복되는거야?"*. Session replacement is reached THROUGH
      here, so **the one situation where a run most needs a fresh session was
      the one situation where it could not get one.**

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT DOES NOT END THE RUN, and that is the other half. A refusal buying
      one more turn is item 428 working; a ceiling that ended the run on the
      third would throw away the turn the check was asking for. This buys a
      REFLECTION — a fresh look, and possibly a fresh session — and the run
      carries on.

      ⚠⚠ ITS OWN COUNT AND NOT `turns_since_reflect`. Reusing the cadence would
      make one refusal spend the improvement budget, which is a different
      decision wearing the same number — the mistake `reflect_every` was split
      out of.

      ⚠ `refusals + 1`, because the increment belongs to the edge below and this
      one fires INSTEAD of it: the question here is *would this refusal be the
      Nth*, asked before the count has taken it.
    -->
    <transition event="judge"
                cond="_event.data.done &amp;&amp; _event.data.checked == 'failed' &amp;&amp; refusals + 1 &gt;= reflect_after_refusals"
                target="reflecting">
      <assign location="reflect_reason" expr="'refused'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT GOES SOMEWHERE OF ITS OWN RATHER THAN STRAIGHT BACK TO WORK —
      register item 448, and the reason is a fact about the DRIVER rather than a
      preference about layout.

      This edge used to carry `<send event="prompt.turn"/>` and land in
      `working`, exactly like the two ordinary edges below it. The driver pays a
      transition's `<send>` by asking *what does arriving at this state, by this
      event, owe* (`Owed::on`) — and BOTH of those edges are `judge` landing in
      `working`. **They are the same key.** So the refusing edge could not be
      given a different prompt without the driver guessing which of the two it
      was looking at, and a driver that guesses is a second author.

      A state of its own makes the key different, and it is `redirecting`'s shape
      one door over: a state entered for one reason, whose `onentry` composes the
      one thing that reason owes, and which hands straight back to `working`.
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="_event.data.done &amp;&amp; _event.data.checked == 'failed'" target="disputing">
      <assign location="refusals" expr="refusals + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="judge" cond="_event.data.done" target="reflecting">
      <assign location="reflect_reason" expr="'milestone'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE BUDGET IS SPENT, SO ASK FOR AN ACCOUNT BEFORE ENDING. This used to
      go straight to `exhausted`, and that made the ONE ending a person most
      wants explained the one ending that explained nothing: a run that reached
      its north star handed back its agent's own report, and a run that stopped
      short handed back the single word `exhausted`.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS THE ONLY UNFINISHED ENDING THAT CAN BE ASKED, and the other four
      are refused by mechanism rather than by scope:

        cancelled  the run's context is gone — there is no time left to spend on
                   a turn, and the driver is about to interrupt the very pane the
                   question would go into.
        failed     every producer of `fail` is a session or a datamodel that has
                   stopped answering, so there is nothing left to ask WITH: the
                   brief was refused at the door, the prompts cannot be read out
                   of the datamodel, or the replacement session never came up.
        blocked    reached only through `awaiting_human`, and all three of ITS
                   doors say the same thing: somebody or something else owns
                   that pane. `screen.none` and `redirect.none` leave a dialog
                   nobody answered up, and typing a question into that pane
                   ANSWERS THE DIALOG — which is exactly what those two refuse
                   to do, and for the same reason. `turn.interrupted` and `hold`
                   put a PERSON at the keyboard, and a run must not prompt
                   underneath one. A question needs a pane this run may type in,
                   and by the time `blocked` is reached there is not one.
        peer_gone  the sharpest of the four: the program that would answer has
                   EXITED, and its pseudoterminal is not a hole but a wall with
                   a queue in front of it — a last question typed there is bytes
                   towards a write that blocks for ever. There is nobody to ask
                   and asking is the harm.

      So the run's account is asked for wherever it CAN be, and where it cannot
      the reason is a fact about the pane rather than a decision anybody took.

      ⚠⚠ AND THIS IS THE ONE CEILING NO DRIVER CAN SEE, so this edge is the only
      place `turns` can be written down — see `stop_reason`. The word is spelled
      here rather than left to the absence of the other door's: a reader that
      told the two apart by *which key is missing* is the reading register items
      264 and 265 are about.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE BUDGET CAN BE DECLINED, AND THE SPELLING IS A WORD BECAUSE TWO
      PROBES KILLED THE ALTERNATIVES.

      A repository may want a loop that does not end on turns. Three spellings
      were measured before this one was written (`probe_absent.scxml`):
        * a magic `0` or `-1` — refused twice already in this tree, and the
          `ready_timeout_ms` entry argues why: a value's meaning changing under a
          caller is what earns a wire bump;
        * DECLARING the id and leaving it empty — the datamodel answers `Null`
          for that AND for an id nobody declared, so a kind that FORGOT the key
          would be granted an unbounded run;
        * a boolean beside the number — an absent boolean and a `false` one are
          both falsy, so it has the same disease.
      Only a value that is neither a number nor nil can carry the decision, so
      the decline is the word `never`.

      ⚠⚠ `turns >= max_turns` is left EXACTLY as it was and guarded in front,
      rather than rewritten to tolerate a string: comparing a count against a
      word is where a Lua datamodel stops being readable, and this document has
      already paid for one guard that compiled and never fired.
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="max_turns != 'never' &amp;&amp; turns &gt;= max_turns" target="stopping">
      <assign location="stop_reason" expr="'turns'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ A STANDING INSTRUCTION HAS FIRED THAT THE PROMPTS DO NOT YET CARRY, so
      reflect NOW rather than at the next multiple of `reflect_every`.

      This is not a budget; it is a correctness edge. `screening` refuses a call
      and says what to do instead, and the very next `turn_prompt` asks for the
      original milestone again — measured on a six-turn run as ONE instruction
      against SIX re-issues, with a live agent reporting the deadlock in words.
      Adopting the instruction means composing it into the prompts, which happens
      in `priming`, which is reached only through a restart.

      ⚠ A SEPARATE TRANSITION from the budget one rather than an `or` in its
      guard, because they are different reasons and a reader of this document
      should meet them separately.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND A RUN'S TRACE NOW TELLS THEM APART, WHICH IT DID NOT. This comment
      used to end *"which one fired is not published anywhere, and saying so here
      is cheaper than a comment that implies it is"* — and it was true for as long
      as both edges existed. Every one of these three assignments was written and
      read by nothing but the livelock guard in `reflecting`, so three runs with
      three different remedies — *look at the checkpoint the agent chose*, *look
      at the instruction and the dialog behind it*, *nothing, this is the loop's
      own housekeeping* — left journals that were byte-for-byte identical on the
      line that mattered.

      The driver reads `reflect_reason` ON THE EDGE THAT ENTERS `reflecting` and
      puts it in that line. **So the assignments below are load-bearing**: an edge
      into this state that assigns nothing does not merely leave the walk silent,
      it reports the PREVIOUS reflection's cause, because nothing clears the
      variable on entry. A gate reads this file to hold that
      (`every_edge_into_reflecting_says_why_in_a_word_this_driver_knows`) and
      three runs hold what each word means
      (`the_walk_says_why_a_run_stopped_to_reflect`).
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="screened &gt; screened_carried" target="reflecting">
      <assign location="reflect_reason" expr="'instruction'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THERE IS NO ROOM LEFT — register item 424(b), and the door the owner
      asked for: *"컨텍스트가 너무 꽉 차기 전에 새 pane을 열 수 있어야 되는 거
      아니야?"*

      Every other entrance to `reflecting` is *something happened*: the agent
      declared a milestone, a standing instruction fired, the count came round.
      **None of them reads `context`**, so this loop could not decide to hand over
      because it was filling up — it could only find out afterwards, at
      `reviewing`, once something else had already brought it there.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND A CADENCE CANNOT STAND IN FOR THIS, measured: one request adds 861
      tokens at the median and 633,749 at the maximum, and this loop's own run
      reached `context - floor` of 255,809 in a SINGLE turn. No count of turns
      bounds a quantity whose per-turn spread is three orders of magnitude.

      ⚠⚠ THE «BAND» ARGUMENT IN `context`'s OWN ENTRY DOES NOT REACH THIS, and
      that entry says so itself: it refuses a threshold placed INSIDE the band a
      workload sits in, because such a threshold decides by rounding. This is not
      inside the band — it is the end of it. The two axes point opposite ways:
      economics says almost never restart (cache write is 20x read), capacity says
      restart before the window fills, whatever it costs. An economic argument
      followed alone drives the loop into a wall it is not modelling.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS BELOW THE INSTRUCTION EDGE AND ABOVE THE CADENCE, and both halves
      are decisions. An instruction the prompts do not carry yet is a CORRECTNESS
      matter and keeps its word. The cadence is a count nobody measured against
      this workload, so where both are true the run should say the one a reader
      can act on — the ceiling, which is a number they authored.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ ZERO ON EITHER SIDE DECIDES NOTHING, and the direction is stated
      rather than hidden: an unauthored ceiling (`0`) is a caller who has not
      said, and a `context` of `0` is a session whose reading could not be taken.
      **So this guard cannot fire exactly when the loop can see least — it fails
      TOWARD the wall.** The alternative is reflecting on every turn of every run
      whose transcript is unreadable, which replaces sessions that may be nearly
      empty; the run says the record was unreadable on its own walk instead
      (register item 431(a)).

      ⚠ WHAT IS NOT MODELLED, DECLARED: an agent that COMPACTS ITSELF. `context`
      would fall with no transition to explain it and this document would read the
      fall as room. Nothing here measures compaction and no gate stages one — it
      is registered rather than quietly assumed away.
    -->
    <transition event="judge"
                cond="context_ceiling &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt;= context_ceiling"
                target="reflecting">
      <assign location="reflect_reason" expr="'capacity'"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="judge" cond="turns_since_reflect &gt;= reflect_every" target="reflecting">
      <assign location="reflect_reason" expr="'budget'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE TURN WAS UNREADABLE, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS UNFINISHED — and until
      this edge existed, this document could not tell them apart because the
      driver could not either.

      `done` is a yes-or-no and cannot carry *"I could not see"*. The pane's
      retained history is bounded; a turn whose output outran it has lines
      nothing can recover, and if the line carrying the marker is one of them the
      agent's `MILESTONE REACHED` is simply not in what this run was handed.
      `_event.data.unheard` is the count of complete lines that were thrown away
      — `false` when nothing was, so an absence is trustworthy.

      ⚠⚠ MEASURED, ON THE OWNER'S OWN LOOP: across two runs and about four hours,
      every judgement took the edge below and `reflecting` was reached NOT ONCE,
      while the pane held the marker the whole time. A loop that never reflects
      never replaces its session, which is the one thing this file says it is for.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE DECISION IS THE SAME ONE, NOW MADE OUT LOUD: an unreadable turn
      buys ONE MORE TURN, never a convergence. That is `said_marker`'s own
      fail-safe direction and it has not changed — what changed is that the run
      SAYS SO. A reader of a walk full of `unreadable` knows to give the pane a
      bigger scrollback or the turn a shorter leash, where a walk full of the
      plain edge below told them their agent had simply not finished.

      ⚠ IT IS NOT `awaiting_human`. A person cannot answer *"how many lines were
      evicted"*, and waking one for a condition they cannot act on is the cost
      this document refuses everywhere else. The remedy is the scrollback, and
      the walk is where somebody learns to change it.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THESE TWO EDGES ASK FOR THE SAME ACT, AND THAT THEY CAN SAY SO
      SEPARATELY IS THE POINT — register item 470, stage 2, the last prompt to
      move. `Owed::on` keyed on `(event, landed state)`, and BOTH of these are
      `judge` into `working`: one key, and no expression over the driver's
      inputs could ever have told them apart. They happen to owe the same
      sentence today; the day one of them owes a different one, the document
      says so and nothing out there has to be taught the difference.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THESE TWO DECLARE `judged` ON THEMSELVES, WHICH NO `In(...)` COULD.
      `working` is entered from four states and only a JUDGEMENT has a reading
      behind it; the other three doors would be handed a value belonging to some
      earlier turn. So the word rides the EDGE, which is this document saying
      `this door` rather than a driver reconstructing which door it must have
      been from the state it came from.
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="_event.data.unheard" target="working">
      <assign location="reflect_reason" expr="'unreadable'"/>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="turn_prompt"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'work'"/>
      </send>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="arrival.note">
        <param name="note" expr="'judged'"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
    <transition event="judge" target="working">
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="turn_prompt"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'work'"/>
      </send>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="arrival.note">
        <param name="note" expr="'judged'"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ A PEER THAT LEFT AFTER ITS TURN WAS BANKED, UNDER AN ORDER TO STOP,
      IS NOT A RUN THAT FAILED — register item 604.

      Being in `judging` at all means `working` raised `turn.done`, so the turn
      COMPLETED and its account is in hand. A person who typed `sprag stand-down`
      asked for exactly that and for nothing further to be typed at the pane, and
      an agent that finishes its work then EXITS, which is the ordinary case
      rather than the unlucky one. Without this edge the plain one below calls it
      `failed`, and `sprag-host`'s `stand_down_sentence` tells somebody whose turn
      HAD been banked that it was NOT. **The relieved answer and the alarming one
      are swapped**, which is the one direction a report must never be wrong in.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ IT IS FIRST, AND THAT ORDERING IS THE WHOLE FIX. SCXML takes the
      first ENABLED transition in document order, so this guarded edge placed
      after the plain one below can never be reached whatever its condition says.
      Item 605 spent four rounds and five guard expressions on the theory that
      `In('standing_down')` was reading false here, ending with a trivially true
      arithmetic guard that also "did not fire". It was never the guard: upstream
      drove this document at this repository's own pinned engine revision and had
      the guarded edge fire, and the gate
      `a_stood_down_run_whose_peer_leaves_after_a_banked_turn_converges_rather_than_fails`
      now asks the document here, with the neighbouring `judge` guard as its
      control so a red can never be about an order that failed to arrive.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT TARGETS `converged` AND NOT `closing`, which is where the `judge`
      edge above sends the same order. `closing` exists to ASK a question and its
      `onentry` sends `prompt.end`; at this moment the document already knows the
      only party that could answer has gone. Entering a state to do something
      known to be impossible is what `closing`'s own `prompt.unasked` edge is
      there to repair, one pass later and at the cost of typing at a dead pane.

      ⚠⚠ THE GUARD IS THE ORDER, and it has to be. An agent that vanishes with
      turns still to take really has cost the run its remaining work, and the
      plain edge below is the right answer for that. What separates the two is
      whether somebody had already asked the run to stop HERE.
    -->
    <transition event="peer.gone" cond="In('standing_down')" target="converged"/>
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE CLAIM WAS REFUSED AND THE AGENT IS TOLD SO, IN THE CHECK'S OWN
    WORDS — register item 448, the return path item 428 was missing.

    The check runs in a pane of its own, says yes or no, and says why (item 461).
    Every one of those facts reached the run's JOURNAL and none of them reached
    the AGENT: the refusing edge sent the ordinary turn prompt, so a run refused
    nine times asked its agent the identical question nine times and got the
    identical answer. **The verdict was readable by everyone except the only
    party who could act on it.**

    ⚠⚠⚠ THE STATE EXISTS FOR THE DRIVER'S SAKE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A STATE AND
    NOT AN `<assign>` ON THE EDGE. What a transition owes the peer is answered by
    `(event, landed state)`, and the refusing edge and the ordinary one were both
    `judge` into `working` — one key, two prompts, and no way for the driver to
    tell which it had taken. The refusing edge landing HERE is what makes the two
    distinguishable, and it is `redirecting`'s shape: entered for one reason,
    composing the one thing that reason owes, handing straight back to `working`.

    ⚠⚠ IT IS NOT AN ENDING AND IT DOES NOT COUNT AGAINST ANYTHING. The refusal
    was already counted on the edge that got here (`refusals`), the turn it buys
    is counted where every turn is (`working`'s own `turn.done`), and the ceiling
    that stops the buying is `judging`'s. This state's whole part is the sentence.

    ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE TURN IS WATCHED IN `working` RATHER THAN HERE, exactly as
    `priming`'s is. A state that both delivered a prompt and waited out the turn
    would be a second `working` — with its own screening, its own service branch
    and its own blocked routing to keep in step, which is the copy-drift this
    file refuses everywhere it composes rather than repeats.
  -->
  <state id="disputing">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE ONE BRANCH IN THIS DOCUMENT'S EXECUTABLE CONTENT, and it is here
        rather than on two transitions on purpose: the `judging` edge that
        reaches this state is ONE decision (*the check refused*), and whether the
        check also explained itself is a fact about the ANSWER, not a second
        route. Written as two guarded edges it would have been two copies of the
        refusal count and the guard that reads the verdict — the drift this file
        warns about wherever it composes rather than repeats.

        ⚠⚠ `_event.data.explained` IS A NON-EMPTY WORD OR `false`, NEVER `''` —
        the driver's, and it matters here rather than there: this datamodel is
        Lua, where the empty string is TRUE, so a check that explained itself
        with nothing would take the quoting branch and quote nothing.
      -->
      <if cond="_event.data.explained">
        <assign location="dispute_said"
                expr="dispute_quoting + ' ' + _event.data.explained"/>
      <else/>
        <assign location="dispute_said" expr="dispute_unexplained"/>
      </if>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE ORDINARY TURN PROMPT IS COMPOSED IN RATHER THAN REPLACED, and
        that is the difference between telling an agent it was refused and
        telling it to stop. `turn_prompt` carries the milestone, the standing
        instructions and the marker instruction — everything the agent needs to
        go on working — so a refusal that replaced it would answer the check by
        taking the work away.

        ⚠ Composed on ENTRY for `stopping`'s reason: the parts cannot have
        changed since, and the check's words do not exist until the edge that got
        here was taken.
      -->
      <assign location="dispute_prompt"
              expr="dispute_opening + ' ' + dispute_said + ' ' + dispute_asking + '\n' + turn_prompt"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THIS STATE ASKS THE HOST TO PUT ITS SENTENCE rather than
        announcing a name the machine drops. Register item 470, stage 2, and the
        FIFTH act to move — after `closing`, `stopping`, `priming` and
        `reflecting`.

        What stood here was `<send event="prompt.dispute"/>`. NOTHING in this
        document transitions on that name, so the only thing it ever did was
        mint an event; what decided the sentence was a Rust table keyed by this
        state (`Owed::on`, arm `Disputing`). The state says it itself now.

        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ `asks='work'`, AND THIS STATE IS THE ONE WHERE THAT WORD DOES
        SOMETHING RATHER THAN MERELY BEING TRUE. The block above already argues
        it in the other direction: `turn_prompt` is composed IN rather than
        replaced, so what goes to the peer is the ordinary turn the refusal
        bought, opened by the reason it was bought. The turn is then watched in
        `working`, by the same arm that watches every working turn — and that
        arm collects the run's ACCOUNT off any turn whose question asked for
        one. So a refusal that said `account` here would take the agent's next
        piece of WORK and publish it as the run's closing report, on a run that
        had not finished. ⚠ Unlike `reflecting`, whose turn the driver watches
        in an arm of its own, this word is load-bearing the moment it is wrong.

        ⚠ THE ORDER: a send's arguments are evaluated when the send executes
        (W3C SCXML 6.2.3), so what goes out is the `dispute_prompt` the assign
        above just composed — with THIS refusal's words in it — and never the
        one a previous refusal left behind.

        ⚠⚠ A LITERAL IS QUOTED INSIDE `expr`, because `expr` is an EXPRESSION
        in this datamodel and a bare `work` would be a variable nobody declared.
      -->
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="dispute_prompt"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'work'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="prompt.sent" target="working"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ EVERY STATE A PROMPT CAN BE OWED FROM NEEDS THIS EDGE — the driver raises
      `peer.gone` at the state the delivery LANDED in, and a refusal typed at a
      pane whose program has exited is the write that never returns.
    -->
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    Improve the loop's own setup from the record so far: what the next milestone
    is, what the next session should read, and the standing instructions this run
    has been given. None of those takes effect until a session reads them, which
    is why this state's only forward exit is a restart.

    ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS A TURN, AND THE AGENT IS THE ONE ASKED. `reflect_prompt` goes into
    the pane on entry — the same way `priming` sends the start prompt and
    `closing` the end one — and the driver waits for that turn to end exactly as
    it waits for a working one. What the agent writes back is what `restarting`
    hands the replacement.

    Until that existed the only thing a reflection could carry across a session
    boundary was something the AUTHOR had written (a fired `screen_rule`'s text),
    so a run's milestone was fixed for its whole life by whoever started it. The
    shape of the fix is not that the driver became cleverer: it is that the party
    who has been doing the work is now asked about it, because *where the work
    should go next* is a judgement about the work and this driver cannot make one.

    ⚠⚠ THE TURN IS NOT JUDGED, which is why there is no edge to `judging`. A
    reflection is the loop asking about itself, not the agent making progress on
    the milestone, so it does not spend `max_turns` and cannot converge a run.

    ⚠⚠⚠ AND A DIALOG HERE WAKES SOMEBODY, where the same dialog during work is
    screened. `screening` returns to `working` unconditionally, and this document
    has no history state — so a reflection routed through it would resume the WORK
    loop having silently abandoned the reflection, and the run would carry on with
    the milestone nobody had updated. Waking a person is the honest answer until
    the document can resume a screened state; the prompt asks for no tool
    precisely to keep the case rare. ⚠ Registered as owed rather than hidden.
  -->
  <state id="reflecting">
    <onentry>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="0"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE REFUSAL STREAK, cleared here for the reason directly below
        and one of its own: the ceiling buys a REFLECTION and not an ending, so
        a run that has just had one starts counting again. Cleared on ENTRY, so
        it is cleared however this state was reached — the ceiling, the cadence,
        a passing check — and a streak cannot survive a reflection that answered
        `reflect.none` and went back to work.
      -->
      <assign location="refusals" expr="0"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠ BOTH REASONS TO BE HERE ARE CLEARED ON ENTRY, not on the way out. A
        reflection that finds nothing worth changing answers `reflect.none` and
        goes back to work, and if this only moved on `reflect.applied` that run
        would be sent straight back here by the same guard on the next
        judgement — for ever, having judged no further turn.
      -->
      <assign location="screened_carried" expr="screened"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THIS STATE ASKS THE HOST TO PUT ITS SENTENCE rather than
        announcing a name the machine drops. Register item 470, stage 2, and the
        fourth act to move — after `closing`, `stopping` and `priming`.

        What stood here was `<send event="prompt.reflect"/>`. NOTHING in this
        document transitions on that name, so the only thing it ever did was
        mint an event. TWO facts about this state lived out in the driver
        instead: WHICH SLOT holds the sentence (`Owed::on`, arm `Reflecting`),
        and — a second read, and the one stage 2 wrote its own name against —
        `OuterLoop::proposed` fetching `reflect_prompt` back out of this
        datamodel to discount the loop's own echo off the pane. The state says
        the first itself now, and what the host TYPED is what it discounts.

        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ `asks='direction'` IS A THIRD WORD, AND THE ARGUMENT FOR IT IS
        THAT NEITHER OF THE OTHER TWO IS TRUE HERE.

        `work` is the ordinary turn: make progress on the milestone. This turn
        is not that, and this state's own comment above says so in this
        document's words — it is not judged, it does not spend `max_turns`, it
        cannot converge a run, and the sentence asks for no tool. Writing
        `work` here would put in the one argument this act carries a claim the
        file contradicts four lines up.

        `account` is a report over a verdict already reached, and its defining
        property is that the run PUBLISHES the answer as the agent's account of
        itself. A reflection's answer is read back too, but by a different
        reader, into the milestone the REPLACEMENT session is briefed with, and
        the run carries on. Writing `account` here would mean a reflection's
        working-out was collected as the run's closing report the day anything
        watched this turn the way an ordinary one is watched.

        So the third thing an agent can be asked for is WHERE THE WORK GOES
        NEXT, which is this state's own sentence for itself: *where the work
        should go next is a judgement about the work and this driver cannot
        make one*. It is a word here rather than a state's name out there.

        ⚠ THE ORDER: a send's arguments are evaluated when the send executes
        (W3C SCXML 6.2.3), so what goes out is `reflect_prompt` as `priming`
        composed it for this session, never the template it came from.

        ⚠⚠ A LITERAL IS QUOTED INSIDE `expr`, because `expr` is an EXPRESSION
        in this datamodel and a bare `direction` would be a variable nobody
        declared.
      -->
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="reflect_prompt"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'direction'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      The driver rewrote the loop's parts and/or its environment; adopt them.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE PARTS, NOT THE PROMPTS, and the change is forced rather than
      tidy. This transition used to assign `start_prompt` and `turn_prompt`
      whole, from text the driver had composed — a second author for the one
      thing this template exists to own. Now that `priming` composes, a prompt
      assigned here would be overwritten on the way through it, which is a
      document that contradicts itself. Handing over the PARTS is the shape that
      survives: `restarting -> priming` recomposes from them, so a reflection
      changes what the loop is aiming at and the template still decides how that
      is said.

      ⚠ `north_star` is deliberately absent. It is where the loop is ultimately
      going, and a run that may rewrite its own destination cannot be said to
      have reached it.
    -->
    <transition event="reflect.applied" target="reviewing">
      <assign location="milestone" expr="_event.data.milestone"/>
      <!--
        AND THE AGE RESETS WITH IT - register item 592. A reflection that moves
        the checkpoint has authored a NEW claim, so the one thing a briefing
        must not say about it is that it is as old as the run.
      -->
      <assign location="milestone_at" expr="turns"/>
      <assign location="reference" expr="_event.data.reference"/>
      <assign location="standing" expr="_event.data.standing"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      Nothing worth changing: carry on without paying for a restart.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT STILL ADOPTS THE STANDING LIST, and that is what keeps this state
      from growing one. `screen.matched` APPENDS, so an agent that asks the same
      thing twice puts the same line in twice — and reflection is where the list
      is normalised, on BOTH ways out. Assigning it only on `reflect.applied`
      would leave duplicates behind on every reflection that changed nothing,
      and each duplicate is a `standing` the prompts do not carry, which is a
      restart. **A run whose agent kept asking the same question would replace
      its session every time.**
    -->
    <transition event="reflect.none" target="working">
      <assign location="standing" expr="_event.data.standing"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠ THE ARGUMENT IS EVALUATED AFTER THE ASSIGN ABOVE, which is W3C SCXML
        6.2.3 and matters here as much as at any state entry: `turn_prompt` was
        composed by `priming` out of parts this edge has just tidied, and a
        send evaluated first would carry the list before the duplicates came
        out of it.
      -->
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="turn_prompt"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'work'"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE REFLECTION TURN'S OTHER TWO ENDINGS, and they go where a turn that
      nobody can carry on from has always gone.

      A reflection is a turn, so it can be blocked by a dialog or interrupted by
      a person exactly as a working turn can. Neither can be answered here: see
      this state's own comment for why `screening` is not reachable from a
      reflection, and a person typing in the pane is somebody the loop must not
      prompt underneath. `awaiting_human` is what this document already says
      happens when the run needs somebody, and both arrive there rather than
      inventing a new ending.

      ⚠ The reflection is LOST when either fires — the milestone stays what it
      was and `turns_since_reflect` has already been cleared, so the run carries
      on toward the old checkpoint and reflects again at the next budget point.
      That is the safe direction: the alternative is adopting a milestone from a
      turn that did not finish.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE AGENT SAYS THERE IS NOTHING LEFT — the ONE way a run reaches its
      closing report, and the only place `north_star_marker` is heard.

      The reflection asks two questions in one prompt: *what is the next
      checkpoint* and *is the whole thing finished*. This is the second answer,
      and it is deliberately the ONLY route to `closing`: a run's account is
      written once, about the whole run, rather than once per milestone.

      ⚠ `north_star` is the one part a reflection may never rewrite (see
      `reflect.applied`), which is what makes this answer meaningful — the agent
      is reporting against a destination it could not have moved.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND *THERE IS NOTHING LEFT* IS TWO DIFFERENT SENTENCES. The driver
      raises this event from two places and they are not the same run: the agent
      said `north_star_marker`, or the milestone was reached and the reflection
      named no successor — the livelock this state exists to prevent. Both end
      `converged` and, until `done_reason` existed, both wrote one arrow. The
      word says which; the guard is why a raise that forgot to say cannot close
      a run in silence. See `done_reason`'s own comment for both.
    -->
    <transition event="reflect.done" cond="_event.data.done_reason" target="closing">
      <assign location="done_reason" expr="_event.data.done_reason"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ LOOK AT WHAT THIS RUN'S OWN CLOSED SESSIONS DID, before opening another
    one that could do it again.

    # Why it sits HERE and nowhere else

    The things a review could improve about a session — what it starts knowing —
    are read when the session STARTS. So the only moment an answer can change
    anything is the gap between deciding to restart and restarting, and that gap
    is exactly one transition wide. Placed after `restarting` the finding would
    arrive at a session already briefed; placed before `reflecting` it would
    describe sessions the run had not finished with.

    ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS A SEPARATE STATE FROM `restarting` FOR THAT STATE'S OWN REASON,
    written one door down: *replacing a pane happens ONCE*, and a state that both
    reviewed and replaced would leave the driver remembering which half it had
    done. Arriving here means REVIEW; arriving at `restarting` means REPLACE.

    # ⚠⚠⚠ A REVIEW MUST NEVER BE ABLE TO STOP THE RUN

    Every ending CARRIES ON — to `restarting` or, once there is room and the
    economics say keep it, to `working`. The review is ADVICE about work already
    finished, and the run was going on whether or not anybody looked — so a
    review that finds nothing, cannot read the records, or breaks outright must
    cost the run exactly one transition and no more. That is why there is no edge
    from here to `failed`: a `fail` raised by a reviewer is not a failure of the
    loop, and giving it that power would let a file nobody was waiting on end a
    run that was working.

    ⚠⚠⚠ THIS PARAGRAPH READ *"every ending leads to `restarting`"* UNTIL REGISTER
    ITEM 445 SWEPT IT, and it had been false since 424 gave `reviewing` a way to
    keep the session. The claim it is actually about is *a review cannot stop the
    run*, which never depended on where the endings went — a sentence that states
    a shape the document no longer has is item 437's class, and no red can reach
    one.

    ⚠ `cancel` is the one exception, and it is not the review's doing: a person
    or a ceiling ending the run is answered here as it is answered everywhere.

    ⚠⚠ THE DRIVER OWNS THE REVIEW'S LIFETIME because this document cannot — see
    `crate::review::ContextReview`, which holds the measurement of why an
    `<invoke>` was not usable. Leaving this state must drop it, and that is a
    property a gate has to hold rather than the engine.
  -->
  <state id="reviewing">
    <!--
      The review carried a line for the session that does not exist yet. ⚠ It is
      assigned rather than appended — see `carried`.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHETHER THE NEXT MILESTONE IS TAKEN HERE OR IN A NEW SESSION IS
      DECIDED ON WHAT THE SESSION HAS READ, NOT ON HOW MANY TURNS IT HAS TAKEN.

      Both of these edges went to `restarting` unconditionally, so EVERY
      reflection cost a cold start whether or not the session had grown enough
      to be worth replacing. The cadence that reached them was a turn count, and
      a turn is not a unit of context: measured on this loop's own run, ONE turn
      produced three commits and left `context - floor` at 255,809 tokens, while
      the number the "no guard" reasoning was computed from was 18,736.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO THE ORDER IS: finish the turn, ask what is next, and then ask
      WHETHER THERE IS ROOM TO DO IT HERE. Room means this session's reading has
      not reached `context_ceiling`. With room, the next milestone is taken in
      the session that already holds the work — a replacement re-pays `cold` and
      re-reads everything it discarded.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THAT LAST CLAUSE USED TO END *"which is the whole saving"*, WHICH
      IS THE SENTENCE THE ECONOMIC HALF OF REGISTER ITEM 424 CORRECTS. Keeping a
      session saves one `cold`; it also charges every later request the whole
      accumulated reading, and past a point that is the larger number. So room is
      necessary and not sufficient, and the edge below asks the second question:
      has what this session could DISCARD already reached twenty times what its
      replacement would re-write? Above that the keeping is what costs.

      ⚠⚠ ZERO IS NOT A SMALL NUMBER on either side, and both fall back to the
      old behaviour rather than deciding on a value nobody could read: an
      unauthored ceiling (`0`) is a caller who has not said, and a `context` of
      `0` is a session whose reading could not be taken at all. Replacing is the
      conservative half — it is what this loop has always done.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE SECOND REASON TO HAND OVER, WHICH IS NOT CAPACITY: THE
      SESSION HAS GROWN ENOUGH THAT REPLACING IT HAS ALREADY PAID FOR ITSELF.
      Register item 424(a), the ECONOMIC half of the same question the ceiling
      answers on the capacity one.

      `context`'s own entry refused an economic guard for two rounds, and its
      stated reason was that the workload sits INSIDE the 15,000-21,000 band where
      a restart begins to pay — *"a threshold placed anywhere in a band the
      workload sits inside decides by rounding, and would read as a measured
      policy while being a coin toss."* It also named the one thing that would
      make a guard writable: *"A GUARD REMAINS WRITABLE the day a workload sits
      clearly outside the band, and what would justify one is a RE-MEASUREMENT,
      not a preference."*

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE RE-MEASUREMENT HAS BEEN TAKEN AND THE WORKLOAD IS NOWHERE NEAR
      THAT BAND. Over 214 local agent sessions of 20 billed requests or more,
      read exactly as the driver reads one — first request's cache WRITE is
      `cold`, second request's cache READ is `floor`, last request's total is
      `context` — the discardable part grows by 72,212 tokens per TURN at the
      median and 120,928 at p75, against the 2,342 per turn the *inside the band*
      conclusion was computed from. That is a factor of thirty-one. Five turns
      discard 361,061 at the median: **211 of those 214 sessions are ABOVE the
      band at five turns and NOT ONE is inside it, and at eight turns not one is
      inside it either.** The band argument was true about the workload it was
      measured on, and this is a different one.

      ⚠ ONE MACHINE'S NUMBERS AGAIN, on the same terms as the budget note above:
      what travels is that a workload can sit far outside that band, not the
      particular multiple. An author adopting this file re-derives it the same
      way — every billed request records its own cache read.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO THE THRESHOLD IS NOT A NUMBER SOMEBODY PICKED INSIDE A BAND. It is
      the break-even the same measurement names: writing cache costs TWENTY TIMES
      reading it, so a replacement has to be able to shed twenty times what it
      re-writes before it is worth anything at all. Both sides are measured by the
      run about ITSELF — `context - floor` is what this session could actually
      drop, `cold` is what its replacement re-pays — so no author is asked for a
      number here and nothing is being rounded.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS ORDERED AFTER CAPACITY AND CANNOT OVERRULE IT. This edge asks its
      question only where there IS room; a session past its ceiling takes the
      fall-back below whatever the economics say, because the two axes point
      opposite ways and only one of them can lose the session. ⚠ And it is inert
      in the shipped document for the same reason the ceiling is: with no ceiling
      authored there is no *room* to have, and every reflection replaces.

      ⚠⚠⚠ ZERO IS NOT A SMALL NUMBER HERE EITHER, and now on all three. `cold` and
      `floor` degrade to 0 with `context` and for the same cause, and an unreadable
      number must not be able to BUY a replacement — so all three have to have been
      read, and a run that cannot see itself keeps the session it has.

      ⚠ WHAT THIS TRADE DOES NOT PRICE, stated rather than hidden: a replacement
      also costs whatever the session was holding, and that side has never been
      measured. On the numbers above this edge moves the handover from the ceiling
      down to about `floor + 20 * cold`, which buys roughly a seventh of the read
      bill for roughly a fifth more handovers. The saving is measured and the cost
      to the WORK is not, so if these replacements ever read as too frequent the
      honest repair is to measure that side — not to widen this one.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND EACH OF THESE DOORS SAYS WHICH ONE IT WAS — register item 445,
      and register item 477 for the last three of them. They all replace the
      session and they disagree about the remedy: *economics* says nothing is
      wrong, *capacity* says the ceiling is the number to change, *no ceiling*
      says author one, *unread* says go and find out why the record cannot be
      read, and *nobody could say* says both of those are owed. One arrow for
      several findings is the shape items 261, 265, 267 and 423 have each cost a
      round; see `restart_reason`.
    -->
    <transition event="review.done"
                cond="context_ceiling &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &lt; context_ceiling &amp;&amp; cold &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; floor &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context - floor &gt;= 20 * cold"
                target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="_event.data.carried"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'economics'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE SESSION IS KEPT AND THE PROMPTS ARE STILL RECOMPOSED. Register
      item 523, and the reason this door no longer goes straight to `working`.

      `reviewing` is reached by ONE edge, `reflect.applied`, so every run
      standing here has just adopted a new milestone. The text a working turn
      actually carries, `turn_prompt`, is composed in `priming` and NOWHERE
      ELSE. A door that kept the session by going straight back to work left the
      run driving toward its ORIGINAL milestone while being judged against the
      new one, for ever. Measured on run 20: the walk read
      `Reflecting, ReflectApplied, Reviewing, ReviewNone, Working` while the
      pane's prompt still named the milestone the run had moved past.

      ⚠⚠⚠ `priming` IS NOT A SESSION REPLACEMENT, and the difference is what
      keeps this door cheap. `restarting` is what replaces a session, and it
      counts `restarts` on the way in; this state composes and sends a prompt,
      which is what its driver says in as many words: *a session exists and has
      not been prompted*. The agent's whole context is kept; the toll is one
      prompt.

      ⚠⚠ AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THIS DOOR SAY ANYTHING AT ALL. The driver owes a
      prompt on the STATE for `priming`, where `working` is keyed on the EVENT
      and `review.done` sits in its silent list. So the old door typed nothing:
      the run went back to work still holding the question it had been asked
      before the milestone moved.
    -->
    <transition event="review.done"
                cond="context_ceiling &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &lt; context_ceiling"
                target="priming">
      <assign location="carried" expr="_event.data.carried"/>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="0"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE CAPACITY DOOR IS WRITTEN OUT rather than left to the fall-back it
      used to share, and the guard is spelled WHOLE rather than relying on the
      two edges above having declined. Document order would make
      `context &gt;= context_ceiling` redundant here; a reader deciding whether
      this edge is the one they are looking at should not have to derive it from
      the absence of two others, and an author who reorders these edges should
      not silently change what this one means.
    -->
    <transition event="review.done"
                cond="context_ceiling &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt;= context_ceiling"
                target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="_event.data.carried"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'capacity'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE FALL-BACK IS THREE DOORS, NOT ONE, because the two things
      that reach it want OPPOSITE repairs and the sentence they shared named
      both without separating them. Register item 477, measured on a live run at
      97 iterations: EIGHT out of eight `reviewing` exits carried the identical
      line, so `reviewing` had never once decided anything, and nobody reading
      the run could tell which of the two to go and fix.

      The fall-back is reached exactly when the guards above declined, which is
      `context_ceiling &lt;= 0 || context &lt;= 0`. Those are separate facts:

      * NO CEILING is a caller who has not said what a session may spend. The
        repair is to author `context_ceiling`, and the reading itself was fine.
      * UNREAD is a session whose own record could not be taken at all, which is
        item 431's failure (a transcript that exists and reads 0). The ceiling is
        already authored; what is broken is the reading.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ `nobody_could_say` KEEPS ITS OWN SENTENCE AND NARROWS TO WHAT THAT
      SENTENCE ALWAYS SAID: *neither question could be asked*. That is the AND,
      and the edge had been firing on the OR. So the word is not renamed and its
      description is not rewritten; the two single-cause doors are lifted OUT of
      it, and a reader who has that word now learns both repairs are owed.

      ⚠ ORDER IS THE MEANING HERE: the both-missing door is written FIRST because
      a reader told to author a ceiling would author one and come back to an
      unreadable record on the next round, having been told half of it.
    -->
    <transition event="review.done"
                cond="context_ceiling &lt;= 0 &amp;&amp; context &lt;= 0"
                target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="_event.data.carried"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'nobody_could_say'"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="review.done"
                cond="context_ceiling &lt;= 0"
                target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="_event.data.carried"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'no_ceiling'"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="review.done" target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="_event.data.carried"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'unread'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ NOTHING TO CARRY IS THE HEALTHY ANSWER, AND IT MUST CLEAR THE SLOT.
      A run whose last review found a habit and whose next one finds none has
      stopped doing it; leaving the old line in place would brief every later
      session about a habit that is over, and the loop would keep correcting
      something nobody was doing any more.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ THE SAME ECONOMIC QUESTION, ASKED THE SAME WAY. A review that found
      nothing to carry says nothing about how big the session got, so the two
      events differ in what they assign and in nothing else — and a guard written
      on only one of them would make *did the reviewer find a habit* decide the
      handover, which is not what either number is about.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ AND THE SAME FIVE WORDS ON THE SAME FIVE DOORS. A vocabulary written
      on only one of the two endings would make *did the reviewer find a habit*
      decide what a reader is told about the handover, which is the mistake the
      guards themselves are written to avoid one paragraph up.
    -->
    <transition event="review.none"
                cond="context_ceiling &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &lt; context_ceiling &amp;&amp; cold &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; floor &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context - floor &gt;= 20 * cold"
                target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="''"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'economics'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE SAME DOOR ON THE OTHER ENDING, and it must be the same door for
      the reason its neighbours give: a vocabulary or a route written on only
      one of the two endings would make *did the reviewer find a habit* decide
      whether the run is told which milestone it is now working toward. See the
      `review.done` twin above for what going straight to `working` cost.
    -->
    <transition event="review.none"
                cond="context_ceiling &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &lt; context_ceiling"
                target="priming">
      <assign location="carried" expr="''"/>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="0"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="review.none"
                cond="context_ceiling &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; context &gt;= context_ceiling"
                target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="''"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'capacity'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ THE SAME THREE FALL-BACK DOORS, for the reason the paragraph above the
      other ending gives: a vocabulary written on only one of the two endings
      would make *did the reviewer find a habit* decide what a reader is told
      about the handover. See that ending for why the fall-back is three.
    -->
    <transition event="review.none"
                cond="context_ceiling &lt;= 0 &amp;&amp; context &lt;= 0"
                target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="''"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'nobody_could_say'"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="review.none"
                cond="context_ceiling &lt;= 0"
                target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="''"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'no_ceiling'"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="review.none" target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="''"/>
      <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'unread'"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    Close the inner pane and open a fresh one. The new session reads whatever
    `reflecting` wrote on its way up.
  -->
  <state id="restarting">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠ COUNTED ON ENTRY, WHICH IS WHERE THE TOLL IS ACTUALLY PAID. Counting
        it in `reflecting` would count intentions: that state is reached by a
        judged milestone and by a standing instruction alike, and only the ones
        arriving HERE cost a cold start. See `restarts`.
      -->
      <assign location="restarts" expr="restarts + 1"/>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE REPLACEMENT AND THE WAIT ARE TWO STATES, and the reason is this
      document's own rule that the machine instructs its driver THROUGH ITS
      STATE.

      Replacing a pane happens ONCE; waiting for the agent inside the fresh one
      to come up takes as many looks as it takes. Written as one state, the
      driver would have to remember whether it had already done the replacing —
      and a driver that gets that wrong opens a pane per pump, which is a run
      that spawns agent processes without limit. Nothing about the document
      would have said it was wrong.

      So arriving here means REPLACE, and arriving at `resuming` means WAIT.
      Neither the driver nor a reader of the trace has to infer which half a run
      is in.
    -->
    <transition event="session.replaced" target="resuming"/>
    <!-- A session that will not come back is a failed run, not a stuck one. -->
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    A fresh inner session exists and its agent is starting. Nothing is typed
    until it is up: a prompt into a pane whose agent has existed for ten
    milliseconds is echoed by the terminal's own line discipline and read back
    as delivered, which is measured and is what `idle`'s barrier exists for.
    This is that same barrier, on the pane that replaced the old one.
  -->
  <state id="resuming">
    <transition event="session.ready" target="priming"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ A DIALOG THAT WOULD COMMIT A DESIGN DECISION, TURNED DOWN AND REDIRECTED.

    The same act `screening` performs on a matched rule — refuse the call with
    the product's key, then say what to do instead — reached by a different
    route. It is a state of its own rather than an arm of `screening` because a
    reader of a finished run has to be able to tell WHICH authority acted: a
    quote the author wrote, or a judgement about this one dialog. `screening`
    carries the rule's own words out on `Screened::when`; this carries the
    criterion.

    ⚠ `redirect.none` is the honest exit and not a defensive one. The refusing
    key may not take the dialog off the screen — `not_dismissed` where it was
    watched for the whole bound, `unwitnessed` where the run ended inside that
    bound and nobody looked — and a dialog still up reads an Enter as an answer
    to itself. So nothing is typed and the person is woken, exactly as they are
    when no rule claims a question. ⚠ Which refusals arrive is `Unanswered`'s
    business, as it is on `screen.none`; naming them is not counting them.
  -->
  <state id="redirecting">
    <transition event="redirect.done" target="working">
      <assign location="redirected" expr="redirected + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="redirect.none" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!-- The goal was met; ask for the closing report before ending. -->
  <state id="closing">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE FIRST ACT THIS DOCUMENT ASKS THE HOST TO PERFORM, rather than
        one it merely NAMES — register item 470, stage 2.

        This send used to be `event="prompt.end"` with no `type`, which meant it
        went round the machine's own queue and nothing listened: the DRIVER
        decided, from a Rust table keyed on this state's name, that arriving here
        owes the agent `end_prompt` and that the answer is an ACCOUNT rather than
        work. Two facts about this state, written in a second file, in a table
        that had one arm per state of this one.

        Now the state says both itself. `type` names the host (W3C SCXML 6.2.5
        makes it an extensible identifier and this crate declares the one below
        to its build); `event` names the act; the `param`s are its arguments.

        ⚠⚠⚠ `asks` IS THE HALF THAT RETIRED A TWENTY-EIGHT-ARM MATCH, and it is a
        WORD rather than a flag on purpose: what a courtesy turn asks for is a
        property of the QUESTION, and the run reads the answer back as the
        agent's account of itself. A run that asked for work would keep no such
        record. A word this host does not serve is REFUSED, which is what stands
        in for the compiler that used to keep the Rust table exhaustive.

        ⚠⚠ A LITERAL IS QUOTED INSIDE `expr`, because `expr` is an EXPRESSION in
        this document's datamodel and a bare `account` would be a variable
        nobody declared.
      -->
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="end_prompt"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'account'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="turn.done" target="converged"/>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="screening"/>
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE REPORT COULD NOT BE ASKED, AND THE RUN STILL GOT THERE. This is
      the region's rule (below) declined, for `stopping`'s reason and one of its
      own: the agent that did the work is the only party who can account for it,
      so replacing the session here would open a fresh one and ask IT to
      summarise work it never did — a report worse than none, on the ending a
      reader trusts most.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT IT COSTS, STATED: a converged run with no account, where the walk
      says why. `Verdict::Converged` is a fact about the RUN — the north star was
      reached and `reflecting` said so — and it does not depend on the courtesy
      turn that follows it.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING, MEASURED: deleting this one line makes
      `closing` fall through to the region's rule and REPLACE THE SESSION — the
      gate reds with `Restarting` where `Converged` was expected, which is both
      halves of the arrangement proved at once (a child with no answer inherits
      one, and a child with an answer wins by depth).
    -->
    <transition event="prompt.unasked" target="converged"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ AND THE CLOSING REPORT IS NOT WORTH A WEDGE. An agent that has left
      cannot write an account, and the run ends saying which pane went rather
      than `converged` about a report nobody wrote.
    -->
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ THE BUDGET IS SPENT; ASK WHERE THE RUN GOT TO, THEN END IT.

    `closing`'s twin for the ending that did NOT get there — see `stop_prompt`
    for why the question is a different one and not that one reused.

    ⚠⚠ TWO DOORS, ONE STATE, and they are two different budgets rather than two
    routes to one. `turns >= max_turns` is this document's own; `stop_short` is a
    ceiling of the RUN's that this machine cannot see at all (a step count, a
    spend, a wall clock). What they share is everything after the guard: the same
    question, the same reader, the same three endings. A second state would have
    been a second account to keep right.

    ⚠⚠⚠ BUT FOUR REASONS, NOT TWO — the outside door carries any of the run's
    three — AND THE QUESTION USED TO NAME THE WRONG ONE. `stop_prompt` was a
    single sentence opening *"This run has spent its whole turn budget"*, so
    three runs in four were told something false in the one turn where they are
    asked to account for themselves, and that sentence is not a journal line a
    reader can weigh: IT IS TYPED INTO THE AGENT'S PANE. So both doors now write
    `stop_reason`, and the entry below composes the ceiling's own clause into the
    question. Register item 264, and 265 for the walk that reads the same word.

    ⚠⚠⚠ EVERY WAY THIS TURN CAN END ARRIVES AT `exhausted`, AND THAT IS THE
    WHOLE SHAPE OF THE STATE. The ending was decided by the guard that got here:
    the run is out of turns, and nothing the agent does with this last question
    can change that. An account is a courtesy on top of a verdict already
    reached, so **a run that cannot be given one is still that run** — a peer
    that stops to ask, and a person who takes the pane, both end `exhausted`
    with no account rather than being routed somewhere that would treat this
    turn as work.

    ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE ALTERNATIVE IS NOT MERELY UNTIDY, IT IS UNBOUNDED. Both guards
    that reach here stay true for the rest of the run — `turns >= max_turns` is
    arithmetic that cannot go back down, and `stop_short` is a latch over a
    ceiling that has already bound — so any edge from here back into the working
    cycle would come round to `judging`, take the same transition, and ask for
    another account, for ever. `screening` and `awaiting_human` are both such
    edges, and they are what `closing` uses. This state must not have them, and
    the reason is arithmetic rather than taste.

    ⚠⚠ WHAT IT COSTS, STATED. A run out of turns now spends one more turn before
    it ends, and that turn is bounded by the run's own guardrails rather than by
    `max_turns` — which is already spent. So a peer that hangs on this last
    question turns an `exhausted — turns` into an `exhausted — duration`: the
    same ending, reported against a different ceiling. Accepted, because the
    alternative is refusing to ask the question at all; the run's journal carries
    the walk either way.

    ⚠ `cancel` and `fail` keep their ordinary targets, unlike the turn's own
    endings above. Those two are facts about the RUN and not about this turn: a
    person cancelling here has stopped the run, and reporting `exhausted` for it
    would tell them their own act was a budget running out.
  -->
  <state id="stopping">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE CEILING GOES INTO THE QUESTION, and this is the whole of register
        item 264. Both doors above have just written `stop_reason`; this is where
        the word becomes a sentence the agent can act on. Composed on ENTRY, for
        `priming`'s reason one state over: the parts cannot have changed since,
        and the answer does not exist until the edge that got here was taken.

        ⚠⚠ AN UNKNOWN REASON PUTS THE WORD `nil` IN THE SENTENCE — measured, not
        assumed: the concatenation succeeds and the agent reads it. `stop_said`
        is held TOTAL over the driver's ceilings by a gate that reads this file,
        which is what makes that unreachable; see its own comment.
      -->
      <assign location="stop_prompt"
              expr="stop_opening + ' ' + stop_said[stop_reason] + ' ' + stop_asking"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE SAME ACT AS `closing`'s, WITH THIS STATE'S OWN QUESTION — see
        that state for the whole argument (register item 470, stage 2). The two
        differ in exactly the thing they should differ in: `text`. What they ask
        FOR is the same, and it is now said here rather than derived out in the
        driver from which of the two states the turn was taken in.

        ⚠⚠ THE ARGUMENT IS EVALUATED AFTER THE ASSIGN ABOVE, which is W3C SCXML
        6.2.3 — a send's arguments are evaluated once, when the send executes —
        so the sentence this carries is the one the line above just composed and
        never the template it was composed from.
      -->
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="prompt.say">
        <param name="text" expr="stop_prompt"/>
        <param name="asks" expr="'account'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="turn.done" target="exhausted"/>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="exhausted"/>
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="exhausted"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND A QUESTION THAT NEVER WENT IN IS A FOURTH WAY THIS TURN CAN END,
      answered here rather than by the region's rule below — because the rule
      below REPLACES THE SESSION, and this run is already over. The ending was
      decided by the guard that reached this state; the account is a courtesy on
      top of a verdict, and a fresh agent cannot write one about work it never
      did. So the run ends as it was always going to, with no account.
    -->
    <transition event="prompt.unasked" target="exhausted"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ AND UNLIKE THE THREE ENDINGS ABOVE IT, THIS ONE DOES NOT COLLAPSE INTO
      `exhausted`. Those three are a courtesy turn ending one way or another over
      a run whose verdict was already reached; this one says the PEER IS GONE,
      which is a fact about the world the reader has to act on and would be lost
      under a word that tells them to raise a budget.
    -->
    <transition event="peer.gone" target="peer_gone"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ **A QUESTION THE PEER NEVER TOOK IS ANSWERED ONCE, FOR THE WHOLE
    REGION** — register item 446's remedy, made total.

    446 measured the condition and built the remedy in `priming`, where the
    FIRST prompt is refused: the text reaches the pane and is read back off it,
    the submit never becomes a question, and the pane's own counter says so. By
    hand on the wedged pane afterwards: Enter did not submit that draft, `Ctrl-C`
    did not clear it, an interrupt left it standing — while ordinary typed text
    in the SAME pane submitted at once, and a FRESH SESSION took the identical
    prompt. So the condition is the SESSION's, and replacing it is the remedy.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE REMEDY WAS ON ONE STATE WHILE THE CONDITION IS ON SIX. Every
    state that OWES the peer a prompt can meet it — `priming`, `working` (the
    turn prompt, carried by four transitions into it), `disputing`, `reflecting`,
    `closing` and `stopping` — and five of them had no edge at all. What that
    cost, stated exactly: the event is raised at the state the delivery landed
    in, nothing takes it, the machine STAYS PUT, and the driver goes on watching
    a pane for a turn nobody was asked to take. **A run that would have been
    saved by a session replacement waits out its wall clock instead**, and every
    line of the walk while it does says the machine went from `working` to
    `working` on `prompt.unasked`.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠ SO IT IS THE REGION'S RULE AND NOT SIX COPIES, which is this file's own
    warning one screen up: a transition repeated on every working state is *two
    copies of one rule, and the failure of letting them drift is silent*. A child
    that needs a different answer says so on itself and wins by depth — which is
    exactly what `closing` and `stopping` do above, because a run that is already
    ending must not buy a session.

    ⚠⚠ ONE FREE REPLACEMENT, AND THE GUARD IS WHAT MAKES THIS A RECOVERY RATHER
    THAN A SPIN. A peer that will not take the question EITHER, in the session
    opened to fix exactly that, is not something another restart reaches. That is
    a person's, and `failed` is how this document says so. The counter is cleared
    the moment any prompt IS taken (`priming`'s `prompt.sent`), so the budget is
    one replacement per question that landed rather than one per run.

    ⚠ IT IS NOT `awaiting_human`: a condition this document can fix by itself
    must not wake anybody, which is the rule everywhere else here.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED, BY DELETING THESE TWO LINES: the gate reds with the machine
    reporting the state it was already in — `Priming` where `Restarting` was
    expected. **Staying put IS the defect**, and it is what five of the six
    states did before this rule existed.
  -->
  <transition event="prompt.unasked" cond="unasked_since_taken == 0"
              target="restarting">
    <assign location="restart_reason" expr="'unasked'"/>
    <assign location="unasked_since_taken" expr="1"/>
  </transition>
  <transition event="prompt.unasked" target="failed"/>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THIS DOCUMENT'S OWN CONTENT FAILED, AND THE RUN STOPS SAYING SO —
    register item 505, and the one edge in this file whose event the ENGINE
    raises rather than the driver.

    W3C SCXML 3.12.2: an `error.*` goes on the internal queue and is IGNORED
    when nothing matches it. W3C 3.8: the error abandons the rest of the block
    that raised it. Put together, they are the failure this loop ran in until
    now: a state whose `onentry` composed half a prompt and never sent it, a
    guard that could not be evaluated so its transition was never taken, a
    `<send>` naming a type nobody serves — and the machine sitting exactly where
    it was, with no event, no arrow and no reading anywhere that differs from a
    healthy run's.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED BEFORE THIS EDGE EXISTED, by mutation: one unserved-type
    `<send>` in `priming` and a real run walked from `priming` into `working` on
    `prompt.sent` and then took ELEVEN eventless `working` to `working` passes.
    The run went nowhere and every other gate in this crate stayed green.

    ⚠⚠⚠ WHY `failed` AND NOT A RECOVERY. An error in this document's own
    content is not a condition the run can fix by trying again: the block that
    failed is the block that would run again. Every OTHER ending this file has
    is about the world — a peer that would not take a question, a service that
    went away, a budget spent — and each of those has a remedy the loop can
    perform. This one says *the instructions themselves did not execute*, and
    the only party who can act on it is the person who can edit the file or the
    host. `failed` is how this document says exactly that, and its comment on
    `prompt.unasked` above says why that is not `awaiting_human`: a condition
    this document cannot fix by itself is not one it should wake anybody
    mid-run for. It ends, and the run's account carries the sentence.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT IS THE REGION'S RULE, at the foot of `work` beside
    `prompt.unasked`, for that item's reason exactly: EVERY state here runs
    executable content, so every state can meet this, and fourteen copies of one
    rule is the shape this file warns against on every page. A child that ever
    needs a different answer says so on itself and wins by depth.

    ⚠⚠ WHY `error.execution` AND NOT `error.*`. The wildcard would read as the
    careful choice and is a guess about the generator: at the pinned SCE the
    event enum of this document carries exactly one error variant, and a
    descriptor naming a class the codegen never mints is a transition nothing
    can ever take. So the edge names what the machine can actually raise, and a
    RATCHET reads the generated artefact and this file together — the day the
    engine mints a second error class, the gate goes red and asks for an edge
    rather than letting a wildcard silently look after it.

    ⚠ It records the event's own name and nothing else. WHERE it happened is the
    walk's to say: the run's journal writes the arrow that took it here, naming
    the state it left on `ErrorExecution`, so the state that failed is already
    recorded without this document keeping a second copy of it.
  -->
  <transition event="error.execution" target="failed">
    <assign location="fault" expr="_event.name"/>
  </transition>

  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ THE ORDERS REGION — what a person has told this run, standing beside the
    work rather than interrupting it.

    It has no ending, and that is the point: an order is not a task. SCXML
    completes a `<parallel>` only when EVERY region is final, so a run's ending
    is decided by the WORK region reaching a state outside the parallel — see the
    note above.

    ⚠⚠ ONE ORDER TODAY, AND THE REGION IS FOR THE ONES AFTER IT. *Pause*, *change
    the milestone*, *reflect at the next boundary* are all the same shape: a
    sentence that arrives mid-turn and changes what the next decision point does.
    Each is a state here, not another boolean somewhere.
  -->
  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ BEFORE ADDING AN EVENT TO EITHER CHILD BELOW, READ THIS. Register item 547.

    `standing` answers `hold`, `stand.down` and `resume`; `held` answers none of them. That
    asymmetry is deliberate and it has compiled every round since the region was written — because
    SCE's handler-consistency check SKIPS a compound entirely until one event exists that EVERY
    transition-carrying sibling matches. While no such event exists, the three gaps are invisible.

    The moment one appears, all of them are reported at once, and the message names THE OLD THREE
    rather than the line you just wrote. Measured 2026-08-21: adding `hold.expired` to `held` did
    exactly that; renaming it to `abandon` compiled again.

    THE RULE A ROUND APPLIES BEFORE EDITING: an event is dangerous here when a sibling already
    answers any PREFIX of it. SCXML matching is by prefix, so `standing`'s bare `hold` matches
    `hold.expired` — the token that flips this compound need not be spelled anywhere in it.

    ⛔⛔ AND `sce:unhandled` CANNOT BE ARMED IN ADVANCE: a declaration is judged against the same
    gap map the precondition gates, so declaring these three today is refused as stale. There is no
    way to make this compound safe ahead of time; the three gates in
    `tests/when_the_engine_refuses_a_compound_it_was_tolerating.rs` drive all of it against the
    pinned engine, so a pin bump that changes any of it goes red rather than silent.
  -->
  <state id="orders" initial="standing">

    <!--
      Nobody has said anything. The resting state of every run, and the one a run
      spends all of its life in unless somebody speaks.
    -->
    <state id="standing">
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE SENTENCE THAT COULD NOT BE SAID. It changes NOTHING about the
        turn in flight — no target outside this region, no interruption of the
        work — which is the difference between this and `cancel`. What it changes
        is what `judging` will decide when the turn it is in the middle of is
        over.
      -->
      <transition event="stand.down" target="standing_down"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ A PERSON IS READING THE PANE. Like `stand.down` this changes nothing
        about the turn in flight; unlike it, it is the one order that can be
        taken back, so it has an edge home.

        ⚠⚠ The WORK region takes this same event to `awaiting_human`, which is
        what parks the loop. This region is what makes the order READABLE: the
        work region cannot say WHY it is waiting, because a person typing, a
        peer that stopped speaking and an unreadable dialog all park it in the
        same state.
      -->
      <transition event="hold" target="held"/>
    </state>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ SOMEBODY IS HOLDING THIS RUN ON PURPOSE, and `awaiting_human`'s
      `unattended` edge reads this state to tell that apart from a run nobody
      came to. Register item 470's first stage: the decision used to live in the
      driver as a flag it read before raising the event, and a decision that
      lives in the driver is one the document cannot be asked about.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT CLOSES A WINDOW THE FLAG COULD NOT. The driver reads the
      person's flag at one instant and the machine processes the event at
      another, so a hold that arrives between those two moments used to end the
      run as unattended anyway. Here the order and the ending are decided in the
      same queue, in the order they really arrived.

      ⚠ THE WAY BACK IS `resume`, which the work region takes to `working` in
      the same step. This is the only order with one; see `standing_down` below
      for why the others do not get it.
    -->
    <state id="held">
      <transition event="resume" target="standing"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE HOLD'S OWN ENDING — THE ONE THIS ORDER DID NOT HAVE.
        Register item 534, which is item 9's residue: the fix that stopped a held
        run burning its iterations left it burning nothing instead, for ever.

        ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS ON THIS STATE AND NOT ON `awaiting_human`, which is the
        opposite placement to `unattended` beside it, and the asymmetry is the
        argument. `unattended` is a fact about the WORK — nobody came to a run
        that needed somebody — so it belongs where the waiting happens, and it
        has to ASK this region (`cond="!In('held')"`) whether an order explains
        the wait. This event is a fact about the ORDER itself, so it belongs
        here, and being here it needs no guard at all: the state is the
        condition. A structural answer where the other one needs a written one.

        ⚠⚠ WHAT IT IS NOT. Not `unattended` — somebody DID come, said stop, and
        then did not come back, and sending a reader to look for a person who is
        standing right there is precisely what item 470 took that decision out of
        the driver to prevent. Not `cancelled` — nobody cancelled anything, and a
        run reported cancelled sends its reader looking for whoever cancelled it.
        Not `exhausted` — no budget was spent; the run did nothing at all, which
        is the whole complaint.

        ⚠ THE CEILING IS `hold_within_ms` AND THE DRIVER RAISES THIS when it
        elapses, for the reason every other ending here is arranged that way: the
        driver can MEASURE how long an order has stood, and what a run does about
        it is a judgement, which lives in this file.
      -->
      <transition event="abandon" target="abandoned"/>
    </state>

    <!--
      ⚠ AN ORDER, ONCE GIVEN, STANDS. There is no edge back: a person who changed
      their mind cancels the run or starts another, and a *stand down, no wait,
      carry on* that raced a milestone would be a run whose ending depended on
      which of two messages arrived first. ⚠⚠ It is also why this is a state
      rather than a flag somebody could flip twice.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ IT RUNS NOTHING ON ENTRY, and one round tried to change that. A mirror
      of this state as a datamodel boolean was written here to sidestep the guard
      defect registered against `judging`'s `peer.gone` edge; it did not sidestep
      it, and what it left behind was executable content in a TEMPLATE — which
      then owes an `error.execution` edge, because W3C SCXML 3.8 abandons the rest
      of a block that raised one and 3.12.2 drops what nothing matches.

      ⚠ `OuterLoop::standing_down` answers the same question from outside with
      nothing added here at all, which is why the mirror is gone.
    -->
    <state id="standing_down"/>
  </state>

  </parallel>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ EVERY ENDING BELOW SAYS WHICH WORD IT PUBLISHES, on its own entry.

    Register item 470, stage 3. `AiLoop::ended` chose the verdict from a `match`
    over all twenty eight states of this file: seven arms naming an ending and
    twenty one written out to say *not an ending* so the match stayed exhaustive
    without a wildcard. It was a second copy of this document's topology, decided
    in Rust, keyed on ids nothing over there parses.

    ⚠⚠ THE WORD MOVES AND THE PAYLOAD DOES NOT, which is the line item 470 draws.
    Which ceiling fell, what question was left on the pane, which pane the dead
    peer had: those are facts about the RUN, latched by the driver as it went,
    and it stays the only thing that holds them. What it stops doing is deciding
    which of the seven this is from a state's NAME.

    ⚠⚠⚠ AN ENTRY CAN CARRY THIS AND NOTHING ELSE CAN. An ending is arrived at,
    never pumped, so there is no later moment to ask at; and `probe.rs` measured
    that a top level `<final>`'s onentry does reach the host with its `<param>`,
    even though entering one COMPLETES the machine and no reply can be delivered.
    That measurement is what opened this road; it is not assumed here.
  -->
  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND EACH ONE ALSO SAYS WHAT A STOP WOULD STILL HAVE TO REACH.

    `AiLoop::driving` answered that with a `match` over all twenty eight states:
    THREE arms answering `nothing` and TWENTY FIVE answering the pane. All three
    of the three are endings, so the twenty five were never a decision — they
    were one fact, `a run in flight has a pane`, written out twenty five times to
    keep the match exhaustive.

    ⚠⚠ THE DIRECTION FAILS SAFE TOWARDS `pane`, and that is why the argument is
    required rather than defaulted: a needless interrupt costs a peer one
    keystroke it was waiting at anyway, and a missed one leaves a model spending
    somebody's tokens on a question nothing is waiting for. An ending that forgot
    to say must arrive as a refusal, not as a safe guess.
  -->
  <final id="converged">
    <onentry>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="end.publish">
        <param name="publishes" expr="'converged'"/>
        <!-- The closing report's turn ENDED, so the peer is at rest. -->
        <param name="signals" expr="'nothing'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
  </final>
  <final id="exhausted">
    <onentry>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="end.publish">
        <param name="publishes" expr="'exhausted'"/>
        <!--
          ⚠⚠⚠ RE DERIVED SINCE `stopping`, NOT CARRIED OVER. This has three
          doors now and none of them leaves a model spending tokens:
          `turn.done` is the account written and the peer at rest;
          `turn.blocked` is the peer parked at a dialog, waiting rather than
          producing; `turn.interrupted` is a person who has already taken the
          pane, and signalling underneath them is the one thing this driver
          must never do. The other three doors out of `stopping` land in
          endings that DO answer the pane.
        -->
        <param name="signals" expr="'nothing'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
  </final>
  <final id="failed">
    <onentry>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="end.publish">
        <param name="publishes" expr="'failed'"/>
        <!-- Says nothing about what the peer is doing; an unknown answer must
             fail towards stopping. -->
        <param name="signals" expr="'pane'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
  </final>
  <final id="cancelled">
    <onentry>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="end.publish">
        <param name="publishes" expr="'cancelled'"/>
        <!--
          ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE ENDING THIS WHOLE QUESTION EXISTS TO SERVE, and the obvious
          rule `answer nothing once the document is finished` gets it exactly
          wrong. This is reached because `watch` saw the run end WHILE THE AGENT
          WAS MID TURN, so by the time anybody asks what to stop, the document
          is finished and the agent is not.
        -->
        <param name="signals" expr="'pane'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
  </final>
  <final id="blocked">
    <onentry>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="end.publish">
        <param name="publishes" expr="'blocked'"/>
        <param name="signals" expr="'pane'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
  </final>
  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE PROGRAM THIS RUN WAS DRIVING HAS EXITED.

    A SIXTH ENDING, and the argument for it is the one this document already
    made twice — for `blocked` (a run that wants an ANSWER is not a run that
    wants a FIX) and for `awaiting_human` (a run that wants NOTHING done is
    neither). This one wants something none of the five can ask for: **the agent
    back**. Nothing in the run is broken, nobody asked it a question, no budget
    ran out and no person is at the pane.

    ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT IT COST TO NOT HAVE THIS WORD, MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED. A
    pseudoterminal whose child is dead takes 16,896 bytes of newline-terminated
    input and then blocks FOR EVER, holding the pane's writer lock, and the
    blocked write cannot be cancelled. A driver that types its stimulus every
    step walks there in 3,380 steps — about 29 minutes — at a pace nothing about
    it looks wrong. One run held a build machine for 43 hours that way.
    (A dedicated gate in the terminal layer measures that wall directly, and it
    is worth keeping: the number is a property of the pseudoterminal, not of any
    one loop.)

    ⚠⚠⚠ AND WHY IT IS THE DOCUMENT'S DECISION AND NOT THE DRIVER'S. The driver
    can MEASURE that a pane's program has gone — `pane_eof` has answered that
    since long before any of this — but *what a run does about it* is a
    judgement, and every major defect of this file's history was a judgement
    living outside it. So the driver reports `peer.gone` and this file says where
    that lands. A guard in the driver that quietly stopped stepping would be the
    same fix with the decision hidden.

    ⚠⚠ IT IS REACHED FROM EVERY STATE THAT TOUCHES THE PEER'S PANE, which is all
    of them but `reviewing` — a child machine reading sessions that are already
    closed, which types at nobody.

    ⚠ AND THE TWO ROUTES ARE ONE FACT. A state that was about to TYPE meets the
    refusal at the door (`PaneAccess::inject`); a state that was WAITING meets
    the turn that nobody is left to end (`Over::PeerGone`). Both raise this
    event, and a reader of the walk sees which state it came from.
  -->
  <final id="peer_gone">
    <onentry>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="end.publish">
        <param name="publishes" expr="'peer_gone'"/>
        <!--
          ⚠⚠⚠ THIS ONE ANSWERS ON EVIDENCE RATHER THAN ON THE FAIL SAFE. The
          other two `nothing` endings are arguments that no model CAN be mid
          turn; this is the one case where the product has LOOKED — the state is
          only reached because `pane_eof` said the pane's child has exited. There
          is no job left to signal.
        -->
        <param name="signals" expr="'nothing'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
  </final>
  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠ SOMEBODY HELD THIS RUN AND DID NOT COME BACK.

    A SEVENTH ENDING, on the argument this document has now made three times —
    for `blocked` (a run that wants an ANSWER is not a run that wants a FIX), for
    `awaiting_human` (a run that wants NOTHING done is neither) and for
    `peer_gone` (a run that wants its AGENT back). This one wants something none
    of the six can ask for: **the person who paused it**. Nothing is broken,
    nobody asked it a question, no budget ran out, its peer is alive and well,
    and a person is not merely absent — they were HERE, and they said wait.

    ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT IT COST TO NOT HAVE THIS WORD. Before `hold` had a wait at all a
    held run spent its whole iteration budget looking at a pane nothing was
    happening on, and died as `exhausted (iterations)` in 24 minutes telling its
    reader to raise a number that would have bought nothing (register item 9,
    measured on run 18). The fix for that gave the hold a real wait — and then
    the run had no ending at all: patience is not spent while a person holds it,
    `unattended` is refused for it by `held`, and a bound of `Duration::MAX`
    answers only a cancel. Register item 534. **Both defects are the same one
    seen from two sides: an order with no ending.** This is the ending.

    ⚠⚠ AND IT IS REACHED FROM ONE STATE ONLY, which no other final here can say.
    Every other ending is about the WORK, and the work region can be in several
    states when it arrives; this one is a property of the `orders` region, whose
    `held` state is the only place a hold exists. So there is exactly one edge,
    it needs no guard, and a reader of the walk cannot be told anything but the
    truth about how the run got here.
  -->
  <final id="abandoned">
    <onentry>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="end.publish">
        <param name="publishes" expr="'abandoned'"/>
        <!--
          ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE ARM WHERE THE FAIL SAFE DIRECTION DOES REAL WORK. The obvious
          reading is that hours of holding means nothing can be mid turn, and
          this document's own `HOLD_TAKES_EFFECT` refuses it: a hold PARKS THE
          LOOP and deliberately leaves the turn its agent is in the middle of
          alone. The last thing anybody knows about that peer is that it was
          working, and nobody has looked since.
        -->
        <param name="signals" expr="'pane'"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
  </final>
</scxml>
